      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>PERIL OF THE TANNERY.</head>
        <p>PERIL OF THE TANNERY.</p>
        <p>Hide Worker's Death From</p>
        <p>Anthrax.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at the Warrington Police Station on Monday, by Mr. F'. A. Jones (the deputy coroner,, on the body of John A.rthilr Robson (52), of the Fold, Orford, who recently died at the Warrington Infirmary. Present at the inquiry were Mr. H. E. Brothers (H.M. In- spector under the Factory Acts), Mr. A. E. Sea- bury (on behalf of the relatives), and. Mr. H. J. Westbrook (on behalf of Messrs. Wm. Mortimer and Co., tanners, Orford, by whom the deceased was employed). Sarah Robson, daughter of the deceased, said that on October 29th, her father complained of not feeling well, but he seemed to get better. On November 4th he complained of his face being swollen, and suggested that he had caught cold. On Saturday, November 6th, he was much worse, and she told him to go and see a doctor. Later he was taken to the Infirmary, where eventually he passed away. Told to Wear Gloves. Walter Pawsey, foreman at the tannery or Messrs. Wm. Mortimer and Co., said that the work of the deceased consisted of putting hides into large drums in order to soften them. For this work he was told to wear gloves, which were provided by the firm. There was a printed notice in the shed which stated that the gloves had to be worn. On Saturday the deceased asked him (the witness) to look at his neck. The witness suspected that the deceased -was suffering from anthrax and sent him to a doctor. He (the foreman) always made it his duty to see that the men wore glove's.</p>
        <p>In reply to the Factory Inspector, the witness said that food was never taken to •the place where the skins were handled. The men did not carry the hides on their shoulders. The de- ceased never said if he had any cuts on his hands or face. Mr. Seabury asked the witness if there were any wash-bowls in the department where Rob- son was employed? Witness: There is a lavatory close by. Is it a fact that there are wash-bowls that have been erected for three years, but have never been fitted up with water ?--It is a fact. Do I understand that these wash-bowls are only in the dining-room?—Yes. The Medical Testimony.</p>
        <p>Miss M. E. Brown, a sister at the Warrington Infirmary, said that after the deceased went into the Infirmary he gradually grew worse. In reply to a question by Mr. Westbrook, she said that she had no doubt that it was anthrax that the-aibeased was suffering from. Dr. Lappin, resident house surgeon at the Infirmary, said that the cause of death was anthrax. Mr. Westbrook, on behalf of the firm, ex- pressed his sympathy with the relatives, saying that the deceased was a very good workman. The particular hides on which he was engaged were not considered to be dangerous. Summing up, Mr. Jones said that it seemed a great pity that men employed at this work did not consult a doctor as soon as they felt ill. If the deceased had consulted a doctor straightaway, it was possible that the treat- ment prescribed by the doctor would have saved his life.</p>
        <p>The jury returned a" verdict of "Death due to anthrax, contracted by misadventure at the works."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>FOXES.</head>
        <p>FOXES.</p>
        <p>How The War May Increase Them.</p>
        <p>The President of the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries, in view of the inevitable increase in the number of foxes consequent on the reduc- tion or cessation of fox-hunting dnring the war, has drawn the attention of the Masters of Fox- hounds' Association to the desirability of adopt- ing measures to destroy foxes, and the Presi- dent of the Association has issued a letter urg- ing Hunt Clubs throughout England and Wales to do their best in this matter to protect the interests of farmers and poultry-keepers. Lord Selborne has also arranged for inquiry by his Department as to the extent to which the sur- plus of foxes is being reduced, and to what degree their depredations are still affecting the actual and potential supply of food. This il.ii_Tmation will be la. at the dis- pfltlat of the County War Agricultural Com- mittees and the District Sub-committees, in order that additional consideration may be given to the protection of poultry in localities where the results of the inquiry indicate that sach a course is desirable</p>
      </div>
