      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDA f, NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDA f, NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</p>
        <p>MAYORAL CHURCHGOING.</p>
        <p>epresentative Procession Accompanies His Worship</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>To Parish Church.</head>
        <p>To Parish Church.</p>
        <p>RECTOR AND THE AUTHORSHIP OF THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Twice has it befallen Alderman Peter Peacock, fresh from re-election as Mayor of the import- ant, growing and flourishing County Borough of Warrington, that he should undertake his civic churchgoing 'neath the shadow of the clouds of the greatest war that yet historian has been called upon to record. The first occasion was a twelvemonth ago; the second, last Sun- day—as dolorous a day as ever befitted an occasion overclouded by the spectre of inter- national hatred—of grisly slaughter. A pall of fog hung over the streets which his Worship and his representative retinue trod on their way to the grand old I'arish Church—there to pay their homage and offer their humble pleas to the Great God of Battles. There was that which was symbolic about that impenetrable cloud of whitish mist; something symbolic in the clam- ing air and in the spectral appearance which familiar forms and places took, for the nonce, upon themselves. "The times are out of joint "; the usually peaceful Mayoral church- going became, in a sense, a belligerent demon- stration—a demonstration of the invincibility of British determination to win, or to die, in a cause which Britons know to be a just and righteous one. Hence, the dominant note of khaki in the civic cavalcade was not out of tune with the high purpose for which the pro- cession was marshalled—that of rendering thanks, and making supplication, to the Most High. It is safe to say that no Mayoral pro- cession ever excited greater general interest, for both going.,to and coming from church it passed through serried rows of spectators, as many of whom as could possibly be accommodated in the glorious old edifice took their seats in the pews or were content to stand in the aisles. and thus take their part in A memorable and deeply Im- pressive service.</p>
        <p>THE RECTOR'S STRIKING SERMON. Apportioning The Blame For The War.</p>
        <p>Canon Willis—who has served several Mayors as official chaplain, as he, for long, has also faithfully served the Parish of Warrington as its spiritual head and guide—was the preacher, and, despite his great age, and all-the, physical and vocal defects that attach to weight of years, he gave a striking sermon—one which focussed and crystalised the thoughts of all in his great and representative congregation—a con- gregation embracing most phases of thought in the town; just as it also embraced members of every class in the community—high, low, rich and poor. The Canon found his text in the Book of Amos—Amos, wise among the many wise prophets of old! "Shall," the text ran, ".hero be evil in a city and the Lord hath not done it? Surely the Lord God will do nothing, but He revealeth His secret unto His servants the prophets. The lion hath roared, who will not fear? The Lord God hath spoken, who can but prophesy?" The prophet, the preacher told his ccmgrega- tion, lived in about the middle of the Eighth Century, before Christ; in the days when the Kingdom of Israel was at the height of its prosperity and at .the lowest depth of its de- pravity. It was not moral evil, but physical evil to which Amos referred—to disaster. And therefore the text should be rendered "Shall disaster befall a city, and the Lord hath not done it?" The words of the prophet were most appropriate to the present time. The first point which Amos proclaimed in his message to the people was his idea of God—that He was the Supreme ‘Being. to whom nobody must be com- pared. Next he dwelt upon the relationship of Israel to God. Israel fiad thought that there was a natural tie between themselves and God; that ,they were His children, and that, in all circumstances, He would protect them. Amos, on the other hand, pointed out that the tie was a moral and not a natural one, and that if the race fell away from God He was not hound to protect them. Thirdly, Amos dwelt noon a coming judgment, speaking of it as a day of gloom and darkness—and finally he spoke of the happier future which was in store.</p>
        <p>put into operation in some districts—were, in his opinion, sufficient. Notwithstanding the changes, they still read about drunkenness-- chiefly, he was grieved to say, among women. Again, before the war there was great im- morality, but people apparently thought very little about it. There was also much desecration of the Lord's Day. He did not wish to be misunderstood on that point. He believed in Sunday being a very bright, cheerful and happy day, but nobody had kept the Lord's Day aright who had not been present at the Holy Communion. He had no objection to music in the afternoon, to help to make the day bright and cheerful; indeed, if people did their duty in keeping the Lord's Day in the morning, and, if they could, in the even- ing, then he would be satisfied. Realisation Dawning!</p>
        <p>After referring to the fact that two thousand years had passed since Christ came on earth and yet only about a third of the people of the world were even nominally Christian, Canon Willis went on to examine the conditions existing after fifteen months of war, and said that he had noticed an increased realisation of the awful seriousness of the conflict, although the awaken- ing had been but gradual. They were begin- ning to realise whom they were fighting against and what they were fighting for. They used to think that only the_military party in Prussia were their foes, but now they 'knew that the whole German nation was against them. The Germans knew very well what would happen to them if they lost this war. They knew that they would have to give up their fleet4,and the territory which they had taken in France and Belgium, that they would.hame to pay large in- demnities'•arrd aliandOn their idea of world dominion. What would this country lose if she were defeated? She would lose liberty; her people would be slaves, ground down under the heel of the most cruel and brutal power that ever existed. Britons were fighting for liberty and justice, for the rights of small States as well as for their own. They would conquer if only they did their duty and trusted in God.</p>
        <p>Question for Those Who Have Not Yet Gone.</p>
        <p>Next the preacher touched upon the splendid response of the men of the nation to the call to arms; adding, however, that there were still many who should ask themselves whether it was not their duty to be prepared to give up every- thing for their country and their God. They still heard—though, thank God, not in the neighbourhood of Warrington—of strikes, and, whilst he was not going to apportion the blame as between employers and employed, he would lay it down emphatically that there ought to be no strikes at such a time as this. Russia and France could not understand Buell disputes at so critical a time. Again, the, p6ople of this land were still too much bent on pleasure. God forbid that they should do away with all inno- cent amusement—particularly for the soldiers and sailors who were in such need of relaxation —but he thought that those who remained at home should guard against excessive indulgence in pleasure. In conclusion the Canon expressed regret that there was such a lack of earnest public prayer and intercession. They should, he said, seek to realise more and more their dependence upon God, Who was all-loving and all-merciful, and Who was allowing this war for the good of His PeoPle, as well as for the punishment of their enemies.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>War a Visitation From God.</head>
        <p>The War a Visitation From God.</p>
        <p>A few days ago a clergyman asked a number of his brother clergy whether they were to regard the present war as a visitation from God. The answer was "Yes; it is to be considered a visitation from God." Nothing happened by chance; everything was sent or permitted by God. Applying the text to the war they might well ask "Who is the author of all this strife and bloodshed?" Without any hesitation he would answer "The German Emperor!" If there was any man who could have stopped the war he was that man. The Germans professed that this country was the cause of this war, but one had only to look at the unprepared state of this country to give the answer. If there were any two men who tried to stop the war, they were the Foreign Secretary (Sir Edward Grey) and his Majesty the King. As proof of that they had only to read that book "J'accuse." The writer, a German, evidently had access to very important documents of State. and he proved beyond all question or doubt that Ger- many caused the war, and that Britain's Foreign Secretary and her King did their utmost to prevent it. The first of the authors of this war was the German Emperor; upon him rested the responsibility, and he would have to give answer to God at the last.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>Deeds Prompted by the Devil.</head>
        <p>Deeds Prompted by the Devil.</p>
        <p>Secondly, however, the devil was the author of the war, for who could have prompted the awful things which had come to pass but Satan? Whoa they thought of the conduct of the' Ger- mans in Belgium—conduct, -the awfulness of which was proved beyond question by Lord Bryce's report; a report which contained no mere rumour, no exaggeration, but was full of plain fact—and read accounts of what they did to defenceless women and helpless children, they- might well ask, "Who could have prompted such deeds but the devil?" Thirdly, God was the cause of the war, in the sense that He either sent or permitted everything that happened. It was God, as Amos told, who, allowed the for- eign enemies to conquer His own people. As he had already said, the kingdom of Israel, although prosperous in a worldly sense, had at that time sunk to the lowest state of depravity. There was commercial dishonesty, social op- pression, and immorality. The poor were op- pressed and could get no redress. The Rector went on to refer to the condition of the world just before the present war broke out,, remark- ing that there was vast wealth, chiefly among the upper classes, in America, Germany and England. Newspaper readers would recall the many accounts which appeared in the Press of weddings in America, and of the fabulous sums of money wasted upon entertainment. If they looked w at Germany, • was it not 'marvellous to think of, the enormous wealth which that nation had accumulated during the last forty years? They had only to think of the great sums which she was able to spend upon her navy, and also upon her merchant fleet, which, before the war, went to all parts of the world. Again, let them think of the great wealth as a nation of Britain, and of the number of millionaires that they had read of in America, Germany and Great Britain. Moreover, vast wealth had not always been spent as wisely as one could wish. Business and pleasure had become everything.• Men worked hard for six days of the week to make money, and from Saturday to Monday they went off in their motor cars to enjoy them- selves. The picture palaces and theatres were all #lled with people; there was nothing but business and pleasure.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>Great Deal Of Excessive Drinking.</head>
        <p>A Great Deal Of Excessive Drinking.</p>
        <p>On top of all the pleasure-loving a great deal of excessive chinking prevailed before the war; they all knew how common drunkenness had be. come in Russia and France. and in ,thir cv n ' land. But, what a change had taken place in Russia, which, at the Czar's wish, had given up intoxicating drink! France, they were• told just lately, had given up what she had been spending upon absinthe. Fifty-three million gallons of absinthe were formerly consumed, and 1,25000 people were occupied in distilling it. They had decided to give that up. .In Eng- land changes had also been made. They were having shorter hours and fewer houses—a very good thing. Two hours and, a .half at mid-day and three hours in the evening—the hours tehie.h he believed had been proposed, if not actually</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>MUSIC.</head>
        <p>THE MUSIC.</p>
        <p>The service was.most impressive in character, and in conducting it the Rector had the assist- ance of the .Revs. T. Vickers, J. M. _Buck- master and S. G. Stanton. The choir rendered in most praiseworthy mann.er Mendelssehn's beautiful anthem "Hear my Prayer," Master Nevill Davies having charge of the difficult solo part and • acquitting himself with distinction. Special prayers for the soldiers who have fallen in the war and for those who still fight manfully on were offered, and the hymns "0 God, our help in ages past," and "Stand up! stand up for Jesus," were sung. The service concluded with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>PROCESSION.</head>
        <p>THE PROCESSION.</p>
        <p>As in previous years the procession set out from the Town Hall. It was headed by the band of the 2nd South Lancs. Regiment, under the leadership of Mr. R. E. Cahill. Next came the members of the 12th South Lancs. Regiment —the Bantams Battalion, who have been for some time in training at Orford Barracks, and who were under the command of Lieutenant- Colonel Ritchie, D.S.O. Following behind the band of the 2nd/4th South Lancs. Regiment-- , which was conducted by Acting-Bandmaster It. E. Sadler—was a strong detachment of the local Territorials, who excited special interest in the crowd as they marched along under the com- mand of Captain Eric Fairelough, audible com- ments being passed upon their soldierly appear- ance and general smartness. The Boteler Grammar School Cadets, spruce in their neat khaki 'uniforms marched on the heels of the Territorials, Captain Pearson and Lieutenant Pape having charge of the lads. Headed by their Area-Commandant, Mr. W. Peter Rylands, the members of the Warrington Company of the Lancashire Volunteer Regiment came next, the Stockton Heath ,Platoon of the Cheshire Volun- teer Regiment (in uniform), the Warrington Battalion of the Church Lads' Brigade, the Latchford Cadet Corps, and the Crosfield and Whitecross troops of Boy Scouts following in the order named. The Stockton 'Heath Volun- teers were led by Platoon-Commander F. War- burton and Platoon-Sergeant Stirrup and the Church Lads'sere in charge of Majors Brooks and Ford, Captain Bunch and Lieutenant Norris; whilst 'Mr. J. B. Starkey, the founder of the, body, walked at the head of the Latch- ford Ciitrps, Sergeants Jones and Cartwright 'being in command. Assistant Scoutmaster Young had charge of Crosfield's Boy Scouts, and Assistant Scoutmaster Williams commanded the Whitecross Troop.</p>
        <p>THE CIVIC AND PUBLIC DIGNITARIES.</p>
        <p>After the military and semi-military bodies came the civic dignitaries; preceded by the Fire Brigade (under Superintendent F. H. Turner), and a detachment of the Borough Police (with the Chief Constable in command). The Mayor,. with the mace and sword-hearers, walked at the head 'of the representatives of the Corporation, the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) coming next, and after him the following members and officials of the Town Council :—Aldermen Ben- •nott, James Evans and Tinnion, Councillors Barton, Chantler, Chrimes, Craik, Cunnington, Dickenson, Gough, Halliwell, Jenkins, Lash- ford, Lindley, . Locker, Shaw and Skelland. Officials': The " Tewn Clerk (Mr. •J. • Lyon Whittle); Mr.•A. M. Ker (borough engineer and surveyor), Mr. M. A. Piercey (deputy surveyor), .Mr. George 11.• F. White (magistrates' clerk), Mr. F. G. Ruth-lock (public analyst), Mr. Walter T. Flood (inspector of nuisances), Mr. Robert Young (cemetery registrar), Mr. R. Whitfield (head gardener), Mr. W. S. Howard (cashier, gas ' department), Mr. C., Madeley .(musenan director and librarian), • Mr. J. Gray (waterworks. engineer) and Mr. F. .V. L. Mathias (electrical and tramways engineer). Other public -bodies represented were : Local Magistrates: Messrs. S. Wright, William Furness,-.T...Locker and Joseph Bailey. Education Committee: Mr. J. M. Murray (director). • Board. of Guardians: Mr. A. J. Jolley (chair- man), 'Miss Broadbent, Mrs. Hall and Messrs. R. Lee and F. McKinley. Insurance Committee: Mrs. Mary Roberts, Mrs.. Annie Bell,. and Messrs. S. Brocklehurst, W. E. Clegg, Peter Delaney, Joseph Elwell, J. F. Forster and J. Kendrick.</p>
        <p>Infirmary and Dispensary: Mr. J. IT. Smethurst (chairman of the Board of Manage- ment), am! Messrs. George Walker, H. Collin, W. Daintith, R. Cook and J. 11. J. Hampson (secretary).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</head>
        <p>THE FRIENDLY SOCIETIES.</p>
        <p>The rear of the procession was brought up by the representatives of the various Friendly Societies. the band of the R.A.0.8., under Mr. Robert Smith. marching at the head of this section, Bro. T. P. Bradshaw, P.P.G.M., of the</p>
        <p>M.U. 1.0.0. F., acting as chief marshal. Other Societies represented, with the names of their respective marshals, were : R.A.0.8. : Bro. J. Holden. Independent Order of Oddfellows (Manchester Unity): Bro. W. Holbrook. National Independent Order of Oddfellows: Bro. Hands.</p>
        <p>Loyal Ancient Order of Shepherds: Bro. W. L. Clegg. Free Gardeners: Bro. Budge. Ancient Order of Foresters: Bro. W. Hipkiss.</p>
        <p>Ancient Order of Druids: Bro. W. Bibby</p>
        <p>Catholic Friendly Society; Irish National Foresters and Ancient Order of Hibernians : Bro. R. Hennigan. Grand United Order of Oddfellows : Bro. J. Green. Hearts of Oak and Rational Association: Bro. Lingard.</p>
        <p>At each side of the entrance to the church- yard a number of Red Cross nurses stood lined up as the procession filed past into the sacred edifice. The nurses belonged to the 22nd Cheshire Detachment (under °Commandant Miss M. Broadbent) and the 48th Cheshire Detach- ment.</p>
        <p>MAYOR'S HEALTH TOASTED. His Worship's Work For National And Imperial Patriotism.</p>
        <p>After the service the procession wended its way back to the Town Hall, where before dis- persing the processionists sang the National Anthem, Master Reginald Peacock the while waving a Union Jack. Light refreshments were afterwards served in the Council. Chamber, where a few speeches were made, the Rector expressing his thanks to the Mayor and Corporation and other local bodies for their attendance at church, and ex- pressed the hope that the music had been enjoyed.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett, in felicitous terms, pro- posed the health of the Mayor, wishing his Wor- ship a very happy and prosperous year of office, though, he said, it was difficult to think of happiness and prosperity in these dark and gloomy times. Still the spirit of the English nation always came out better in stormy times, and they had "a pulse like a cannon" as a great American writer once expressed it. He was quite sure they would weather the storm and come out stronger and better than ever. (Hear, hear.) That day's service and the •civie spirit it represented was evidence of that. That spirit was also represented in every way by the Mayor, who was ,sparing no 'effort to promote not only local but national and imperial patriotism. He (Alderman Bennett) had no doubt we should go through this horrible war chastened and purified, and • When the hurly-burly's done, When the battle's lost and won,</p>
        <p>we should have a happier town. and nation as well as a greater Empire, and have probably done something to guarantee throughout: tie generations the future peace of the wi rid. (Applauße.)</p>
        <p>The toast having been honoured, his Worship briefly returned thanks, going on to remark that these were gloomy times, and the gloomy weather that morning must have sent their thoughts to the battlefield, and to the sea, where their soldiers and sailors were fighting such desperate battles for them. But there was always a bright spot in every dark cloud, and while a dark cloud was overhanging them, especially that day, he was optimistic enough to believe that brightness would in the not very distant again shine upon England as in the past. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>"THE BRIDGE OF SIGHS."</head>
        <p>"THE BRIDGE OF SIGHS."</p>
        <p>Vicar Of Burtonwood Heaves</p>
        <p>A Sigh Of Relief.</p>
        <p>As we have already recorded, the new bridge at Sankey Bridges has at length been completed and opened for traffic. The occasion, in view of the inconvenience caused during the transi- tion from the old- to the new state of things, .has inspired 'the -following humorous outpour- ing' by 'the Rev. -A. M. Mitchell in his Burton- wood Parish Magazine for November:— •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BRIDGE OF SIGHS.</head>
        <p>THE BRIDGE OF SIGHS.</p>
        <p>The Bridge of Sighs' Who has not heard of it? Who has not pas4ed over it? For us, for many, many weary months the Bridge of Sighs has been at Sankey. The San- ke) Bridge is well named the Bridge of Sighs. Chauffeurs, motor-waggon and engine-drivers, carriage and foot passengers will all concur. _ _ _</p>
        <p>The-inhabitants of ". die raided" districts— raided by heavy road-destructive waggon traffic —Bold and Burtonwood especially, have for a long year and upwards been only too familiar with the Bridge of Sighs. Of late, the mere mention of Sankey Bridge in either parish has evoked a sigh, a deep sigh, too, a sigh which might be heard at Warrington, if not at " proud " Preston. All things come to an end sooner or later, even the sighing of the miserable. The discomfort, inconvenience, weariness of the cruel situation, has terminated; at last, at last, we can hardly believe it, the bridge is re- ported complete and actually opened for traffic. As we write this down we sigh, sigh a big, deep, soughing sigh of relief. Our roads, our lanes, our men 'rejoice. Can't you hear them singing? Or should we ask "sighing"? The sigh of relief is a tuneful sigh, there is -music in it, it is a brief momentary song of praise and thanksgiving! The Bridge of Sighs still re- mains, but no longer as the bridge of sorrowful sighing, of the long-suffering, but the bridge of thankful, glad sighs, of patient miserables re- lieved. Long may the new Bridge of Sighs re- main. May it wear well! May it voice for many years the sigh of satisfaction and con- tent " !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>OTHER SIDE OF THE PICTURE.</head>
        <p>THE OTHER SIDE OF THE PICTURE.</p>
        <p>We in Warrington have cause to sigh because the bridge has been completed. The heavy traffic between Liverpool and Manchester, which was diverted from the Warrington route, to the discomfiture, as we have seen, of the Rev. A. M. Mitchell and his country folk, has come back to the Warrington .streets, and the cotton trains that nowadays traverse our main roads are ,once again 'crossing the !midge and slowly, rattling down our houses by ,their- weight and vibration; Last Friday night one .of these monsters broke an axle when on the tramline in Church-street, and held up the tram service , till well into Saturday. Much inconvenience was caused to people going to and from the market, and there were loud complaints about the length of time token to clear the tramlines. ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>SUPPER FOR "TOMMY."</head>
        <p>SUPPER FOR "TOMMY."</p>
        <p>Warriors Entertained At Bold-street Home.</p>
        <p>Saturday of. last week was a special day at the local Soldiers' Home in Bold-street. Through the kindness of Mrs. John •Fairhurst, Mrs. Frank Harris, Mrs. Plinston and Miss 'Jones and others, a " hot-pot " 'supper was provided free for all „soldiers who clued to partake of the same, and, needless to say, the innovation proved a great success. Quite an animated scene met the eye, when at 7.30 the steaming .viands . Were brought in; and khaki-clad " Tommies " from almost every part of the Empire Passed quite visibly .from an attitude of expectation, to one. of satisfaction, and, finally, to another of gratification. Men from Scotland and Ireland and most of the counties •of England were there. A brave; fine, upstand- ing member of the glorious Belgian Army, who is just about to return to the Front, was also present, as well as men from Barbadoes and distant Manitoba. Though all were ,miles away from home and kindred, with grace • and kindly tact the ladies made plain the fact that a " home " in Warrington might be found With ever "wide open door." Though no formal votes of thanks were passed (indeed there was no formality), extreme appreciation was ex- pressed to the officials.. It.. is said that the absence of irritating rules, the bountiful pro- vision" of all kinds of games and amusements, the stock of reading matter, the writing room, daintily furnished, and 7 the unostentatious. management make . the Warrington " home " one of the most talked of in Flanders, Gallipoli and Egypt, as well as -where soldiers have a rendezvous in the United Kingdom. Mr. Lumb and Mr. - Isaac, the secretary and treasurer respectively, are always willing to show visitors round the place, and we can com- mend it to the kindly interest of all sections of the Warrington public.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY,</head>
        <p>TEMPERANCE SUNDAY,</p>
        <p>Should Warrington Be Made A</p>
        <p>Scheduled Area?</p>
        <p>CURSE OF THE AGES.</p>
        <p>Mayor's Sunday was also observed by the local Nonconformist bodies as Temperance Sunday, special reference being made in the sermons at most of the chapels to the drink evil. In the evening came a big public meeting at the Co-operative Hall, over which Mrs. James Dodds presided, being supported by the Revs. A. J. Morton, W. Reid and W. Thompson Derry, and Messrs. Dale and Gandy (secretary of the Warrington Band of Hope Union).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>WONDERFUL CAUSE.</head>
        <p>A WONDERFUL CAUSE.</p>
        <p>The meeting having opened with the hymn Sing we a song of praise' to-day and with prayer,</p>
        <p>- Mrs. Dodds said she thought it had been proved during the past year what a wonderful cause the temperance cause was—so great, so attractive and so necessary. Even the King of the Realm had thrown in his lot with those who were on the side of temperance. (Applause.) They were glad, and proud, that night, to be able to say that they stood beside his Majesty. (Renewed applause). They had cause to be thankful for the work that had been done, and, despite the horrors of the past year, they could not, somehow, help feeling that they had reaped a little bit of what they, as temperance people had sown; they could not, somehow, help feel- ing the more convinced that all along the line they had been on the right side! Let them take heart of grace and resolve, more than ever, that they would persevere. (Hear, hear.) They knew not what was before them, but they did know that their cause was a right one. It was a holy cause, a righteous cause, and if they were true to it they would - find that God would honour their faith and honoUr their trust, and that greal good would be done, because it was done in His name. (Applause). They often met with discouragement. In this town the fight was such an uphill one, but she was convinced that God would bless their efforts and give them fruit of their labour.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>IMPOTENCE OF THE GOVERNMENT.</head>
        <p>IMPOTENCE OF THE GOVERNMENT.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. J. Morton, in the course of a striking address, said there was one thing for which he would be grateful to the war and that was for the light which it had thrown on the drink traffic, thus compelling the nation, in some degree, to grapple with the life-destroying octopus, which, for so long, had thrust out its baneful tentacles over the lend. Surely all true temperance reformers must have' been pain- fully impressed by the apparent impotence of the Government to face, and deal with, the hydra-headed monster—strong drink! About twice in his life had he felt almost ashamed to acknowledge his nationality. Those periods of humiliation had been caused by the attitude of the Governments of the day towards drink and the opium trade. Many another Englishman must have blushed with shame and been filled with bitter regret. Who, the speaker went on, could help but feel the utmost contempt for that Member of Parliament who, the other day, with his debased humour, could make the question put by Mr. Leif Jones about treating in the House of Commons a subject for mocking merriment? (Hear, hear.) Were temperance people not right in looking to the House of Commons for a lead and an example? and it was a scandalous thing that a subject so closely connected with the destiny of the nation should have been treated with levity. The reverend gentleman went on to speak of the lads who are fighting at the Front for the ideal of brotherhood and righteousness, and went on to ask wfiy, in this matter of the drink traffic, this nation should go on humiliating itself in the eyes of the allied nations, as well as under- mining themselves?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>ritiful Response to King's Lead.</head>
        <p>ritiful Response to King's Lead.</p>
        <p>The King spoke out on this subject, like the brave man he is; and one of the most pitiful things about the war was the meagre response to his example. 'I used," the speaker said, "to think that drink was the King of England, but, thank God, that King is going to be de- throned through this war. Mr. Lloyd George was beaten by it in the House of Commons; the Cabinet went under before it ! Let us hope that when the history of this war is written, that the Cabinet will give us something in the shape of a justification why they withdrew from the attack on the arch-enemy, alcohol. Certainly they have missed' a great opportunity for an act of sublime courage—an opportunity of showing that they 'were in nothing terrified by their adversaries.'" (Hear, hear.) The speaker added that he was grieved beyond expression at the great number of so-called Christians who had refused to follow the King's example. Rather than give up a little self-indulgence they *had continued to aid 'and abet that horrible traffic which had robbed the country of so many of her promising sons—the traffic which had slain so many. He did not think that temper- ance people would gain much by constantly girding at the brewers and the distillers; people who had, he would not say, caused 99 per cent., but, put it at 90 per cent, of the misery and crime in the world. More Murderous Than Pestilence,</p>
        <p>No pestilence crawling over the face Of the earth had such murderous powers as the alco- holic poisons which the brewer and the distiller produced. And yet, when these gentlemen had made their fortunes, and had blasted the hap- piness of thousands of homes, and had destroyed the value of many streets, the country paid them rich honours. ("Shame.") They called them Sir This and Lord That! And when the wife of one of those "barrel-knights" or peers gave a ball—on which she spent, maybe, .2.5,000; £2,000 going in flowers—the _ mob of so-called society ached and hankered to get invitations to the function, oblivious of the cries and groans of men and women which mingled with the dance music. But, as he had said, it was no use girding; they must do something else— something more-practicable; they must go down and help the cringing, broken slave to alcohol. Not Reason, But Sympathy And Help.</p>
        <p>Figures and abstractions were nothing to the man whom the temperance worker wanted to save. They could talk statistics to him until they were blue in the face; but they were of very little help to the man whose soul was all athirst for the terrible stuff. Let them remem- ber that the dullest quack who ever quacked— (laughter)—eould always clinch his argument by statistics; but that fact did not make him any the less dull and ineffective. No; if help .vas to be .given it must be given through the medium of the touch of human love, through the influence of personal magnetism. Workers in the cause would probably not have to attend more temperance meetings, but fewer. They would have to go out and carry a little bit of •what they had learned and .experienced to the side of the brother who had fallen—to live with him, by him and near him, and make him strong. (Hear, hear.) The cure of intemper- ance was a great work, but the prevention of intemperance would be a much better one. As temperance workers let them accept everything and anything that would advance them one little bit towards their great goal. Sunday Closing.</p>
        <p>Let_ them go in for .Sunday closing—(hear, hear)—and let them endeavour to get the pub- lic-houseS closed on the day before elections and on election day itself. (Hear, hear.) Let them endeavour to keep the present war regulations in force after the war, is over; let them do all they could to keep. the Ptiblic-houses closed as long as they coLld in the morning and to get them shut up as early as possible in the even- ing. (Hear, hear.) Somehow, he could not help feeling that the present War-time regulations had come to stay. (Applause.) Temperance people must look after their Members of Parlia- ment and must see that they were straight on this question, and they must also do all in their power to keep publicans and brewers off the city, town and parish councils. (Hear, hear.) If they failed in keeping them off the city and town. councils, by all means let them endeavour all they could to keep them off the Watch Com- mittees. (Hear, hear.) In his opinion no lion- est.or self-respecting brewer or publican would allow himself to be elected on a Watch Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>" A Lesson In Decency."</p>
        <p>Should .a brewer or a publican attempt to get on to such a committee, it was up to temper- ance people to teach him a lesson in decency. It was most important; ntiy, it was essential that the children in the schools should be given definite instruction with regard to the effect of alcohol on human life; and .he was anxious</p>
        <p>that each day school should include on its staff teachers qualified to give such instruction—in- struction which should form part of.the curri- culum as geography and history. (Hear, hear.) It was a grand thing to think that it had been made possible to keep the children out of the public-housed; it would be grander to remove the public-houses altogether. (Applause.) Let them hope and pray that out of the war there would come a sober and a greater England, for if such a consummation were brought about it would have been worth while to have had the war. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0041" n="art0041">
        <head>SYMPATHY FOR THE VICTIM.</head>
        <p>SYMPATHY FOR THE VICTIM.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. Reid also addressed the meeting at length. He deprecated the fact that, over and over again, where temperance was men- tioned in certain quarters, there was not lacking a great deal of abuse, which was poured out on the head of the unfortunate victim of drink. "Why on earth does he not know when to stop? —that was the burden of the argument. Not the slightest sympathy was extended to the drinker in the quarters to which he referred, but, so to speak, any amount of sympathy was given to the stuff itself, whilst any amount of sympathy was also extended to the man who made it, and to the man who sold it. But, when it was a case of considering the victim; the poor, unfortunate wretch who had been victimised by drink—why, then the people, to whom he referred, shut up their hearts of com- passion against him and had no sympathy to extend to him. It seemed to him (the speaker) that the hankering after the pleasure of the drinking-bout was something that was inherent in human nature. "I am," said the speaker, "a life-long abstainer, but—if this does not sound like an Irishism—l know the taste of strong drink, of spirits and Or wines; and I have the firm conviction in my own mind that only for the grace of God—and, perhaps, the scarcity of money—twelve months need not have elapsed before I might have been a victim of strong drink. Where did I get that, shall 1 say, desire from? I do not know!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0042" n="art0042">
        <head>Latent Desire In Human Nature.</head>
        <p>A Latent Desire In Human Nature.</p>
        <p>"The only way I can account for it is on the hypothesis that there is something latent in human nature; a latent craving that has been there a long time." When they read Scripture, they learned that away back down the ages men knew quite well what it was to make intoxi- cating liquor and to drink it, and what it was to fall under its baneful influence, and to become outcasts because of it. He half believed that that apple business in the Garden of Eden long ago was a cider incident, if only the facts were properly discovered. (Laughter.) Seriously, for ages back the human race had been victimised by the horrible curse of strong drink. They knew how speciously it covered itself; how it argued most subtly; how it would pass itself off upon them as if it were some .wholesomo. kind of food and medicine! And was it not' strange that they believed all that; was it not strange that human nature was so constituted that it liked to be deceived; that, in fact, there were occasions on which it was never happy unless it could deceive itself? How many apologists for strong drink had quoted St. Paul's words to Timothy, "Take a little wine for thy stomach's sake"? The best answer when those words were quoted, was that given by the Irishman who said "Shure, an' me name's not Timothy, and if it was there's nothin' the matter wi' me stomach !" (Laugh- ter.) Still, it was remarkable how much there was discovered to be the matter with some people's digestive apparatii when they read that passage of Scripture. (More laughter.)</p>
        <p>"A Man Once."</p>
        <p>Temperance people should never make the mistake of forgetting that the drink-wrecked ,!reature had been a man once; and would be a man still, save for the fact that there was an enemy without and an enemy within, combining to bring him down. A plausible argument used by those who defended strong drink was that the very fact that human nature desired it, and that nature made such an ample provision for the manufacture of it, was pJtoof that it was one of God's best gifts. But the argument was a wrong one. Strong drink was not a gift of God: it was a gift of God which had been prostituted. Referring to the fact that there were churches in the country which derived income froth brewery shares, the speaker said that the first call to the churches was to cleanse their own garments. The minister who preached temperance was not popular in War- rington, and he need never expect to be so, and he would get no gratuities. (Hear, hear, and laughter.) But, if he was going to stop his temperance work because of that he might as well give up and go right over at once. Tem- perance people need insult nobody, but they could speak the truth and live the truth, and keep themselves clean and undefiled. They might have to become martyrs and. if they were not prepared for possible martyrdom they had better draw out of the movement at once. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0043" n="art0043">
        <head>WHY IS NOT WARRINGTON A SCHEDULED AREA?</head>
        <p>WHY IS NOT WARRINGTON A SCHEDULED AREA?</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. Thompson Derry expressed the thanks of the meeting to the chairlady and speakers and proposed that, if the meeting was to have any practical value, it should make a formal protest against the fact that Warring- ton had not been included as a scheduled area under the existing Restriction Order. They were told that they could not win in the present war except by means of - men, money and munitions. They had found many men in the town, they had turned out a considerable quan- tity of munitions and they had tried to do some- thing in the way of thrift—so as to raise some of the money—but to his mind it was trifling with the money question to let it stop at such suggestions that people should substitute mar- garine for butter, nothing being said about the fearful waste of money in intoxicants, which went on day by day and week by week. (Hear, hear.) If the King could do without' alcohol, surely other men could. (Hear, hear.) Mrs. Dodds seconded, and the proposition having been carried, the meeting terminated with the pronouncement of the Benediction.</p>
        <p>During the meet ng the 'United Temperance Choir rendered Gounod's "By Babylon's Wave" and Stephen Adams's "Comrades' Song of Hope," both splendid compositions being splen- didly sung. The large choir was under the baton of Mr. A. Spruce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0044" n="art0044">
        <head>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. BROTHERHOOD.</head>
        <p>WYCLIFFE P.S.A. BROTHERHOOD.</p>
        <p>English And Welsh Sundays Contrasted.</p>
        <p>An open meeting was held under the auspices of the P.S.A. Brotherhood on Sunday after- noon, the Rev. A. J. Chessum presiding, in the absence of Mrs. Dodds, who, at the last moment, found herself, unable to attend. In the course of his opening remarks, Mr. Chessum regretted the hindering of good influences that went on in Warrington, owing to the drink traffic. He had, the reverend gentleman went on, recently spent Sunday in Wales, and, thanks to the closing of the public-houses, there seemed to pass through the air a sweet calm, that was meet and fitting to the Lord's Day. In conclu- sion, Mr. Chessum expressed the pleasure he felt at . being able to help in some slight way the noble cause of the Warrington Temperance Union.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Hoyle,- of Ashton-on-Mersey, gave an earnest address, remarking that if them were many things that Warrington could be compli- mented upon there was one thing that it could :not be complimented on and that was the posses- sion of so many public-houses. Drink was, without doubt, the greatest enemy this country had to contend with, and the problem, "How can we overcome it?" was a terribly difficult one —one which- the temperance' bodies of the coun- try were doing their best to solve. It was the duty of the Churches and of the State to save the nation from the drink curse.</p>
        <p>The United Temperance choir, under the con- ductorship of Mr. A. Spruce. sang the anthems "By Babylon's Wave" and "The Comrade's Song of Hope." Mr. T. Tanner was at the organ, Messrs. T. Dale and Carter took part in the meeting.</p>
        <p>M.P. FOR ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Rigby Swift Recorder Of. Wigan.</p>
        <p>It is officially announced that the King, on the recommendation of the Home Secretary, has appointed Mr. Rigby Swift, K.C., M.P., to be Recorder of Wigan, in place of Sir Lancelot Sanderson, K.C., who resigned on his appoint- ment as Chief Justice of Bengal. Mr -2n:by Swift's appointmt will necessi- tate his. seeking re-election for St. Helens, which he represents in the Unionist interest.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <p>CHAPPED HANDS &amp; BLOOD-POISON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>Daily Perils of Housewives.</head>
        <p>The Daily Perils of Housewives.</p>
        <p>When the cold weather sets in there is no more common cause of blood-poisoning among housewives and other workers than "chaps." It is the easiest thing in the world for disease germs and poisonous grit to enter the blood through a break of the skin, caused by rough- ness, rawness, or cracks indttcP.l by the cold and inclement weather.</p>
        <p>Chapped hands, sore lips, and cold-sores generally, are not only dangerous, but annoy- ing, and women with sensitive skins often suffer untold misery, especially after a busy day at the washing-tub or general house-work.</p>
        <p>Hands which suffer like this can be restored to a sound and healthy condition by the appli- cation of Zam-Buk. This Pure balm exerts a delightful, cooling, and sbothing influence, and quickly renders the skin soft, white and strong again, so that it can withstand the weather. Zam-Buk is the ideal skin-protector. Its unique healing powers are helped by the absolute purity and the great antiseptic value of its herbal ingredients. Zam-Buk contains none of the rancid animal fats and coarse mineral poisons such as are found in ordinary ointments and salves.</p>
        <p>Zam-Buk is prepared solely from the saps and juices of rake healing plants, and these extracts are so highly refined that they are easily and naturally absorbed by the skin. Keep ever by you that needful box of Zam-Buk. A nightly dressing with the pure balm will prove an inestimable boon. You can buy Zam-Buk at any chemists or drug stores. But be sure you get Zam-Buk. Owing to the war the prices now are !--for ordinary size ls.lid. plus lid. war-tax; for large size 2s. 9d. plus 3d. war-tax. If your chemist is out of stock, don't be put off with something else but send direct to The Zam-Buk Laboratories, Leeds.</p>
