      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE.</p>
        <p>St. Paul's Company Roil Of</p>
        <p>Honour.</p>
        <p>The Church Lads' Brigade has responded to the call of the country more generously than even its friends who had found for the past twenty-four years' military means effective to the formation of the Christian character, could have dreamed. Over 120,000 of its members have joined the Colours, and among their distinctions are 3 V.C.'s and numerous D.C.M.'s and Mili- tary. Crosses. In addition, it has raised the 16th (Service) Battalion, King's Royal Rifles (C.L.8.) entirely from its ranks. This Bat- talion has now gone to the Front, and the second Battalion is in formation at Andover, Hants. Among other public patriotic works at pre- sent the C.L.B. forms over half of the entire Cadet Force, and remains the only definitely Church organisation which deals with the Christian training of lads between the ages of 14 and 19.</p>
        <p>That the Warrington Battalion is in the fore- front of these patriotic services is testified by the long roll of honour which we have the pleasure to give below in connection with the strong and successful company attached to the St. Paul's Church, a company ably supported by the members of the parish, and in particular so enthusiastically conducted by the officers, Captain Bunch and Lieutenant Morris, who have also working with them warrant officers who do not spare themselves in advancing the best interests of the company. The following members, present and past, of the St. Paul's Company, Warrington (2872), of the second Battalion Liverpool Regimert of the lads' Brigolle, are ser di.g in the war with the regime/As stated:—</p>
      </div>
