      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>raters to the sifhitgr AMONGST THE HEATHEN.</head>
        <p>raters to the sifhitgr AMONGST THE HEATHEN.</p>
        <p>BELGIANS IN BELGIUM.</p>
        <p>Sir,—l enclose for the information of your readers a copy of a letter I have received from the Lord Lieutenant of Lancashire. I may say I shall be very pleased to receive any subscrip- tions in aid of this worthy object, and to for- ward them on behalf of Warrington to the County Fund. PETER PEACOCK, Mayor.</p>
        <p>Town Clerk's Office, Warrington, 12th November, 1915.</p>
        <p>[copy.) Gawthorpe Hall, Burnley, Lancashire, _</p>
        <p>11th November, 1915.</p>
        <p>Dear Sir,—As President of the Lancashire County Committee, which has been forthed to co-operate with the National Committee, for Relief in Belgium, I sincerely trust that you will feel disposed to assist us in our efforts to keep from starvation the destitute Belgian people who have remained in Belgium. Their needs are increasing with the approach of winter, and funds are more urgently required than ever. If a branch of the County Fund has been opened by the Mayor, I trust that you will give him all possible support. All contributions will be credited to our County Fund.</p>
        <p>Yours faith-fully, SHUTTLEWORTH, Lord-Lieutenant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>MEMORIAL TO NURSE CAVELL.</head>
        <p>MEMORIAL TO NURSE CAVELL.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Will anyone who wishes to subscribe to this memorial, which is to take the form of .. Nurses' Home at the Lc•ldon Hospital, kindly send their donation to me here? The home, which is being built, was to have been called the Alexandra Home, but our President, H.M. Queen Alexandra, has ex- pressed a wish that the name should be changed to "The Cavell Home." To all time, Nurse Cavell's name and example will, by this memorial, be present to the nurses in her old ttf aining school. KNUTSFORD, Chairman, - London Hospital.</p>
        <p>Whitechapel, E., 15th November, 1915</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>COST OF POSTAGE FOR SOLDIERS'</head>
        <p>COST OF POSTAGE FOR SOLDIERS'</p>
        <p>PARCELS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—A fortnight ago I sent a small parcel out to my son in hospital, Cairo, Egypt. The weight of the box was lib., value of contents is. 3d., postage is. Had that box been about 71b., what would it have cost? If the parcel had been going, say, to London, it would have cost about 3d. Now, I do not complain for myself only, but for many a poor mbther, wife, sister, and friend, who are charged these extortionate prices for a few small gifts, which our soldiers are glad to receive. They would receive more were the post- age not so heavy. Why should relatives of our soldiers have to pay more for postage than for the contents of the parcel? I myself 'think that it ought to be an honour and a pleasure for our Government either to deliver free of charge, or at a reduced rate, parcels to our men who are fighting for freedom to-day. LUCY AUSTIN. - Holly Bush, Rixton.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>TRADE UNIONISTS ON CO-OPERA-</head>
        <p>TRADE UNIONISTS ON CO-OPERA-</p>
        <p>TIVE COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>Sir,—In perusing the brief report of the monthly.meeting of the Warrington Trades and Labour Council in the last issue of "The Ex- aminer," I notice that they have decided to recommend two of their members for the Sup- port of trade unionists in their efforts to secure representation on the committee of the local Co-operative Society, as representing the trade union movement. I am pleased to see that their efforts in the past have been more successful in securing a predominant representation on the committee of the local Co-operative Society, and with much less expense, tlian in attempting to secure a just and fair representation on the Town Coun- cil. I hope the day is not far distant when they will be successful, and that they will not be satisfied until this is accomplished. What I want to point out is that while the Trades and Labour Council have a perfect, and legitimate, right to nominate two of ;their mem- bers for the' support' of trade unionists for the local Co-operative Committee, they have no right to ignore the fact that the two members they have recommended do not represent the whole volume of trade union opinion in War- rington, while, on the other hand, there may be other trade unionist nominations for the Co- operative Committee, and although not affiliated with the Trades and Labour Council, they have a right to represent the opinions and voice of the Trade Union movement.</p>
        <p>We need to bear in mind that the majority of the members of our local Co-operative move- ment are not trade unionists, and that there are many trade unionists who,are not co-opera- tors; and even trade union officials overlook the fact that the Co-operative movement offers ex- ceptional facilities for the investment of their funds, and yet trade union funds are largely in- vested in capitalistic concerns. It may be of interest and information to trade unionists generally to know that at least two-thirds, not including the President of our local Co-operative Committee, are members of trade union organisations, and that trade union principles and interests are rigidly and jealously guarded. A CO-OPERATOR &amp; TRADE UiiIONIST.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</head>
        <p>DRINK RESTRICTIONS.</p>
        <p>is quite evident that your corre- spondent—Mr. Simpson, of Liveipool—would not believe, or at any rate would not admit that! he believed the logic of facts in regard to drinking facilities and the inevitable results therefrom, no matter what amount of irrefut- able evidence was submitted to his considera- tion. If he had the experience which large employers of labour are constantly passing through he would know that over-indulgence in drink is in a greater or less degree the curse of nearly every worshop in the country, and he 'would know further that if the " facilities " could only be ,suppressea from Saturday noon until Monday noon, there would be practically no absentees from duty when the workshops open on Monday morning. What we are discussing, however, is the de- sirability or otherwise of getting the Central Control Board to schedule Warrington as a "Munitions Area," and I do not hesitate to say that if the decision were left to the responsible working heads of the various con- cerns in the town, and no social or political influence were used, there would be a practi- cally unanimous decision in favour of inclusion Only a day or two ago I met two gentlemen— neither of them teetotallers—one of whom is the responsible head of a big department in a huge works largely engaged on munitions, and the other has for many months been buying and dispatching large supplies for the Govern- ment, and both men assured me that the time lost through the excessive facilities which obtain for the supply of liquor was deplorable in the extreme.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>Wesleyan Missionary Describes Work In West Africa.</head>
        <p>Wesleyan Missionary Describes Work In West Africa.</p>
        <p>EFFORT THAT PAYS.</p>
        <p>The cause of the Wesleyan Foreign Missions was earnestly pleaded at the Bewsey-road Wes- leyan Church on Sunday, both by the minister of the church (the Rev. W. Thompson Derry) and by a missionary who; Was able to speak from first-hand knowledge of what the W.F.M.S. is accomplishing in one great tract of the great Continent of Africa. The Rev. Mr. Derry urged the claims of the Society' from the pulpit in the morning, and the missioner-deputation, the Rev. 0. J. Griffin, added his plea for sup-</p>
        <p>_ port and sympathy at the evening service, when there was an excellent congregation. In the course of his Sermon the Rev. Mr. Griffin told of the wonderfully, successful circuits which had been established along the coast line of Western Africa, how the Christian message was eagerly sought after, and how the people 'crowded into the churches, congregations of 600, 800, 1,000 and even 1,500 souls, being quite common. And what was very wonderful was that, so far as part of the West Africa he knew so well .was concerned, all the churches were self.support- ing. Not a halfpenny went from the funds to any of the churches along the coast line; the churches had taken upon themselves the respon- sibility of their own work. And their zeal did not stop at looking after their own churches and congregations, for they sent their converts to do home missionary work in the hinterlands of their country.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>"RELIGIOUS" RACES.</head>
        <p>"RELIGIOUS" RACES.</p>
        <p>Natives Who Are Steeped in Misguided Faith. Mr. Isaac Barlow presided over a public meet- ing held in the Bewsey-road Chapel on Monday evening—a meeting which, despite the incle- mency of the weather, wag attended by a most encouraging number.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>YEAR'S WORK LOCALLY AND NATIONALLY.</head>
        <p>YEAR'S WORK LOCALLY AND NATIONALLY.</p>
        <p>The Hon. Secretary presented the balance sheet, which showed that altogether the local circuit raised £316 12s. 4d. last year for foreign missions. Of that sum £27 is. was raised at the Bewsey-road Church. That figure showed a de- crease on the previous year's total of £3 Bs. id., a fact which was due to a few of their best sub- scribers having left the town. He was grieved that there should have been a decrease during his first year of secretaryship, and trusted that _ _</p>
        <p>the church would make it its special business during the coming year to see that the falling off was made good. (Hear, hear.) Taking the Society as a whole, • the total income was 015,764, and there was a deficit at the end of the year of £9,519 9s. 6d., as against £11,977 14s. sd. at the end of the preceding year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>RECORD YEAR FOR CONVERTS.</head>
        <p>RECORD YEAR FOR CONVERTS.</p>
        <p>The report was received with satisfaction, and the Rev. W. Thompson Derry then proceeded to read a number of extracts from the annual report from headquarters, which dealt with progress made in the various parts of the mission field where the Wesleyan Society is active. The total number of converts received by baptism during the year, as a result of the Society's efforts, was, he stated, 9,533— the highest total ever recorded for any one year. The net result was a splendid increase of 4,145 full members of the churches in the mission field during the twelve months. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>WORK IN FOUR CONTINENTS.</head>
        <p>WORK IN FOUR CONTINENTS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman then gave a brief address, in the course of which he recalled the mission meetings of years ago, and ventured the opinion that if they could again catch something of the spirit that inspired missionary effort in those days they would all feel much better for it. He was looking through the Society's annual report the other day, and he found that it worked—in four of the five continents, whilst it was a mat- ter of some surprise to him to find that they actually had their missionaries in Portugal, as well as in France, Spain and Italy. He often felt that the people at home did not sufficiently realise the extent of the work carried on by the Wesleyan Missionary Society, and of the need there was for that work. The work was many sided, embracing not only the ordinary church work, but also valuable educational work, done in schools and training colleges; library work, tract and booklet distributing work and medical missionary work. The Society had nearly 20,000 persons at work in the mission field; that number including, besides the missionaries, Sunday and day school teachers and local preachers. This huge army of missionary workers was divided among nearly 4,000 places of worship and centres of missionary effort, One interesting statistic in the report set forth that the number of full members in the mission field totalled something like 400,000. Is It Worth The Money?.</p>
        <p>There were people who said that missionary work cost a great deal of money, and were in- clined to doubt whether it was -worth while, but happily the number of such people was a diminishing quantity. No doubt missionary work was an expensive item, but it was well worth every penny spent on it, and they owed it as a duty to the memories of their forefathers— who did so much for the cause—to see to it that missionary enterprise was not allowed to dwindle or fall away at all. Each one could do "his bit" in the good work, and if they did that " bit " they might rest assured that they would, in the long run, reap benefit from so doing. The work could not be carried on unless it was backed up by the interest and the sub- stantial support of the people at home. Foreign missions had not to be looked at from a corn-</p>
        <p>mercial sfandpoint, but regarded from a higher —in fact from the highest—standpoint.</p>
        <p>THE CONTINENT OF CONTRASTS.</p>
        <p>The Rev. 0. J. Griffin then gave a fascinating address on missionary work in Africa, and more especially in that part of the great Con- tinent in which he has particularly laboured, viz., Lagos. To, give his audience an idea of the immensity of the Dark Continent, the speaker mentioned that into Africa the whole of China, India, Europe and the United States of America could be put, and that there would then be plenty of room to walk about in. Africa had been aptly dubbed by a well-known writer "A Continent of contradictions .and ex- tremes." It contained within its confines the oldest civilisation and the most uncivilised savagery. In Western Africa they got twelve hours of intense darkness and twelve hours of intense light; they -also got six months of rain and six months without any rain at all. Wes- leyan missionary effort in Western Africa was split up amongst three great tracts of country— Gold Coast, Sierra Leone and Lagos. The Lagos district itself was split up into three divisions— the great British colony of Nigeria, the French colony of Dahomey, and Tqgoland, which until recently was a German colony. He had been in Togoland when he had been the only Britisher there; all the other white men being Germans. But to-day there was a great change, and the country was now administered partly by the British and partly by the French.</p>
        <p>Germany's Lost Possession. In Togoland the Wesleyan Missionary Society had very great and special responsibilities, for it was left to that society to evangelise that big country—much larger than England—prac- tically single handed. The speaker then went on to describe in racy and intensely interest- ing fashion the kind of work which he has been doing amongst the natives of that ,part of the huge district in which he has more particularly laboured, and exhibited to the audience many curios, which he had brought home with him. In many parts, he said, the natives persisted in regarding him as a spirit, and left votive offer- ings in his path through the dense forests from town to town, but in the great towns they were now getting to know him very well, and had in- vented a name for him. It was peculiar to most of the African tribes that they always believed in spirits, and that those spirits were invari- ably evil ones; they never believed in good spirits. But the instinct of worship was in the heart and life of every heathen in the part of the world that he knew so well. Not an Irreligious Man in the Country.</p>
        <p>There was not an irreligious man in the whole country, in the sense that he had no religion; but instead of worshipping one godand one thing, they` worshipped many gods and many things. The people at home often sang the line "The heathen, in his blindness, bows down to wood and stone," and perhaps they imagined that that was a thing of the past, but it was not—it was not ancient history, but contemporary his- tory. The heathen did not love their gods; they hated them! And they believed that the gods returned that hate, and all the worship and sacrifice which they lavished upon their gods was done with a view to appeasing that hate and anger. There was one peculiar fact about the African heathen, and that was that it was never necessary for the Christian missionary to teach him that there was a life beyond the grave. He knew it and felt it already. They also had an inherent knowledge of and belief in a supreme being who was over and above all their gods.</p>
        <p>1 --</p>
        <p>The Young Idea. Rickey: "Mother, how old is dad?' Mother: "Forty-three dear. Why?" Rickey: "Oh. I am glad. fwas afraid-he'd funked."--" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDlTOR—continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <p>has ever seen, all our future is at stake, it is up to every one of us to suffer any incon- venience, deprivation or sacrifice in order to win, and yet there are men who wilfully close ,their eyes to the evidence which all our streets afford of the excessh-e self-indulgence-in alcohol on the part of a minority—and I nadmit not a big minority, but one actually guilty of great mischief—of our people.</p>
        <p>Why is it that our civic heads and the clergy and ministers of our town are so "dumb" on this vital question? Is it that personal in- terests on one hand and moral cowardice on the, other explain their silence? WaPrington boasts of its loyalty, and yet how many and whom amongst its prominent citizens are there who have followed their King's example? I am not without hope that in spite of Trade opposition and apparent apathy on the part of the public, it will be found before long that our town will be included amongst the districts defined as , "Munitions Areas," and I am confident that the uniformly satisfactory results which have followed the restrictions which the Control Board has established in other, places will come to pass here. I claim to knoii guite as well as "Mr. Simpson" what the -real sentiments of the vast majority of our working men are, and I assert emphatically that there is no feeling of irritation at the restrictions already imposed— they know too well the sacrifices which their families have in so many instances already suffered—they have given their sons to their country's defence, and they wish to do and they are doing all in their power to help to shorten the war and bring it to a victorious conclusion. Why should their efforts be handicapped by the thoughtless and careless amongst us? I submit that Mr. Simpson is posing as the protege of the brewer and the publican—and that his real interest in the working man is infinitesimal.— Yours, (te.,</p>
        <p>4 - - tit I - ,</p>
      </div>
