      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0065" n="art0065">
        <head>Pre-Germ Period.</head>
        <p>The Pre-Germ Period.</p>
        <p>"To what do you attribute your remarkable health?"</p>
        <p>_ "Well," replied the very old gentleman, "I reckon I got a good start on most people by bein' born before germs was discovered, thereby havin' less to worry about."</p>
        <p>What Was Wrong. Private Yeolaan "Wonder wot's wrong with the bally 'oss? 'E went orf all right, an' now. 'C won't move." Small jioy (pointing to the spurs): "Did yer touch 'im with them things Private Yeoman: "Course I did. 'E—" Small Boy (interrupting): "'B's punctured, guv'nor—that's what's wrong with 'im."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>Difference.</head>
        <p>The Difference.</p>
        <p>A motorist, who was touring in Ireland, one day met a native who was driving a donkey and cart.</p>
        <p>Thinking he would like to have a little fun at the Irishman's sexpense, he began : "What is the difference, Pat, between your turnout and mine?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <p>'S COMPLIMENTS TO THE DIRECTOR OF RECRUITING.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>THREE BROTHERS.</head>
        <p>THE THREE BROTHERS.</p>
        <p>How Lord Derby's Scheme</p>
        <p>Works In Practice.</p>
        <p>"Hello, have you seen the King's Appeal?" said Harold, looking up from the morning paper. No," said Jack, "what is it all about?" "The King has sent out a special appeal from Buckinghain Palace saying that the end of the war is ndt yet in sight, and he wants more men to join his Army and Navy." "Humph," exclaimed Bob, "that's all very well for you, Harold. You could go at a pinch, but what about me? Now lam working on torpedo nets, the firm won't let me go." ",Well," replied Harold, "'as a matter of fact I have been thinking of joining for some time May has been telling me that she thinks I ought to be in khaki like the other boys." "I will join you," said Jack, "but it will take me a month or two to settle up my affairs at the office." _ _ _</p>
        <p>"I would go, if my Boss would let me," added Bob. _ _</p>
        <p>The three brothers that very evening talked the matter over with their mother, who was a widow. She finally gave her verdict that she thought two of them ought to go, but she could not spare all three. That works exactly all I.ight," said Harold. "I will go to-morrow and join the Lancashire Fusiliers. Bob is a ' starred ' man, so he will have to stop here and will be able to look after you, •mother." Jack put in a word: "I wish I could come with you, Harold. I will talk to the governor to- morrow morning and see whether he could let me go in a week or two."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>Group System.</head>
        <p>The Group System.</p>
        <p>The following morning the three brothers read in the paper Lord Derby's Mansion House speech, and spent most of the time at breakfast arguing over the proposed group system. "That will just suit me," said Jack. "But I am going to wait a week or two to see how it works, and to see to which group I belong." Harold went that evening to the recruiting officer, and asked him if he could join the Lancashire Fusiliers. "Yes," said the recruit- ing officer, "there are plenty of vacancies in the, Bth, Battalion,. • You are not a ' starred' man, are your" "No," said Harold. "I am a draper's assistant, and I can join up in two days' time. Before I join I would like to know if I can get into the Life Guards. I have always wantOd to join a cavalry regiment." • , "No," said the recruiting officer, "there are very few vacancies in the cavalry, and-none at all in the Life Guards. We want all the men we can get in the infantry to shoot Germans." "Thank you," said Harold. 41`. I will be round here in two days' time."</p>
        <p>Accordingly, by the week-end Harold had been accepted by the doctor, attested, and had gone to the depot of the distinguished regiment which he had joined. The following day Jack received Lord Derby's letter asking him to enlist and whether the reason he had given hitherto still held good for not joining. He kept thinking about the letter the whole day through, and in the evening went to his master's office and asked whether he had permission to enlist. "Certainly," replied Mr. Cavanagh, a local solicitor. "In fact, I have been wondering for some time when you would want to go. I am glad you came and asked me like this, and I will be very "glad to pay you half wages when you have gone." "Thank you very much," said Jack. • "But if you could .stoh.with me another few weeks," .said Mr. Cavanagh, "it would be a great convenience. You could finish the work on which you are engaged. and leave things 'Square here. Have you seen the proposals for joining under the group system?" "No," said Jack.</p>
        <p>"This is the scheme, and a great 'help to employers it is," said Mr. Cavanagh. "This group system has been organised expressly for men like yourself—men who are not prepared to serve immediately, but are willing to come up when called on. If you join under the group system you will be medically examined, sworn in as a soldier, registered and put in your group. Let me see, how old are you?" "Twenty-four," said Jack. "Well, you will be in group 7, and some time in the future you will see a proclamation in Post Offices and on public buildings, stating that group 7 will commence to be called up in a few weeks' time. Then you will get a notice paper from the recruiting officer telling you exactly when you have to attend, and where you have to go." "Shall I get any pay?" asked Jack. " You.will get your pay for the day on which you are attested, but you won't get any fur- ther pay . until you actually join the colours. But, of course, while you are working with me I shall give you your weekly wage all the time." "But shan't I be worried by peel*, giving me white feathers?" asked Jack.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <p>showing that you belong to group 7, and you will also receive an armlet to put round your arm showing that you are a soldier of the King." "Thank you, sir," said Jack. "I will go off to-day and join under the group system." So Jack presented himself to the recruiting officer. He asked to join the Lancashire Fusi- liers, as that was the regiment to which his brother had gone. . .</p>
        <p>- The 'recruiting officer wrote on the attes- tation paper that this was the regiment Jack wished to join, and told him that so far as pos- sible he would go to the regiment which he chose when he uat, called up later. Jack then asked for the armlet. Regular Supply of Men Assured.</p>
        <p>Jack went home that night in high glee and told his mother what a sensible, simple system this was of joining the Army, giving every- body plenty of time to settle up their affairs. "It is rather hard on Lord Kitchener though, isn't it?" said his mother. "Surely he wants men now. He said he wanted more and more men."</p>
        <p>"Yes, but don't you see, mother," explained Jack, "if most of the men join in the group system and have been passed by the doctor, Lord Kitchener will know exactly how many men he can call upon in case of need, so he will be able to rely on a regular supply to keep up -the British Army in the field." Bob sat very grumpy during this conversa- tion, and finally burst out. "Bad luck on me," he said. "Harold will be swanking about- in uniform. You will have an armlet, and I shall simply be making torpedo nets 10 hours a day, and only wear a war badge to show for it." His mother tried to soothe him down, and'said how much she needed him at home. But Bob the following day went off himself to the re- cruiting officer and asked to join. The recruit- ing officer' looked him up in the register, and then said, "I am very sorry, but you are a ' starred ' man, and we cannot take you. You are doing just as good work, sonny, at the bench as you would do in the trench." ' This was not very satisfactory to Bob, and judge of his delight when a few days later he read that Lord Derby had made arrangementi with Mr: Lloyd George and the .Ministry of .Munitions and the other Government Depart- ments that all " starred " 'men could jour? rmy. Reserve B, provided they agreed to return :m- -mediately to their civil occupation. He 9. ent straight off to the recruiting officer and was .accepted. He was told that he would- be given, a khaki armlet to wear just like Jack. But he he was further told that he. would only be called up-for service if, and when, it had been decided that he would be more useful fighting than making torpedo,. nets,. and.. that. for, the tirne .being he was, to go back and help to win the war by werking. like steam at the wire works. Now the mother of the three brothers is going round telling her neighbours how proud she is of her three sons. They are all in His Majesty's Army, having responded to the King's call, and all will soon be wearing the King's mark show- ing they have been accepted for service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>WHEN "STARS" CEASE TO COUNT.</head>
        <p>WHEN "STARS" CEASE TO COUNT.</p>
        <p>With regard to the recruiting of skilled farm workers, Lord Selborne desires it to be clearly understood , that if a skilled agricultural labourer, who has been "starred" as such, leaves his employment on the farm in order to take up other work not connected with agricul- ture, the "starring" of that man will cease to be operative, and he will be liable to be can- vassed and enlisted for immediate service with .the colours.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>LANCASHIRE SHOW.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE SHOW.</p>
        <p>Not To Be Held Next Year Because 0 The " Royal " Visit.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting- of the Council of the Royal Lancashire Agricultural Society, was held at Derby House, Preston, Mr. Wyndham E. Hale presiding. The Secretary reported that the sum of £494 9s. had been granted to the Moor Park Hospital, Preston, and other grants to war funds, &amp;c., had been made amounting altogether to £1,167 Bs. The Secretary announced that the Finance Committee had met earlier in the day, and had asked Mr. Yerburgh to retain the position of president for a further twelve months. In view of the visit of the Royal Society to Manchester, the society would not hold an ex- hibition next year. The Finance Committee, however, recommended that there should be a show and sale on behalf of the Agricultural Relief of Allies' Fund during the spring, and that the arrangement for this should be left in the hands of the Society's Showyard Committee. This was agreed to. The Secretary said the society had now a balance at the bank of .22,000 in surplus cash, and it had been decided to invest this in War Loan stock.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <p>READ FEHNINGS' EVERY MOTHER'S BOOK. It contains Valuable Hints on Feeding, Teething, Weaning, Sleeping, etc. Ask your Chemist or Groe, IREF one will be sent. POST AT</p>
        <p>4 •- '.;rocer for a copy. T FREE, on application 'S, Cowes. hie of Wight</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>FRED FE</head>
        <p>FRED FE</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>XX Ot -LD-RE PEACEMAKER.</p>
        <p>Meets With The Proverbial Reception. At the. Borough Police Court on Monday, before the Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock), Aldermen Bolton, Evans and Smethurst, Messrs. W. Furness and T. Locker, Harold Alfred Jackson, a labourer, residing at Gleeson's ilodging-house, Town Hill, Warring- ton, was charged with assaulting Charles Wil- son, a general labourer, aged 51 years.' Wilson stated that on- Saturday evening he went into, Gleeson's lodging-house.. • The pris- oner was quarrelling with some of the 'lodgers, and he suggested to them that they Should live peacefully together, With the result that 'the prisoner struck him on the back of the head. Witness was then taken to the Infirmary. P.C. Wise said that when he charged the prisoner with the assault he admitted it. A fine of 198. 6d. Was inflicted, the alternative being 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
        <p>met my husband on the bridge and said that I had to give him a drink of beer. He had the jug in his hand when the officer came in, anti was thinking. I may as well tell the truth." The Chief Constable added that there was no evidence as to money passing between Towey and Mrs. Holloway. There was only evidence of consumption. Detective Skelton gave evidence bearing out this statement. In cross-examination, he admitted that Towey told him that he had not paid for the drink. Evidence was also given by in,pecor Hodg- kiss.</p>
        <p>Consumption Admitted, But Sale Denied.</p>
        <p>For the defence, Mr: Steel admitted that if the prosecution could prove that there had been a consumption of liquor on the premises, there was an onus on the defendant to show that there had been no sale. The evidence he was going to dell would show that there had been no sale: The defendant and Towey had been friends for a number of years. On tilt Saturday previous to the alleged offence, Toweg lost a £1 note, and when he met the defendant on Monday, told him of his loss. Mr. Holloway then said "If. you come inside I will give you a-drink." The defendant went into the kitchen and said to his wife "Give Towey a drink." The defendant was not present to give him the drink, or to exercise any control' as to where Towey should drink the beer. Mrs. Holloway drew the pint and her daughter handed it to Towey. The whole thing Was above board. Mr. Steel's contention was that there was no sale; the beer was a gift. Cross-examined by the Chief Constable, the defendant said that it was "no part of his busi- ness to give beer away in this fashion." Evidence was given by Mrs. Holloway, Miss Nellie Holloway and Patrick Towey. After retiring for a short while, the Bench I decided to impose a fine of 40s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>STOLEN SOAP.</head>
        <p>STOLEN SOAP.</p>
        <p>‘Vhat Watchman _Found In Worker's</p>
        <p>Basket.</p>
        <p>John Forster, 75, Haydoek-street, Warrington, was charged at the Borough Police Court on Monday with stealing soap from the warehouse of Messrs. Joseph Crosfield and Sons, soap- makers; Bank Quay, Warrington.—Prisoner was represented by Mr. Browne. Prosecuting, the Chief Constable said that the value of the soap was 3s. On Sunday afternoon two special constables were on duty at Cros- field's works. They received information that four tablets of soap were missing from the ex- perimental part of the works. Certain men were suspected and the watchman taxed three men With the theft. They all denied it. He then examined their baskets, and in the second basket he found the four tablets. Prisoner was asked what they were doing there. He stoutly denied having stolen the tablets or having had anything to do with putting them there. Later, however, he admitted the theft. A detectiire had since found a quantity of soap in the man's bedroom. A Plea for Clemency. - -</p>
        <p>- -- Mr. Browne said that he had been instructed by the prisoner's friends to say a few words on his behalf. He had worked for Messrs. Cros- field's for eight or nine years, and there had been nothing against him before. Neither the prisoner nor his friends could give any explana- tion as to why he should take the soap- He went to work at 10 o'clock on Saturday night and worked until 5.30 on Sunday night, when he was apprehended by the police. Mr. Browne appealed to the Bench to exercise their clemency in this case, adding that the prisoner was very sorry. Prisoner was filled 205., the Mayor- observing that there Was too much of this sort of thing going on. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>CRUELTY TO A HORSE.</head>
        <p>CRUELTY TO A HORSE.</p>
        <p>Fine For Driver Who Belaboured</p>
        <p>Animal On Head.</p>
        <p>Joseph Maguire pleaded "not guilty" at Tuesday's Borough Police Court, when sum- moned for being cruel to a horse on November 3rd. A youth named Lawrence Moncks said that on November 3rd, when near the Howley Quay, ho saw the defendant strike a horse a number of times on the head. Later; when the witness told the defendant that a piece of timber had fallen on the horse, the defendant replied "Let the horse die." The defendant denied hitting the horse. Inspector Dee stated that when he examined the horse he found that it was very tender on the head and on the ribs. A fine of 20s. was inflicted. The defendant was also ordered to pay 55.. witnesses' fees. The alternative was 14 days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>OFT-CONVICTED SOLDIER.</head>
        <p>OFT-CONVICTED SOLDIER.</p>
        <p>Fined For Imbibing "Not Wisely Bu</p>
        <p>Too *ell."</p>
        <p>- - - - the aslfouathdoLzeannesa.bsßenetgeiems—enmt—oswt Regiment—were ef thembefo ref r tohnel Borough Bench on Wednesday morning. The Mayor (Alderman Peter Peacock) was in tho chair. All the absentees admitted the offence alleged against them, and they wete ordered to be handed over to escorts. One soldier—John Ayres, of the sth South Lancs.—was charged on remand with having been drunk and disorderly. The Chief Constable told the magistrates that the man stood remanded until next Saturday. The man's commanding officer had,' however, , been communicated with and as a result the Bench were asked to deal with the man that k day, an escort having been sent to take him back to his regiment. , Ayres said he was very sorry for what had happened. ' The Chief Constable said that there was a list of twenty convictions against the man, and he had served,various4;er.ms,o£ imprisonment, from three -years downwards. Ile was fined 12s. 6d., which, the Mayor ,assured him, only just about covered the costs.</p>
        <p>"THESE 'BITS OF THINGS ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>Thought Ile Did_" No Harm In Taking</head>
        <p>Thought Ile Did_" No Harm In Taking</p>
        <p>Hair."</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Tuesday, George Mullhall, a labourer, of Pocket-street, Warrington, was remanded for seven days on a charge of stealing 111 b. of curled hair, valued at is. 6d., from a warehouse ,in Scotland-road. The hair is the property of Richard Harding, of Buttermarket-street. The Chief Constable asked for a remand in order that inquiries might be made. The prisoner said that he did not think he was doing any harm in taking these "bits cf things."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>MUG OF BEER. AN as It A Gift, Or Was It Sold?</head>
        <p>A MUG OF BEER. AN as It A Gift, Or Was It Sold?</p>
        <p>OFF-LICENSEE FINED.</p>
        <p>- - - - Samuel Holloway, the holder of the off- licence attached to 40, Kerfoot-street, Warring- ton, was summoned at the Borough Police Court on Tuesday for selling beer- to Patrick Towey on November Bth, and being privy ta Towey drinking it on the premises. The defendant pleaded not guilty, and was represented by Mr. T. S. Steel. The Case For The Police.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable said that at 11.55 a.m. on Monday, November Bth, Detective Skelton visited the defendant's premises. He observed .that at the back of the counter was Mrs. Holloway, the wife of the defendant. In the front part of the shop were two men, of whom Towey was one—and another, who left the shop after the detective's entry. As the -officer entered the door he saw Towey with a pint mug half-full of beer. Towey was in the act of removing the mug from his mouth. The officer said to Towey "You have no right to be drink- ing beer in here." In reply Towey said that he was sorry, and that he should have gone round the corner to drink it. Later in the day Inspector Hodgkiss visited the premises with A view to seeing, the defendant, but the latter had not returned. Mrs. Holloway then said 'This man (Tcnvegl came in and said he bad</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>" FOGGY " NIGHT.</head>
        <p>A " FOGGY " NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Railway Traveller Assaults Collector And Constable. At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. J. Rymer Young (presiding), Dr. Naden and Mr. A. J. Jolley, Matthew Craven, of Irlam, was charged with being drunk and with assaulting P.C. Joynson and Ticket-collector Herrington at the Central Station, Warrington, on Wednesday night. The evidence showed that the prisoner arrive6d at the Central Station by the 11 o'clock train on Wednesday ',night. His ticket was from Manchester to Irlam. The ticket-collector asked him to pay the fare from Irlam to War- rington, but the prisoner refused. Ultimately the prisoner struck the ticket-collector on the mouth, loosening three teeth. P.C. Joynson was about to take prisoner into custody, when he knoCked the constable's helmet off and kicked it about the platform. The prisoner also kicked the constable a few times. He was in a drunken condition.</p>
        <p>" Could Not See His Station." The prisoner said that it was a very foggy night and he could not see his station. The Chairman: Is it not a breach of the com- pany's bylaws to allow a drunken man to gct into a train?</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable: It was a very foggy night, and perhaps they could not see him at Manchester. The prisoner was fined 10s.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>SIXTEEN MILES AN HOUR.</head>
        <p>SIXTEEN MILES AN HOUR.</p>
        <p>Market Gate Dangerous Post For A</p>
        <p>Constable.</p>
        <p>Percy Stubbs, of 110, Padgate-lane, was fined 20s. on Thursday for driving a motor car in a dangerous manner on November 9th. _ . _</p>
        <p>P.C. Hawkins statedl that at 9.10 p.m. on November 9th, he was on duty at Market Gate. when he saw the defendant driving a motor car along Sankey-street in the direction of Butter- market-street, at the rate of about 16 miles an hour. At the same time a Lance-Corporal of the Territorials was approaching the witness to ask him a question. If witness and the sordier had not jumped out of the way they would have been knocked down. The defendant was asked if he thought that it was right to drive a car through the town at the pace at which he was going, and he replied that the car had skidded and that he could not stop. The con- stable examined Sankey-street, but could not find the marks where the car was said to have skidded. Lance-Corporal Malpas gave similar evidence. The defendant said that as he was approach- ing the crossing he aPplied the brake, but as the back of the car turned round he had to release it. He admitted that it was a dangerous position for the constable, but he did the best he could in the circumstances. In addition -to the fine, the defendant was ordered to pay 7s. 6d. costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>DECEIVER OF WOMEN.</head>
        <p>DECEIVER OF WOMEN.</p>
        <p>Fitzgerald Gets Eight Years' Penal Servitude.</p>
        <p>At the Manchester Assizes on Wednesday, be- fore Mr. Justiee Low,</p>
        <p>Gerald Fitzgerald, whose real name was stated by the police in evidence to be Harry Wilkins, pleaded guilty to a series of frauds upon women by means of which he obtained sums of money to the amount of £3,84.5. To several of the women he promised marriage. A remarkable account was given of the prisoner's career, and it was shown that, amongst other sentences, he had served one of five years' penal servitude for bigamy. The Judge said the prisoner appeared to be of quite unreformable nature, and sentenced hisa to eight years' penal servitude.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>SOLDIERS-DROWNED IN THE</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS-DROWNED IN THE</p>
        <p>SHIP CANAL.</p>
        <p>Two drowning accidents due to the dense fog occurred on the Manchester Ship Canal on Wednesday. Private J. Madden, aged 45, at- tached to the military guard, went on duty at the Irlain locks early in the morning, and about two hours after was missed. Dragging opera.- tions were commenced and carried on through- out the day, but up to a late hour at night the body had not been recovered. Servant William Bothwell, who was also at- tached to the military guard, was on duty at the Barton locks when he fell into the water. He raised an alarm, but owing to the darkness and fog those who went to his assistance were unable to find him. The body was recovered on Wednesday afternoon.</p>
      </div>
