      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>EXAMINER,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER,</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>MR. CHURCHILL'S POSITION.</head>
        <p>MR. CHURCHILL'S POSITION.</p>
        <p>Mr. Winston Churchill's resignation is to be regretted on many grounds. It was un- fortunate that, in the present State of affairs, he should have thought it necessary not only to resign, but to offer " explanations " which by their very nature were bound to bring a measure of comfort to the enemy. The nation knew the invaluable—the vitally important—part Mr: Churchill had played in having the Navy ready when the supreme moment arrived to test its efficiency. He could have afforded to await its matured judgment— The Admiralty under his admin- istration provided us with a Navy that has always held, and will now continue to hold, the enemy by the throat, and will, when the war is over be proclaimed as the predominat- ing factor in the victory the Allies will have won. The Navy has isolated Germany and given the Allies the freedom of the seas. We have only to reverse these conditions in our imaginations to realise what might have happened had not Mr. Churchill and the col- leagues who worked with him for four years preceding the war failed in their duty. Naturally, a proud man like Mr. Churchill chafes under such an injustice that has ap- parently been done to him—at the instigation, it is to be feared, of critics who have had other than patriotic motives to urge them forward. The explanations he has deemed it wise to make show that some of the points upon which he has been viciously and un- scrupulously attacked have been largely the product. of ignorance, Or, as we have sug- gested, something even worse. Mr. Churchill ConSidered that he should resign his seat in the Cabinet because it has not been found possible to give him, as the Prime Minister had hoped to do, a seat on the new "War Council." To a man of Mr. Churchill's intense activity of mind it was not compatible to hold an office which has no laborious departmental duties attached to' it unless he had a larger share in the• direc- tion of the war than a place in the Cabinet iuvolves. And, as he explained in his letter to Mr. Asquith, he feels that to be in the Cabinet without being on the " War Council': would mean that he had a share in the respon- sibility out of proportion to hiS share in the shaping of the, Government's policy. At the close of Mr. Churchill's defence of his record at the Admiralty, Mr. Asquith paid a high tribute to his late colleague. Speaking with a knowledge derived from ten years of close association with Churchill, Mr. Asquith described him as "a wise counsellor, a bril- liant colleague, and a faithful friend." There were four counts upon which Mr. Churchill replied to his .critics :—The Sava' battle off the South American coast, in which Admiral Craddock's squadron was destroyed by. an overwhelmingly superior force; the , loss, through submarine attack, of the three Cressy cruisers; the British expedition to Antwerp, and Mr. Churchill's personal in- tervention there; and the first naval attack on the Dardanelles. In regard to the first two counts, Mr. Churchill did not go into details, but he strongly denied that he had overridden expert professional opinion, and expressed the hope that all the papers on the subject might be published.</p>
        <p>As regards the Antwerp Expeditiotr, Mr. Churchill made the interesting disclosure that the project of sending a relieving army origin- ated with Lord Kitchener and the French Government. The armchair criticS who com- plain of the 'dispatch of the Naval Brigade</p>
        <p>I-</p>
        <p>fail to reflect that it was a question, not of what kind of force would be best, but abso- lutely of what was available at the time. Mr. Churchill claims that the prolongation of the resistance of Antwerp, even for a couple of days, held np a large body of German. troops, and ensured that the ensuing battle should be fought on the line of the Yser instead of twenty or thirty miles further west. The Dardanelles Expedition is the most complicated of the four questions with which Mr. Churchill dealt. Many points must in- evitably remain, for a considerable period, obscure. Lord Fisher, Mr. Churchill admits, was against a purely naval attempt to force the Dardanelles. But, Mr. Churchill urges, one of the principal reasons that Lord Fisher alleged was the danger of weakening our naval forces in home waters—a view which, Mr. Churchill suggests, experience has shown to be mistaken. The Admiral on the spot and expert -opinion at home coincided in the report that a naval attack upon the Dar- danelles forts had a fair chance of success.</p>
        <p>The country will applaud Lord Fisher's manliness—and patriotism-7in refusing to reply at the present time to that portion of Air. Churchill's speech that applied to him. "It is unfitting," Lord Fisher said in the House of Lords on Tuesday, "to make per- sonal explanations affecting national inter- ests when my country is in the midst of a great war." Such scruples did not prevent Sir Edward Carson from imitating Mr. Churchill to explain why he resigned, although very few people wanted to know why he did so. Sir Edward endeavoured to justify his resignation by charges against ,the Govern- ment of delaying to redeem its pledge to assist Serbia in her extreme hour of trial. An ex-First Lord of the Admiralty knows what 4 he is talking about' when' he ,discusses the available forces at the disposal erihe Govern- ment. An ex-Attorney-General is not the best judge of how and- when military or naval assistance can be rendered to an Ally. Sir Edward Carson doubtless thought that he war; discharging a duty to himself; but his speech was liable to grave misuse in enemy. coun- tries. We can imagine the use the pro-Ger- mans in Greece and Roumania. will have made of it long before now. "Serbia betrayed !" they will say; " what may we expect if we ,trust to England's word ?"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>COUNCILLOR OSBDRNE'S RESIGNATION,</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR OSBDRNE'S RESIGNATION,</p>
        <p>STATEMENT BY THE BANK MANAGEMENT.</p>
        <p>Referring to the speeches of the Mayor and others affecting the Warrington, Runcorn and District Savings Bank made at a meeting of the 'Warrington Town 'Council on the 9th inst., the Committee of Management of the Bank desire to state that a few months since they had under consideration the question of the salaries of their staff, whenconsiderablo in- creases were made. Mr. Osborne then agreed to give up all outside, work and devote the 'whole of his time and energies to the interests of the Bank and resignation of his seat upon th,e Town Council followed as a matter of course. This arrangement was a perfectly friendly and amicable one on both sides,. and,</p>
        <p>SO far as the committee are aware, Mr. Osborne has never expressed any dissatisfaction there- with, but, on the contrary, he affirmed his sat- isfaction with it and his thanks to the com- mittee.</p>
        <p>The Mayor stated he was in possession of the actual facts, but the conimittee can scarcely think from 'the tenor of his speech that he could have beeu fully aware•of the arrangement referred to.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>"ATHENS OF ENGLAND."</head>
        <p>"ATHENS OF ENGLAND."</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett Lectures On Old Warrington.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Warrington Class Teachers' Association, Alderman Arthur Bennett read a most interesting paper, which he entitled "The Athens of England."</p>
        <p>They might, he said, ask wonderingly, "What place can boast so great a name?" His answer was "It lies right at your door." It is War- rington! This "very old, very ignorant, very sooty, hard-hearted, stony-streeted, meanly-grim little provincial town" which one of the country's finest poets, Richard le Gallienne, christened " Coalchester "; the town familiar to everybody in his audience as a somewhat dull and unromantic hive of much industrial enterprise was once, however curious it might sound, in name at any rate "the Athens of England." ",Neither Oxford, nor Cambridge '- wrote Miss Lucy Aitken, the niece of Mrs. Bar- bauld, "could boast" in that forgotten time "of brighter names in literature or science, • and "not a dozen towns in merry England," said a recent writer, "can claim a history more absorbing or a lineage more ancient." That history began in.. the .first_ century, AgriCola tarrying here in the Year 79 A.D. The Antiquity of the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The 'Emperor Hadrian was said to have paid the place a visit. The Saxons built a church here' and dalicated it. alone in England, to some mystical "St. Elfin "—the church which had, for close upon a thousand years, stood like some holy ark of refuge "at the tayle of al the towne," and the Doomsday Book told how King Edward the Confessor, the builder of the adjacent " city of Thelwall," held " Walintune" awl drew from it a rent of £l5 a year. The Earl of 'Lancaster had stationed there a Norman Baron, Paganus de Villars, and he had built upon the oval mound beyond the church, a rude old stronghold meant to guard the pas- sage to the Ford. And, from a daughter of this house, one Beatrice de Villars, there had sprung, by marriage with Richard Pincerna, the noble Botiller family, whose ancient manor house of Bewsey still nestled, moat surrounded, in a clump of greenery right in the reek of the great chimneys, its sylvan woods and blood- stained floors and ghostly legend undisturbed. The Famous Botiller Family.</p>
        <p>A famous family those Botillers of Bewsey were, whose sons fought valiantly at Evesham, helped to conquer Ireland, marched with a great host to the conquest of Wales, rode northwards in the vain attempt to subjugate the Scots, took part in the fierce wars in France, shared in the glories of the naval victory of Nararete were taken captive in a grim crusade against the Saracens of Barbary, died on the way to Agin- court, or fought at Blore and Tewkesbury, at Bosworth Field, in the •fierce fight with Lambert Simnel and his rebel host, and, once again on bloody Flodden Field; and who, between those deeds of knightly,valour,.built bridges over the river, bought the town kingly charters, founded fairs and -markets, and erected an Augustine Friary, in whose forgotten halls dwelt Thomas Penker, the monk who issued the chief works of old Duns Scotus, was made Provincial of the Hermit Friars of the Augustine Order, and found a place for ever on the lips of the im- mortal bard. So loyal was the old town that the Earl of Derby made it his headquarters in the Cival War and Charles himself, was fain to hoist his standard there. It was the oentre of fierce civil strife for, years and, .;by the bridge, Cromwell ended one of his most fateful battles, tarrying afterwards three days and nights in an old house in Church-street. For centuries the records of the town read like a romance, and were full of valiant deeds. Alderman Bennett went on to deal at length with the history of the Old Academy, thanks to which the seeds of all the centuries burst into splenclid flower, and Warriniton 'obtained 'the title' of'" The' Athens of England "—a title which, he thought, was not wholly undeserved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</p>
        <p>"PIPER OF LOOS."</p>
        <p>Winwick War Hospital Patient Awarded V.C.</p>
        <p>PIPED THE MEN TO THE CHARGE.</p>
        <p>The proudest man to-day in the great Military Hospital at Winwick is Daniel Laidlaw—Piper Daniel Laidlaw, of the 7th King's Own Scottish Borderers, to give him his full style and title. And the reason for his pride is such as to justify the feeling which is proverbially, but sometimes erroneously, sup- posed to precede a fall. For Piper Laidlaw has learned that the most coveted distinction that a soldier can win—the Victoria Cross—has been 'conferred on -him, and that henceforth his name will have a place on that treasured roll of those whom their King and his people have hon- oured for their valour.</p>
        <p>• Piper Laidlaw, as his name would suggest, is of -Scotch descent, and was born at Swinton, in Berwickshire:- He is a keen-eyed, studious - looking man of 42 years .of age, and he speaks with just a suspicion of that "burr" which invariably betrays the man who has kinship with the people of the ancient Northern king-</p>
        <p>dom. Of but medium height and spare of frame, he presents a smart, soldierly appear- ance, save that for the present he walks with just a suspicion of a limp, due to the fact that lie has not yet completely recovered from the wounds which he received on the occasion when he performed the gallant deed which won for him the coveted Cross.</p>
        <p>L .;i'',..,*"' -..! - -'•-*.,:i r• 1 ' '..: • , , .fr ' 4 vir L•• , - i ;4• '1 . - ..k., "?.,, ...,'.. s. ' f , . +4,t-,, --, - •i6,.-i, ~:, ......, '- .A. '• '14,4-41(--, , ~:- - tt ,-, ~, :*- ~.;,:t: *f , ise '' ; '."`• ' 4 '—..'"'' • 9'‘.. . . , ~...* ‘,,,,;•40.-6...,,e7•-• - , ~,,,,,,,,,, '' , !..'• - ~' ''''' ' il, it .. c,‘-` •- `'.;; ' -,;,, ~,,,,s1;1•-•,'1,1 ~ -, ~,,, ,: ~ ..,:•,.,,,,,t,t ~,,:„1,-,,..„-'4„, ~,,-.1:6',.,,i-, 4 ,•s; 2.,. ' e,' ~,,,,,,..-• ‘, , , .:,----., ,- , ,4, .., •-• ;o 4, ---- ~....1.4,-,„ 'Ir40;:::„.. „,.; :44 , ;4. ,I,sit-..-.47 ' -, : '..;:;z• -4-, '',P;,,: i ..,,, •,-, `, 1,5 ;,: 4 4 r , '',, '3,, '''- ' *,t4'9'',?•;!` • ' ' ';'' 'V.* -*, ••.,': IP : A '''' ' ' ''';': '' '"A ' .:,,,,A &gt;,' • . • ',4 ' 1 w. -: , 4#4,4:;'• ,70r -.-• ~,,,</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell. THE PIPER AND HIS PIPES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>TIME-EXPIRED MAN AND REJOINED.</head>
        <p>TIME-EXPIRED MAN AND REJOINED.</p>
        <p>Piper Laidlaw is a married man and the father of four children, and his wife at pre- sent resides at South Doddington, Wooler, Ncrthumbefland. Ile is an old soldier, having had eighteen years in the King's Own Scottish Borderers. Indeed, he was a time-expired man, but when the war broke out the call to arms</p>
        <p>appealed to him with special force and he offered his services to his old regiment once more; and they were cheerfully accepted., for the• Piper's record was a good one, and his old officers knew that he was just the sort of man, despite his age, that the country could depend on to do his duty, and do it cheerfully and thoroughly. It was in the 2nd Battalion that Piper Laidlaw originally served; it was the 7th Battalion:that he was drafted to on rejoining the colours. After he left the Army the Piper went to work for .the Alnwick Co-operative Society, and he was in their employ when the Declaration of War came, and he decided that his place was beside his old comrades in the field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>OFFICIAL ACCOUNT OF THE PIPER'S HEROISM.</head>
        <p>OFFICIAL ACCOUNT OF THE PIPER'S HEROISM.</p>
        <p>It was on September 25th that Piper Laidlaw won his Victoria Cross. How he did so is thus officially recorded :</p>
        <p>For most conspicuous bravery prior to an assault on German trenches near Loos and Hill 70 on September 25, 1915. During the worst of the bombardment, when the attack was about to commence, Piper Laidlaw, see- ing that his company was somewhat shaken from the effects of gas, with absolute cool- ness and disregard of danger mounted the parapet, marched up and down, and played his company out of the trench. The effect of his splendid example was immediate, and the company dashed out to the assault. Piper Laidlaw continued playing his pipes till he was wounded. THE PIPER'S OWN STORY.</p>
        <p>Interviewed by an "Examiner" representa- tive, the gallant Piper, after much pressing, amplified the meagre official record. "We were," he said, "in the front line of trenches before Loos. When I say we.' I refer to the 46th Brigade. The 7th King's Own Scottish Borderers were in the centre of the Brigade and we had the Scottish Rifles on our right and the Highland Light Infantry on our left. Our Artillery had been bombarding the Germans' for a. long time; in fact, they gave them a thorough bombarding—a task that took more than a day, I can tell you. On the Satur- day- morning—a morning lam not likely to forget—we got orders that we were to take the ,German trenches. Lieutenant 'Young, who has since been killed, was in command of my par- ticular section. At about 6.30 we got the order to advance. I got ever the parapet along with Lieutenant Young.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>"I HAD THE PIPES GOING."</head>
        <p>"I HAD THE PIPES GOING."</p>
        <p>"I had the pipes going and the lads gave a (leer as they started towards the enemy's line. As soon as they showed over the trench-top they began to fall very fast, but they never wavered, but dashed straight on as I played the old air they all knew—` Blue Bonnets over the Border!' My, but there's some 'fire' in that Old tune. I ran forward with them, piping for all I knew, and just as we were getting near the German lines shrapnel caught me in the left ankle and leg. I was too excited to feel the pain j.ust then, but stumbled on as best I could, and I changed the tune to 'The Stan- dard on the Braes co Mar.' It's a grand tune for charging on. I kept on piping and piping. hobbling along after the laildies until I could go no more, and then seeing that the boys had won the position I began to get back as best I could to our own trenches. I got there some- how, and that is as much as I can remember. There does not seem much to tell and I never thought tbat I should have won the grand decorationl"</p>
        <p>When the news that Piper Laidlaw had been awarded the V.C. reached the Winwick Hos- pital he was "lionised," and nothing would satisfy the wounded men in the many wards until he had paid them a visit and "given them a taste o' the skirl o' the pipes."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>THREE BROTHERS IN THE ARMY.</head>
        <p>THREE BROTHERS IN THE ARMY.</p>
        <p>Piper Laidlaw's eldest brother is serving at the Front as Pipe-Major in the King's Own Scottish Borderers; another brother is with the Royal Horse Artillery, and a third with the Royal Engineers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>MORE AMBULANCE MEN.</head>
        <p>MORE AMBULANCE MEN.</p>
        <p>Three more members of the Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade left War- .rington for hospitpl service this week. They aib: Privates IL Plinston and A. Royle, Messrs. Crosfield and 'Soils; and A. NiT. Howliston, Mr. RAlph Johnson. • • •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>TEACHER-OFFICER.</head>
        <p>TEACHER-OFFICER.</p>
        <p>Mr. S. J. Price Brings Honour</p>
        <p>To His Profession.</p>
        <p>REWARD OF KEENNESS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Samuel James Price, the well-known assistant master of St. Mary's School, Butter- market-street, became Lieutenant-Quarter- Master Price last week, and his new rank was duly gazetted on the Friday. Lieut. Price's promotion has been gradual, the successive steps he has taken on the ladder proving that his advancement has been the reward of steady application to his work and keenness in his new profession. Although 36 years of age, when the war began he applied for admission to Kitcliener's Army, but was rejected as "too old." The age limit has since been extended. Turning to the Territorials he was enrolled in our local battalion, the 2nd/4th South Lanea- shires, in October, 1914, and he is now stationed with: them in the South of England.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>FINE RECORD.</head>
        <p>A FINE RECORD.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Price is a married man, and Mrs. Price and her two children still reside at 18, Alexandra-street, Manchester-road. His father, the late Joseph Price, was in the Army for forty-eight years, fought in the Indian Mutiny, and was at the relief of Lucknow. It was natural that Mr. Price should 'take an intelli- gent interest in things military. We find him acting as Preparatory Training Corps instruc- tor up to the time of enlistment. In peace times and as a teacher he was a captain of Cadets and</p>
        <p>the leading spirit in the Catholic Boys' Brigade, in whose efficiency he took great and pardon- able pride. He was most energetic in the matter of athletics for children, and was an active secretary for the school children's sports. At different times he was secretary and president of the Schoolboys' Football Association, both Rugby and Association. He was also President of the Catholic Young 'Men's Society.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>PATRIOTIC FAMILY.</head>
        <p>A PATRIOTIC FAMILY.</p>
        <p>As we have indicated, Lieutenant Price be- longs to a soldier-family; he has three brothers on active service. William, who is married and a schoolmaster in London, is with the Artillery in, Prance; George, who is 'married, is "with the Navy" at sea;, and Albert, who is also married and a joiner at Messrs. Rylancis Bros.' Works, is in the R.A.M.C. Their widowed mother lives at 30, Alexandra-street, with a daughter and a son who is, on indispensable work in the Education Office.</p>
        <p>This is a record upon which the Price family may well be proud, and so far as the promotion to which we call attention is concerned, the whole teaching profession in Warrington feel the refleoted distinction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>SECONDARY SCHOOL "OLD BOYS."</head>
        <p>SECONDARY SCHOOL "OLD BOYS."</p>
        <p>Granted Naval And Arm) Commissions.</p>
        <p>Two former pupils of the Warrington Second- ary School have recently been granted commis- sions.</p>
        <p>Samuel B. Williamson, who formerly resided at Vost's• Cottage, Lower Stretton, has been granted a sub-lieutenancy. in the Navy. He was a pupil at the Secondary School for a num- ber of years, and after leaving -was appointed to a commercial position in Manchester. Later he qualified for the Civil Service, and worked at the Admiralty. In addition to holding a commission in the Navy he will act as an in- terpreter. He is proficient in German, French and Dutch, as well, of course, as in his native tongue. He has resided in Holland.</p>
        <p>The other "old boy" who has been granted a commission is Ernest Parsons, of 32, Willis- street, Warrington: He was gazetted a second- lieutenant last .week. He attended the Second- ary School, and there. qualified for entry into a teachers' training college.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>DREADFUL END.</head>
        <p>A DREADFUL END.</p>
        <p>Aged Invalid Burned To Death.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Mary Hannah Greenhaigh, aged 69 'years, widow, of 10, Rodney-street, Warrington, died at about 10.45 on Friday morning as the result of burns. Mrs. Greenhalgh had a stroke seven years ago, and has been a helpless invalid ever since. It was her custom to sit by the fireside in an armchair.: On Friday Morning her daugh- ter Mary went' out of the • house to do some shopping, leaving her mother alone in the house. Opening the street door,, upon her re- turn, she found the house full of smoke:, She rushed into the louse, only to find that her mother was dead. The charred clothes fell from the body as it was touched. An inquest will be held this morning. "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>Musical Society's Forthcoming War-</head>
        <p>Musical Society's Forthcoming War-</p>
        <p>Fund- Performance.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Musi.-210 Society are due to give a performance of the concert version of Morrie England 'p —that particularly bright and attractive opera by Edward German and Basil Hood—in the Parr Hall on Saturday next. A distinguisht-!,41 list of. principals has been drawn up, the most notable among them being: Madame Sara Silcock, Miss Helen Anderton, Mr. J. Rabbitt and M. J. W. Bibby. The conducting will be in the able hands of Mr. F. H. Crossley, and the leader of the orchestra will be Mr. IL H. Pearson. The accompanist will be Mr. W. 11. Payton. It is to be hoped that the performanlv will prove, as highly successful from the.fiaaneial standpoint as it bids fair to be from the musi.:;al asyieet, as it is being organised in aid ';:f the local war funds.</p>
        <p>Wood's College Whist Drive and Dance</p>
        <p>The social re-union of past and present stu- dents of Wood's College took • place on Thurs- day • evening at the Cairo-street Schools, and proved, especially in point of numbers, a huge success. A capital programme of games, coni- petitions, and also a short concert, had been provided for the entertainment of the juniors; while a well-patronised whist drive was in full swing during the earlier portion of the evening. A very hearty reception was accorded .to Miss Sylvia Broadbent, vice-president of the local branch of the British Red Cross Society, who gave a most inspiring address to the large audi- ence. The majority of the members of the party eagerly -participated in a little dancing towards the end of the evening. Gifts for sale were received from many of the students,- the teach- ing staff, and others. The whole of the pro- ceeds are to be devoted to the Red Cross Society. Mr. F. Wilson Wood, principal, has had much pleasure on behalf of students, staff and friends, in handing a cheque. for ,tlO 10,s. to the Society —the amount representing the total takings for the evening. . _• -, • '</p>
        <p>Salvation Army. A special evening service, to be addressed 1-y Adjutant and Mrs. Beaumont, will be held in the Picturedrome, Sankey-street, to-morrow.</p>
        <p>Wedding Present For. Board Of Trade</p>
        <p>Official.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Curwen, of the local Board of Trade Office, was 6n Wednesday presented by his colleagues with a canteen of cutlery to mark the occasion of his marriage.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>Foreign Missions.</head>
        <p>Foreign Missions.</p>
        <p>The foreign mission anniversary will be kept at the Wycliffe Congregational Church to-night and to-morrow, to-night's gathering consisting of a meeting of welcome to the deputation---tho Rev. W. S. Hatch, who has worked in South India, and the Rev. A. J. Hutchinson, of China.</p>
        <p>Mayor's Sunday Collection. A sum of just over £1.4 was realised on behatf of the War Funds by the collection taken -A the Parish Church, on the occasion of the visit of the Mayor and Corporation, last Sunday morning.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Mr. Jarman Thanked.</head>
        <p>Mr. Jarman Thanked.</p>
        <p>The School Management Sub-committee of the Education Committee have placed on record "their unanimous appreciation of Mr. James Jarman's services during the last three years and of their exceeding regret at the severance of his connection with this committee conse- quent upon the expiration of his term of office."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>Shorthand Writers' Association.</head>
        <p>Shorthand Writers' Association.</p>
        <p>The members and friends of the Warrington Shorthand ,Writers' Association will meet on Friday evening next in the Museum Lecture Room at 7.30 p.m., when a lecture is to be given by Mr. C. Madeley. • Non-members are invited to aftend, The qualification for mem- bership is a speed of 60 words per minute, and the subscription is ss. per year. , Last Week's Weather.</p>
        <p>The mean temperature during the week ended November 13th was 42 degrees, or four degrees higher than in the preceding week. The warmest day was the Bth instant, when 55 degrees were recorded, and the coldest day the 13th instant, when the glass dropped to 26 degrees. There was a good amount of rain, 1.98 inches being recorded. '</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>College to the Rescue.</head>
        <p>College to the Rescue.</p>
        <p>, The war has seriously depleted and will further deplete the teaching staff of the town. In these circumstances the following millute of the School Management Sub-committee of the Education Committee is eloquently self-explana- tory—" The committee received with expressions of grateful thanks an offer from the Chairman of the committee (the 'Rev. Canon Stevenson) to place the services of the students at the Training College as far as possible at the dis- posal of the committee in the event of their being otherwise unable to. fill the vacancies caused by enlistments."</p>
        <p>Law Clerk to the Lord Derby NVar Hospital. The Visiting Committee of the County Asylum at Winwick, which is no being used as the Lord Derby War Hospital, have ap- pointed Mr. W. B. Forshaw, solicitor, of War- rington, to the position of Law Clerk, which was rendered vacant by the death of Mr. Stanley Francomb. Mr. Forshaw, who is a son of the late Alderman Forshaw, and who Con- tinues the practice formerly carried on in partnership with his father, under the same style of Forshaw and Forshaw, was an Honours man at both the Intermediate Examination and the Final Examination of the Law Society.</p>
        <p>There were numerous candidates from various parts of Lancashire for the position.</p>
        <p>Soldiers' Comforts.</p>
        <p>The patrons of the Howley Hotel, Warring- ton, have given a good lead to the townspeople in making voluntary contributions for the pur- pose of forwarding parcels of food-stuffs, &amp;c., to old customers of this hotel, who have joined the colours.</p>
        <p>A Roll of Honour was formed, whieh con tamed 36 names, 34 of whom are now serving with H.M. Forces. Of the remaining two, one is a prisoner of war in (lemony, and the other has been killed in action.</p>
        <p>Voluntary collections have been taken at this hotel on each Sunday night since the beginning of the war, with the result that .246 has been collected up to date, out of which numerous and various parcels and monetary gifts La 74,1 been made.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>Friendly Societies In Mayor's Procession.</head>
        <p>Friendly Societies In Mayor's Procession.</p>
        <p>The Friendly Societies who took part in the Mayor's procession last Sunday—which is said to. hold the record for Warrington, being so long that it, took twenty minutes to pass a given point—were marshalled by Mr. T. P. Brad- shaw, a P.M. of the Manchester Unity of Odd- fellows. His Worship has, through the Town Clerk, directed the following letter to be addressed to Mr. Bradshaw :</p>
        <p>"Dear Sir,—l am directed by the Mayor to convey to you his sincere thanks for the success which attended your efforts in the marshalling of the members of the Friendly Societies on the occasion of the church paraeo on Sunday last. His Worship desires me to add that the pro- ceedings passed off with complete satisfaction, due in no small degree to the care and attention which you gave to the details."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>Sunday School Revival:</head>
        <p>Sunday School Revival:</p>
        <p>The local Sunday and Day School teachers are highly favoured in the town being chosen by the London Society as one out of sixty provincial towns where special lecture corifor- ences are to be held. The President of the Sunday School Union for 1914 ,(Mr. A. C. Mitchell) cordially invites all Sunday school workers to tea in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall, Warrington, at 5.30 p.m. next Friday. Mr. J. .Upson will preside over a tea. table conference. At the evening meeting the chair will be taken by the Mayor. Miss Emily Huntley, extension lecturer Sunday School Union, will lecture on "Lessons and Lesson-Making." Miss Hunt- ley is a most intelligent and able adviser to teachers. Her thought is clear, her knowledge accurate, her diction forceful, and she possesses that .mysterious something that we call "per- sonal magnetism." She has never once failed to hold and convince her audience.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>%Vhist Drive and Dance.</head>
        <p>%Vhist Drive and Dance.</p>
        <p>A very enjoyable and successful whist drive and dance was held on Thursday at Messrs. N. Greening and Sons' dining hall, Bewsey-road, in aid of the wounded soldiers at Winwick. There was an attendance of about 200, the whist drive and dance being organised _ by</p>
        <p>Miss W. N. Fair, -of - Hood-lane, and , great credit is due for her perseverance and initiative. Most of the prizes awarded were nresented from different sources, also the refreshments. The hall was gaily de- corated with coloured festoons, which presented an animated appearance. A goodly sum was realised towards this noble and worthy cause. The proceedings were kept up until 2 a.m. The following were the successful competi- tors :—Ladies: Mrs. Briggs, Mrs. Oxley, Mrs. Talbot and Miss Archer; gentlemen: Messrs. A. Gleave, Johnson, Molyneux and Simpson. Booby prize-winners: Miss Povey and Mr. J. Gleave. Interval winners: Miss A. Williams and nr. Tickle. Hidden number prize-winner : Mrs. McMullen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>Warrington Photographic Society.</head>
        <p>Warrington Photographic Society.</p>
        <p>This week's meeting was distinguished by a lecture on "Wanderings in Southern Italy," by Mr. T. H. Greenall, of Chorley, the President of the Lancashire and Cheshire • Photographic Union. As was the case last year, Mr. Green- all's lecture was of the very highest order, of absorbing interest to both photographers and to those who do not carry a camera, and full of humorous sallies. The lantern pictures were very beautiful, both figure studies and land- scapes, and the 30 members present enjoyed a rare feast both for eyes and ears. The Lecturer commenced with the villages of the mountainous region north of. Naples called the. Abruzzi, making Sora his centre, and one would think that if Italy can beast such vil- lages everywhere it must be an even more remarkable country than we are wont to re- gard it. The character sketches, both by word and picture, were excellent, and were received with constant expressions of applause and merriment. Mr. Wileockson moved and Mr. Plinston seconded the vote of thanks to Mr. Gienftll.-- Mr: Davis was In ,the chair.</p>
        <p>Whist Drive Postponed. The whist drive and dance arranged by the St. John Ambulance Brigade to take place in the Drill Hall next Wednesday in aid of the prisoners of war flii2d, has been postponed.</p>
        <p>No Christmas Cards From Trinity Vicarage. _</p>
        <p>As Christmas is approaating, the Vicar and Mrs. Carleton for the sake of economy have de- cided not to send cards or Christmas greetings, except in one or two exceptional cases.—" Holy Trinity Parish Magazine."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Serbian Relief Fund.</head>
        <p>Serbian Relief Fund.</p>
        <p>The Mayor wishes us to state that he has received a cheque for five guineas from the Alliance Box Company for the above fund. While a local fund has not been opened for the Serbians, his Worship will be very glad to re- ceive any donations and will see that they are forwarded to the proper quarter.</p>
        <p>Warrington Market Prices.</p>
        <p>Wednesday.</p>
        <p>Wheat Was firm at late rates, 7s. 6d. to 7s. 7d. per 701 b. Oats remain firm, but inquiries are not so brisk, 4s. 6d. to 46. 7d. per bushel of 451 b. being given. Oat bale straw is still ad- vancing in price, £3 to .e 3 2s. 6d. per ton being offered. Wheat straw, 578. 6d. to 60s. per ton. Clover hay, £6 15s. to £7 ss. per ton. XX flour, 425. 6d. per 2801 b.; best bran, Is. Bd. per 201 b.; eggs, three and four for is. More Deaths Than Births.</p>
        <p>During the week ended November 13th 28 births were registered in Warrington and 30 deaths, this being the first week for many months in which' there were more deaths than births. In the corresponding week of last year there were 39 births and only 18 deaths. Ten of last week's deaths- were those of persons over 65 years of age. Last week's death-rate was 20.6 as compared with 12.4 in the same week of last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>Octogenarian's Death.</head>
        <p>Octogenarian's Death.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Anne Piaton, aged 81 years, of 10, Paul- street, died last week. Mrs. Pickton was the widow of. the late Mr. Thomas England Pick- ton, who was a well-known master wheelwright in Warrington. Mrs. Pickton was born at Sankey, and had resided in the Warrington dis- trict all her life. She was a regular worshipper at St. Paul's Church, and formerly took an active part in parochial affairs. She leaves five sons and two daughters. The funeral took place on Monday.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Christmas Cheer for Very Poor.</head>
        <p>Christmas Cheer for Very Poor.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's meeting of the" Warrington Board a Guaraians the Rev. A. M. Mitchell proposed that an allowance be made, during Christmas week, of is. 6d. to each adult person on the out-relief list and 6d. to each child.— "The only argument that can be brought against this proposal," said the reverend gentleman, "is the argument of economy."— There were no "economists," and the proposi- tion found unanimous favour, Mr. Flanagan. being the seconder. Gift • for Mission Secretary.</p>
        <p>An interesting little ceremony took -place on Sunday evening at the Railway Mission, Bank Quay, when a leather handbag, subscribed for by the members of the congregation, was pre- sented to Miss Bogstrom, who for eight years has faithfully performed the duties of secretary to the Mission. In making the presentation, Mr. H. Rigby, the organist, spoke of the great help Miss, Bogstrom had been to the Mission. Mr. Chamberlain also spoke, endorsing what Mr. Rigby had said.</p>
        <p>• New Council Schools Managers. The School Management Sub-committee of the Education Committee has resolved that the following gentlemen be appointed managers for the purpose of the joint management of the Silver-street and Wycliffe Council Schools, Mr. Matthew Cunningham, Rev. W. T. Derry, Dr. D. Ferguson, Mr. Benjamin Few, Mr. E. S. Garland, Mr. Herbert W. Guest, Mr. J. W. Hayward, Rev. T. T. James, Mr. Alfred J. Jolley. Mr J. Longland, Mr. F. Pendlebury, Mr. I'. T. Winskill, and that the Director of Educa- tion be appointed correspondent for the managers.</p>
        <p>Reehabites and Education Committee Representation. We have received the following copy of a letter that has been addressed to the Town Clerk (Mr. J. Lyon Whittle) by Mr. S. Fernley, district secretary of the Independent Order of Rechabites:—</p>
        <p>"Dear Sir,—The Presi announcement of the election of Mr. Clegg to represent the Friendly Societies on the Education Committee reminds niellat I, received your letter inviting repre- selitatives of the Independent Order of Recha- bites to attend the meeting and take part in this election. I regret to say that your letter unfortunately got overlooked; consequently we were not represented, and theiefore we were unable to take part in an appointment with which we are in agreement, and would have taken pleasure in supporting. In order that our absence from the meeting in question may not be misunderstood by those who were present and represented other Friendly Societies, per- haps you will kindly pass on the copies (which I enclose) of this letter to the Press."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>Caution In Leather Trade.</head>
        <p>Caution In Leather Trade.</p>
        <p>The improved tone in the leather market, noted in the latter part of last week, was again apparent, and inquiries in most directions were numerous and sustained, and whilst much of it has not yet got beyond the sampling stage, the inquiry seems quite genuine, and there is evi- dence of a decided revival of interest. This has been most noticeable'as regards the lower priced grades of bends, and for foreign stock, espe- cially in the lighter weights, inquiry has been almost animated. At the same time there are rumours of some considerable further Go—vern- ment orders about to be placed, so that pros- pects are distinctly brighter than they have been for several weeks past, and whilst it is probably too much to expect any marked revival of the demand before the end of the year, there are good grounds why holders of stock should handle, it very cautiously, and be not too generous with concessions for the purpose of effecting a present sale. Work in the local yards is quiet. All seem to be going cautiously, and according to reports putting in considerably less than the maximum, the main causes contributing towards this being the scarcity of tanning material and the antici- pated shortage of labour. t This reacts upon the raw hide markets, the further reductions at last auctions showing that there is a very small eewaltd at present.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>Source of Pride.</head>
        <p>A Source of Pride.</p>
        <p>The "Holy Trinity Parish Magazine" for November, dealing with the Right Reverend Lewis Bostock Radford, Bishop of Goulburn, New South Wales, says:— "We are sure that it will be a source of pride and satisfaction to many connected with Holy Trinity Church that one of the junior clergy of the parish, who was ordained deacon by the 'late Bishop of Liverpool in 1892, has had the unusual honour conferred upon him of being consecrated (bishop to the (important 'see of Goulburn, in New South Wales. It was well known in Warrington that Mr. Radford was a man of unusually high attainments, especially in classical knowledge, and it was no surprise when his college rewarded him with a fetlow ship in 1894. The college authorities later pre- sented him to the Rectory of Forneett S. Peter, in Lincolnshire, and within a year or two pro- moted him to the Rectory of Holt, in the diocese of NorwiCh. He went out to Australia as war- den of St. Paul's College (theological), Sydney; and There is little doubt that the excellent work he did in that institution has been the 2.n use of his promotion to succeed the late Bishop Barlow in the Episcopate. "Holy Trinity Church has now two former pastors in the Australian Church, of whom the parish may well feel proud, viz., Archdeleon itouch, of Bunbury Diocese, and now the Right Reverend Bishop Radford. "It so happened that when Dr. Radflrd lvent out a few years ago to take charge of St. Paul's College, Sydney, he went ashore for a shaft time at Albany, of which place Archdeacon Louch is rector. The two clerics met for the first time near the pier, Dr. Radford having directed his footsteps to the church. The two men were perfect strangers to each other, but the con- versation that ensued very soon revealed the fact that they had both been curates of Holy Trinity, Warrington, much to their surprise and gratification." •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>For Convalescent " Tommies."</head>
        <p>For Convalescent " Tommies."</p>
        <p>In aid of the The'wall Heys Military Con- valescent Home, a Pound Dap will be hell on the 25th inst. The special needs of the ins:itu- tion are notepaper, tobacco and vegetables.</p>
        <p>Freemasons' Cheque For The • Infirmary. The hon. treasurer, Mr. Roger C. Parr, gratefully acknowledges the receipt from the "Gilbert Greenall " Lodge of Freemasons, of cheque value £2l.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>Permanent Peace?</head>
        <p>Permanent Peace?</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. Graham, the principal of Dalton Hall, Manchester, will speak on "Permanent Peace in Europe" at the Friends' Meeting House, Buttermarket-street, at 6.30 on Sunday evening. Mr. Graham is a recognised authority and his address will be well worth listening to.</p>
        <p>Buckley-street Methodist Church. Anniversary services will be held to-morrow in the Methodist Free Church, Buckley-street, when the preacher will be Mr. R. Pinches, of Chester. At a special service' in the afternoon the President (Mr. T. Hewitt) will occupy the chair, and solos will be sung by the Misses May Hockenhull and E. Sthnway..</p>
        <p>Co-operative Society's Quarterly Meeting. The 220th quarterly meeting of the Warring- ton E.I. Co-operative Society, Ltd., will be held next Monday evening at the Co-operative Hall at eight. Speeial business will be transacted, and the election of three committeemen will take place.</p>
        <p>Carry Your Own Parcels ! The Warrington E.I. Co-operative Society, Ltd., have issued a notice to their customers declaring that they are not in a position to guarantee the delivery of bread or parcels, and requesting members of the Society to assist them, "and the ilovernment " by carrying away their own goods from the shops.</p>
        <p>"The Examiner" Travels Quickly. The Rev. W. Bracecamp, writing from the Front, says: "I thought you would be inter-, ested to know that "The Examiner" of last Saturday reached me on Sunday evening. I think this beats all records"; and the rev. gentleman thanks us for sending it "so promptly." Our thanks are due, in turn, to the postal authorities, whose organisation must be perfect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>Church House.</head>
        <p>The Church House.</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the members of the Warrington and District Church House, held on Wednesday, it was reported that the year ended with a debit balance pf DI 10s. sd. The committee and officers were re-elected, and Mr. H. Beresford Melville was thanked for under- taking the duties of secretary, in addition to those of treasurer, during the absence on active service of Captain Robson J. Chorley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>Secondary School Second Master.</head>
        <p>Secondary School Second Master.</p>
        <p>Mr. John B. Baxter is recommended to the Education Committee by the School Manage- ment Sub-committee for appointment as second master at the Secondary School and Technical Institute at a commencing salary at the rate cf £lBO per annum; his engagement to date from the Ist November, and to be determinable by three months' notice, to expire on the last day of any calendar month, given by or to the com- mittee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>Why The Gas Is Poor.</head>
        <p>Why The Gas Is Poor.</p>
        <p>There have been many complaints during the last week or two as to the poor quality of the gas. We have made inquiries and understand that the gas is at present being manufactured in accordance with the instructions of the Min- istry of Munitions, and that certain constitu- ents of the gas which are invaluable for the making of munitions of war, are being placed at the disposal of the Government. The Corpora- tion, at the same time, are absolved by special order from all penalties under their local Act. We feel sure that the gas consumer will gladly suffer the temporary inconvenience when he knews that by doing so he is materially assist- ing his country. Class Teachers' Association.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington Certi- ficated Class Teachers' Association :was held in the Wycliffe Memorial Hall last week-end.</p>
        <p>In the course of his eighth, annual report, Mr. George Newburn (the hon. secretary) stated that the committee had to record a successful year. The members numbered 169. Many teachers were serving in H.M. Forces, and many • ota re had expressed their ivillingness to enlist under Lord Derby's scheme. The balance sheet showed receipts totalling £l7 9s. 7d., and a balance in hand of ,t 5 16s. sd. Mr. Minard was elected president, Mr. White- house, vice-president; Mr. Cannell, ex-vice- president; Mr. T. Edden, treasurer; Mr. G. Newburn, secretary; and the Misses Brookes awl Kitching and Messrs. Glover, Armstrong, Knowles, Malcolm, Turner, Phillips, and Dendy, committee members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>Bible Society Anniversary.</head>
        <p>Bible Society Anniversary.</p>
        <p>The local auxiliary of the British and For- eign Bible Society, which exists for the single— though vital—object of supplying every man with the Scriptures in his mother tongue, holds its annual meeting next Thursday in the Co- operative Hall. In spite of the extra calls made by the war upon the resources of the public, the Bible Society has continued to flourish both in its finances and its work, the story of the year being notable for the way in which the Book has been sent through the ranks. Many nations, many languages, and the world-wide task of the Society of bringing the Gospel to every creature, has been applied with practised hands to the fields of battle. During the year over 10,000,000 copies of the Scriptures have been issued, a figure behind which lies a wonderful story which will:in part be unfolded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>Old Documents at the Old Fox.</head>
        <p>Old Documents at the Old Fox.</p>
        <p>Reference was made last week in Mr. A. Mounfield's article on the Old Fox to the fact that some old papers had been discovered dur- ing the process of demolition. These were found in the chimney of the upper room. The earliest is dated October, 1772, and is an account for gin at ss. 6d. per gallon. The tenant of the period is Mr. Richard Darwell and the creditor Mr. Robert Hesketh. Next in date is the account of an apothecary, and is for " plaisters, Draughts, chamomile flowers, Drops and pills." The date is 1787, the apothecary G. Orford, the purchaser Mr. Darrow. Of the same year is an- other account of Mr. R. Darwell to Thomas Skid for gin at 4s. 6d. per gallon, rum at Bs. per gallon, and brandy at 9s. per gallon. Of date November 7, 1810, is an account frem Messrs. Worthington and Gilbert to T. Whitley.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>Temperance Sunday at Golbortrestreet Chapel.</head>
        <p>Temperance Sunday at Golbortre- street Chapel.</p>
        <p>Temperance_ Sunday was observed at the Gol- borne-street Baptist Chapel, the preacher for the day being the Rev. H. Ogden, pastor. In the morning he preached upon the limits of liberty and stated that he believed that it was good for the morality and righteousness of the nation that Christians should abstain from intoxicat- ing liquor, and that the strong man should abstain for the benefit of, the weak. In the course of his evening sermon the reverend gentleman regretted that Warrington had not been scheduled under the new Restriction Order, and coltended that there was ample evidence that drmk was the cause of slack timekeeping in the munitions works. Mr. Joseph Poole was to have been the soloist at last Sunday evening's service, but. was prevented owing to indis- position. He has, however, promised to fulfil his engagement to-morrow evening.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>Funeral of the Late Mrs. Knox.</head>
        <p>Funeral of the Late Mrs. Knox.</p>
        <p>The funeral of the late Mrs. Knox, of 26, Wilderspool-causeway, whose death was an- nounced in our last issue, took.place on Satur- day afternoon at the Cemetery. Mrs. Knox was -the widow of the late Mr. Thomas Knox, of Mill-lane, who died ten years ago. The Rev. 11. B. Firth, vicar of St. James', Latch- ford, conducted the service in the chapel and at the graveside, and the mourners were :—Mr. Thos. Gibbs (son-in-laiv), Messrs. Wm. B. Gibbs. John W. Gibbs and Sydney Gibbs, and Masters Denis Gibbs and Langholm Gibbs (grandsons), Messrs. G. R. Parker, F. Croft (Bolton), B. Wood, J. Potter, J. W. Jeffery, S. W. Jeffery' and P. T. Winskill (nephews). Floral tributes were ,received from "Torn and Jennie,! "the grandchildren." "Knox, Martha and great- grandchildren "; Ethel Randall and great grand- children, nephews and nieces "; Mr. and Mrs. Geo. Whitfield, and cousins a Bolton. Messrs, J. Mounfield and Sons carried out the funeral arraDgenients.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>TERRITORIALS RESTING.</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS RESTING.</p>
        <p>Out Of The War Zone, But Training For Berlin Final.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>SERGT. WIDOCKS'S LETTER</head>
        <p>SERGT. WIDOCKS'S LETTER</p>
        <p>Happy As Children On Walking Day.</p>
        <p>We are glad to say that no fresh news of casualties among Warrington Territorials has arrived during the past week, a fact which is accounted for in Sergeant Widocks's cheery and humorous letter to Councillor Henshall, which we reprodu,e:--</p>
        <p>Somewhere in France. Tuesday.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Henshall,—lt is with very great pleasure that I again pen (or rather pencil) you a few lines to let you know the happenings of the old 4th. In the first place, I must thank you very much for your box of fruit, &amp;c., which I reeeived quite safely. I and the gunners had a good "burster" for tea the day the box came, and all enjoyed it, especially the tins of tongue. They talked quite a lot about it! : am very pleased to tell you, that we are hav- ing a rest from trench work. We have been out of the war zone now for about three weeks, and, needless to say, we enjoy it very nivoh. First of all we left</p>
        <p>, about nine or ten miles, which proved to be a delightful little village in Frnnee—a real old-world village, where we found absolute peace. The people were soon on good terms with us (who can resist the 4th?), and we soon repeated the words of the comedian, "Meredith, we're in!" Of course, we had to have exercise of some kind to keep us fit, so we had physical drill before breakfast, company and machine gun drill, before dinner, and a short route march in the afternoon, and at 3.30 we had finished for the day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>Lancashire Dialect Puzzles Natives.</head>
        <p>Lancashire Dialect Puzzles Natives.</p>
        <p>At night we visited different places around, also the estaminets, where we had sing-songs, but the Lancashire dialect somewhat puzzled the natives, who do not speak English like the Belgians did. We spent a very pleasant fort- night, and then we got the order to "move on," but not from a policeman! This time we moved about ten miles, but we did not walk it. Motor 'buses were provided for the whole battalion, and you can take my word that no school children going to the Walking Day field ever thought so much of a ride as we did. It was a sight worth seeing, - and as we tore through the country at seven, miles per hour we would not have changedlplaces with the Kaiser. This ride eventually 'Came to an end, and we found we were at another village, which is not so large or pretty as the previous one was. We soon took possession of our billets, which are barns on the farms. They are roomy and warm. The farm on which the machine gunners reside is a real old-fashioned French place. The only fault is that neither the farmer nor his children (and he has ten!) can speak hardly any English, and as we are not adepts in French—well, we hold some interesting conversations. One thing, though, we can tell him off if he appears to be annoyed at us. I wish they would make every one learn English! Noise Necessary To Sleep.</p>
        <p>It is a purely agricultural district around here, and the farm people are busy getting in their potatoes,', mangolds, swedes, &amp;c. Of course, I could call all these things by their French name, only you might think I was swanking. It is so nice to lie down at night and re4ise that -you won't be called early by a knock on the barn door from a "Jack John- son." In fact, before some of us could go to sleep the first night the others had to go out- side and throw bricks at the door! You see what use does. The weather here has been a bit better, and to-day we had a paper-chase instead of a route march. You see we must keep in form for the final sprint down the Unter den Linden. The King and the Lancashire Cheer.</p>
        <p>You will have seen from the papers that the King came to France and Belgium to visit and inspect the troops. He came and inspected our Division, which was represented by twenty men out of every battalion in the Division. You may guess how pleased r was when 1 found I was one of the twenty to represent the 4th. We first went to and billeted there for the night; and next morning we boarded motor 'buses and were taken to , where the inspection took place. The King looked rather aged, I thought; and he was accompanied by the Prince of Wales, who looked exceedingly well and smart in his staff uniform. He ap- peared to be pleased at what he saw, and on leaving us the whole line gave three hearty cheers, but you could hear the Lancashire cheers above all. The King's face lit up with pleasure at the sound, and I feel sure he felt very much the warmth of his reception. After his departure we once more mounted our 'bus and were driven back to our billets again. I then remembered that that was the third time I had been inspected by a King or Queen of England—once by the 'late Queen Vicloria, twice by the late King Edward, and once by our present King. No, sir, it is NOT time I was drawing the old-age pension! I think I have told you all for this time, so 1 must conclude. Please give my kindest regards to Mrs. Henshall and your son, who, I hope, are quite well. Trusting that you and all other friends are well, I will close this and remain sincerely yours,</p>
        <p>E. J. WIDOCKS</p>
        <p>Writers' Trials At The Front.</p>
        <p>P.S.—I do hope that you will be able to decipher this. Anyway, do your best. I have had to keep getting up from ray easy chair (consisting of a bundle of straw) and chasing hens and pigs out of the, barn, and while I was away a gust of wind blew the writing paper across the field, so these paper-chases come at an opportune time! When I reached the barn 'again a cow had entered, and with me being unable to think of the French for get out," I had to fetch the farmer, and he just said something that sounded like tram- drivers say when there is a lorry on the track and they are expecting to find their tea await- ing them at the terminus. These little trials when one is writing are apt to make ,one make mistakes, &amp;c., but I have done my best, and hope I have made it intelligible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>TOTAL CASUALTIES TO DATE.</head>
        <p>TOTAL CASUALTIES TO DATE.</p>
        <p>The unfortunate news that Private.Roughley was killed and not taken prisoner adds one more to the list of killed and reduces the miss- ing by one. The figures now are : Killed Wounded Missing Prisoners</p>
        <p>Total 547</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>TEN GUINEAS FROM CALEDONIAN</head>
        <p>TEN GUINEAS FROM CALEDONIAN</p>
        <p>WHIST DRIVE.</p>
        <p>The amount handed over to the local prisoners of war fund from the recent whist drive in Cairo-street Schools by the Warrington Cale- donian Association is £lO 10s. The ladies' com- mittee—Mesdames G. Bowman, W. Murray, J. Tennant, .T. Robertson, P. 11. Brown, W. Mitchell, J. McDerraid, E. Clayton, G. Millar and the Misses Ashdown and Hodge—are to be congratulated on the result. The committee also wish to thank all who contributed by goods or money to the fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>INCREASING COST OF COMFORTS.</head>
        <p>INCREASING COST OF COMFORTS.</p>
        <p>Sold everywhere in Hd. Books.</p>
        <p>We are requested to remind our readers that owing to the increased need of the prisoners for food during the winter months, and the increased prices of these articles, the parcels dispatched to the South Lancashire men in Ger- many now cost ss. each, and the cp.ll for sub- criptions is urgent.</p>
        <p>The dispatch- of the special parcels for Christmas has already begun. These contain ,ntip, cigarettes, bread, thick twist. rrrfirlii " sugar, tea or cocoa, margarine, golden syrup, meat cubes, potted meat-paste, corned beef. finned fish, jam or marmalade, and plum pud- ding or rich cake. These consignments are ex- peded to reach their addresses in time to tent. the lonely Christmas ay Upm those whom they</p>
        <p>of the 'tam 4o 'far a Add dear.</p>
        <p>--•'mNIMINOtaM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED WARRIORS.</p>
        <p>LIVING PICTURES FOR WINWICK</p>
        <p>The Lord Derby War Hospital at Winwick is shortly to be fitted up with a permanent cine- matograph and, for the purpose a raising funds to instal the same, a strong and influential committee has been formed. To help to get the money necessary it is proposed to hold a " Tommies' " tea at the Parr Hall on Decem- ber 9th. Though described as a " Tommies' " tea, the event will really be a public tea and sale of work. Each visitor to the hall is ex- pected to bring a gift, and each one is expected to make a purchase. A meeting to form a 54th-committee of the general committee responsible for thit event, was held on Tuesday evening in the Council Chamber of the Town Hall. Councillor H. Roberts was in the chair, and among those present were Mr. H. B. Houghton (secretary). Mr. H. J. Westbrook (treasurer), and Major Rogers, of the hospital.• At the outset Mr. Houghton read a letter from Lord Derby signifying his willingness .to became a patron of the fund. Nothing Better Than The Cinematograph." A short' address on the work of the hospital was given by Major Rogers. He said that, at the present moment, they had nearly 1,500 patients at the hospital, and ultimately they would have something like 2,200. No-fewer than 4,100 patients had passed through the hospital, and of those, 2,600 had been discharged. Those figures showed that the hospital was one of the largest—if not the largest—in the king- dom. The Government provided the necessi- ties for the hospital, but it depended upon the generosity of friends in Warrington and the neighbourhood to supply luxuries. Major Rogers also spoke of the necessity of ample funds being supplied for the provision of cigarettes, &amp;c., for the wounded. He pointed out the desirability of mental entertainment being provided .for the • soldiers, and said he knew of nothing better than thec,inematograph. The whole of the ladies .present were appointed on the ageneral ' committee, and the sub-com- mittees were formed :—Work stall, provisions stall, cake and flower stall, tea stall, antique stall and pound stall. The Mayoress will have charge of the last- named sub-committee.</p>
        <p>It v.-as also decided to ask the Mayor (Alder- nian Peter Peacock) to accept the presidency of the general committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>4MR. ATKINS" ENJOYS EXCELLENT CONCERT AT WHITECROSS.</head>
        <p>4MR. ATKINS" ENJOYS EXCELLENT CONCERT AT WHITECROSS.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday evening the hundred and fifty, or more, sick and wounded soldiers who are under treatment in the Whitecross Hospital wore entertained at a most enjoyable concert held in the dining-hall, Mr. W. Peter Rylands —who was accompanied ,by Mrs. Rylands— being in the Chair. The Chairman of the Man- agement Committee (Mr. A. J. Jolley) had previously conducted Mr. and Mrs. Rylands round the splendid new and spacious hospital, an experience which interested them vastly.</p>
        <p>The concert :was a great success, the con- valescent "Pommies" thoroughly enjoying the musical and other items provided for their delectation by the Misses May ilockenhull, and Doris and Nellie Welsby, Mrs. Dolan, Messrs. Angus, Boscow, Scarisbrick and Walter Sutton and Professor Baxteau, the well-known local conjuror. Miss Dolan and Mr. A. Trow were at the piano, • At the close of the entertainment Mr. Jolley proposed a vote of thanks to the contributors to the programme and also expressed thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Rylands for their presence and kindly interest in the hospital. Mr. Rylands, said Mr. Jolley, was always ready and willing to give his support to any good work that was being done ix the district, whether it be for wounded soldiers or for anybody else in need. Mr. A. Bottomley seconded_ the expression of thanks, which was carried unanimously. Mr. Rylands, in reply, expressed the plea- sure. it had given himself and Mrs. Rylands to he present, and paid a high tribute to the quality of the programme and the services of the artistes.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. C. Brooks was responsible for organ- iging the concert, which' was 'cpiit,e one of the most enjoyable yet arranged for the inmates of the hospital.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>WHIST DRIVE FOR THE THELWALL HEYS HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>WHIST DRIVE FOR THE THELWALL HEYS HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>The proceeds of a whist drive' held in the Socialist Hall in aid of the Thelwall llet3 Auxiliary Hospital, was .f. 41.2 Is. 7d., which sum has been handed over to the Working Men's Committee of the hospital. The whist drive, which was organised by Private and Mrs. J. B. Wood and a small committee, was a huge suc- cess, 300 persons taking part, 'including Mrs. Frost and her-staff of nurses, as well as about 16 wounded soldiers from the Thelwall Heys !Hospital. T,he'duties of M.C. were ably carried out by Mr. J. Helsby and Private J. B. Wood. 'Mrs. Frost kindly presented the prizes to the .winners, and the Chairman, Mr. Helsby, has -received a ,letter from the Hospital Committee thanking all those who contributed to the sue- cess of the whist drive, including the following, who kindly gave the prizes :—.The Independent "Labour Party, Mesdames Morris, Cox, Baker,' 'Marsh, Houghton, Lodge, Maginn, and Messrs. Walton, Grace. Whitney, J. B. Wood. Gifford, Kerfoot, Rathbone, Gittens, Pritchard, Breward, Horrabin, Helsby, Charnock, Carlisle, 'Craven, Gould, Worthington, Turpin and 'Wright.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MISS MARIE HOUGHTON AT</head>
        <p>MISS MARIE HOUGHTON AT</p>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>'The extensions at the Frodsham Auxiliary Military Hospital are approaching completion, and when finished it should be one of the most. useful institutions of its kind in the district. Its situation, on top of the hill, is excellent, and the residents and tradespeople in the locality, who have equipped it so efficiently, are to he congratulated on the result of their efforts to help their country in such a practical manner in this time of need. .</p>
        <p>The thirty-third. Concert for. the entertain- ment of the patients was given on Tuesday, the :arrangements being in the hands of Mr. Holman Kingdon. The artistes were Miss Marie Hough- ton, Messrs. Charles Lambert and Walter E. Jones, who received the hearty applause of their audience. The capabilities of Miss Houghton and Mr. Lambert. are so well known that the mention of individual items is unnecessary. Mr. Jones, of Man Chester, is a. pleasing bari- tone, and well earned a number of recalls, and the May be assured of .a hearty welcome when he is again able to visit the Warrington district.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>MUNITIONS TRIBUNAL.</head>
        <p>MUNITIONS TRIBUNAL.</p>
        <p>There Was only one case of local interest be- fore the Warrington Munitions Tribunal on Monday—the case of a young man who com- plained that his certificate had been refused by the management of the 'foundry at which he was employed, despite the fact that he had been discharged and the wages due to him up to the date of his discharge had been paid to him.— The applicant told the Tribunal that he was 27 years of age, was a fitter by trade, and had been employed by the firm against whom he lodged his complaint for about twelve months. He had been receiving a wage of 395. 3d. per week.-- "How were you discharged?" asked the Presi- dent of the Tribunal (Mr. T. J. Ridgway).—" I • claimed from the firm" replied the applicant, "because I was not receiving the same rate of pay as the other people in the shop, and I was discharged. I asked for my certificate and it was refused."—" You got your discharge simply • for asking for more wages?" the applicant was asked,; -and he answered "Yes."—The firm against whom the young man made his com- plaint was not represented, and the ,certificate he sought was granted him.—With the President of the Tribunal there sat as assessors Messrs. Henry Collin (for the masters) and Mr. Adam Bennett (for the men).</p>
        <p>ecoi by m, your o ligarett</p>
        <p>. A Study f .LWAYS ‘nomy USE aking 'wn • • ci es CICIMETT'E . VSE.SI' ik PUREST</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>_ priutographs are returned after Ilse, tutdama;:d to the owners. /Mr.</head>
        <p>_ priutographs are returned after Ilse, tutdama;:d to the owners. /Mr.</p>
        <p>An letters and photographs are returned after use, T,Lndamozd, to the owners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>STAFF-SERGEANT CUNNINGHAM</head>
        <p>STAFF-SERGEANT CUNNINGHAM</p>
        <p>Dardanelles in July last. About six weeks ago he received a printed document. which gave the following details 10th (Irish) Division, No. 8672, Staff-Sergt. P. Cunningham, R.A.M.C., 31st Field Ambu- lance.</p>
        <p>Your Commanding Officer has informed me that you have distinguished yourself by your good work in the field. I have read his report with much pleasure and have forwarded it to a higher authority for recognition,</p>
        <p>B. XIAHON, - Lieutenant-General, Commanding 10th (Irish) Division,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>29th September, 1915. WOUNDED MAN'S TERRIBLE EXPERIENCE.</head>
        <p>29th September, 1915. WOUNDED MAN'S TERRIBLE EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>Writing to his wife, who is now residing at 17, Stafford-road, Wilderspool, Warrington, and describing his experiences on "Chocolate " Hill, Staff-Sergeant Cunningham, says:—" I had a very near squeak on Friday night. I went for a walk down by the reserve trenches. I was not on duty at the time. A shrapnel shell burst overhead and stretched out one of the 6th Royal Munsters. He was not very seriously injured—he had a hole in the thigh—but he begged me to get him out of the way. All the rest had gone on to their places in the trenches. I slung him over my shoulder—the fireman's lift—and was carrying him off when another shell burst and he was hit again—in the back this time. I carried him about a quarter of a mile and laid him on a big stone and dressed his wounds. I thought we were well away from the danger zone, when, all at once, the shells started flying round, though there was nothing near for the Turks to re at. I lay over the patient's head and shoulders to shield him and we got a shower of shrapnel bullets raining around us. One of them passed between 'my arm and body and hit him in the small of the back. He screamed frightfully and begged me to get him away. I picked him up again, and this time got 'him safely awaY. I took him to the clearing hospital, and he is sure to recover. That is the fourth time I have had patients hit whilst carrying theni. I was once leading a section of eight men, who were in single file, and each of them was hit. . . . The only feel- ing I notice is anger, and I nearly curse the Turks and Germans when things get lively."</p>
        <p>CRAWLED A MILE UNDER HEAVY FIRE. On Another occasion Staff-Sergeant Cunning- ham crawled nearly a mile under a heavy fire. It/was known at the ambulance headquarters that a certain regiment had •suffered heavily, but its exact locality was not known. He and another soldier were detailed to find it. They had not gone far before the other soldier was shot through the heart. The Staff-Sergeant con- tinued alone, crawling on his bands and knees under fire. He accomplished his object and re- turned safely to headquarters.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>FOR SOLDIERS' COMFORTS.</head>
        <p>FOR SOLDIERS' COMFORTS.</p>
        <p>Liberal Women Arrange More Enjoyable Functions.</p>
        <p>In aid of the funds of the War Sewing Class in connection with the Warrington Women's Liberal Association, the said class being devoted to the making of "comforts "—which are daily becoraing increasingly necessary—for soldiers, two, tea meetings and concerts are to be held in the early part of December. The first, in fact, takes place on December 6th, and will be specially set apart for Orford Wardians ; although„,or course, there is not the slightest reason why Liberals from every other ward in the town should not flock to the ( Crosfield Memorial Hall—for that is where the function is to be held—on the evening named. The other tea-meeting concert is primarily intended for Mt Liberals of the Town Hall and Bewsey Wards, and it takes place on the Tuesday—December 7th.</p>
        <p>At each of ,the enjoyable forthcpming func- tions Lady Crosfield will occupy the chair, and will be supported by Sir Arthur H. Crosfield, Bart., and Miss Truda Crosfield;. as well as prominent local Liberals.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>RADDON COURT HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Soldier Arts-and-Craftsmen Exhibit Their Skill.</p>
        <p>Last Friday, at Raddon Court Red Cross Hos- pital, was held an Arts and Crafts Exhibition.' but, unfortunately, owing to the inclement weather, it was not attended by tho success it deserved. Many lovely water-colour paintings, the work of Sergeant Bryan, of the West Yorks Regiment, were on view, and they. earned the admiration of the onlookers. Some elaborate knitting was also shown, including jerseys, scarves, &amp;c., the work of Corporal Brown, of the Northumberland Fusiliers, and Private Titheredge, of the Hampshire Regiment. Prizes are being awarded for the best workmanship, the judges being the Township Leaders. The exhibition is again open to-day between the hours of two and four in the afternoon. _</p>
        <p>There was also, yesterday, an auction sale of goods received from private sources, the bil- ding in some cases reaching very fair figurei. A diminutive khaki cap was put up for sale several times, and realised the handsome total of 15s. 6d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>PRIVATE W. WHEELER.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE W. WHEELER.</p>
        <p>Damaged Fingers Speedily Getting</p>
        <p>-Better.</p>
        <p>Private William Wheeler, a Reservist, of the 2nd Battalion South Lancs. Regiment, who served in the Boer War, has been wounded in the fingers by shrapnel. He is now making a speedy recovery in an English hospital. Private Wheeler, who is a married man, lives in peace times at 111, Church-street, Warring- ton. Before being called up-on the outbreak of hostilities he was employed by Messrs. E. For- shaw atia Son. Ltd., engineers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <p>PRIVATES 0. SANT and W: WILLIAMS and their fellow prisoners, said to be Warring-</p>
        <p>ton: men. Private Sant i-i hatless and standing. Private Williams. is in the centre. t•irting.</p>
        <p>Private Walter Williams to his wife, 12, Clare-street, WinwielL-road.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>RYLANDS'S GALVANISER.</head>
        <p>A RYLANDS'S GALVANISER.</p>
        <p>Private Sant, who was in the 2nd. Battalion &amp;filth Lancashire Regiment, was taken prisoner at Mons: It was five months before his rela- tives heard from him:and it was feared that he was dead. Since then he has been in regular communication with his friends, who have sent him many parcels of goods—including clogs and jersey so that he would not have to dirty hi,: tunic in the mines—all of which have arrived and been acknowledged. It is surmised that he is employed by the Germans in a mine'. Thr wire entanglement shown in the Picture is electrified.,</p>
        <p>Private Sant, who has spent his eighteenth birthday in the German camp, was in the gal- vanising department at Messrs. Rylands Bros. He attended Wycliffe and Beamont Day Schools and St. Peter's Sunday School. • Private Williams. who was employed at the Pearson and Knowles' forges as a furnaeeman. also belonged to the 2nd South Lancs., and wti captured at Mons, after having been wounded in the leg ,and arm. He is 31 years of age, and has two little boys, aged six and seven. In his case many of the parcels that have been sent have remained unacknowledged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0151" n="art0151">
        <head>PRISONER WHO THINKS OF WIFE'S LONELINESS.</head>
        <p>PRISONER WHO THINKS OF WIFE'S LONELINESS.</p>
        <p>On Thursday Mrs. Williams receiv..d the following letter from -her .husband. which was dated October 26th—the letter .having taken about -I;hree weeks to reach</p>
        <p>"Just v line, hoping that yoo rinn . the chil- dren ft.i.si all right. I :am• sending yla a photo of myself t-ind four of my regiment. • The one on the right is Oswald Sant, a Warrington man. I worked for his father on the tramway. You have heard me speak of him at home. I know how lonely you will be without me, but I will be at home some day with God's help to you and our Walter and Arthur: I want some shirts and socks and some clothes fot• the winter as the winter here will be cold, I think . . . and don't forget the cigarettes, my best friend here. Kiss Walter and Arthur for me any my best love to you all. Good night and God bless you all.'• It will be noticed that two of the prisoners are wearing horseshoes, perhaps a device for wishing their friends and country good luck. Only two of the men are known to us. Do you know the other three?' ;',,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>" READY FOR THE . TASK ! "</head>
        <p>" READY FOR THE . TASK ! "</p>
        <p>Rifleman Colter Describes Rushing Of</p>
        <p>German Trenches.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Colter, of Battersby-lane, a brother of the late Rifleman Victor Colter, who was killed in action while serving with the King's Royal Rifles in October last, has received from another soldier brother—Rifleman Cecil Colter, of the Prince Consort's Rifle Brigade—an in- teresting letter. Guns That Spat Forth Death.</p>
        <p>"We went into the trenches about 2.30 a.m. the other day," he writes. "We were all astir ready for the time when we should go over. Everything was so quiet until the artillery opened .fire. This was at about 4.25 a.m. I could not helri admiring the grand sight. It was one semi-circle of fire on our side and a similar spectacle on the enemy's tide. The sky was well ablaze—and then the smoke came. The smell of this seemed to get a grip on us, but of course with a drop of rum to warm us we were ready for the task before us. While the guns spat forth death, we crept out to the enemy's trenches. Then our guns lifted from the first line of trenches to the second. By this time we were against their wire entanglements.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE G. DCMGIILEY. ' Billed in Action.</p>
        <p>Geneva on November 10, R. Roughley, 1548, 4th South Lancashires, is reported to have fallen in action between September 25th and October Ist, south-west of Ypres, and to have been buried near there, at the Friedhof Nachtigall. The number of his grave is 350. Should any further details come to hand they will at once be for- warded to you." His Captain's Regard.</p>
        <p>Last week Mrs. Itoughley received a letter from Captain T. Ridgway saying that her son was with the bombers, September 25th, and if not killed was probably a prisoner in the hands .of the Germans, as he was wounded before the enemy re-occupied the trenches the Territorials had. taken. "I knew your son well," added the Captain, "and had a great regard for him. He was always cheery and bright, and in re- (rest at anything in the nature of a concert."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>"Great Massive Prussian Brutes."</head>
        <p>"Great Massive Prussian Brutes."</p>
        <p>"As soon as the guns lifted we went forward over their parapet. There were only about a dozen in this line give. These were old men, and what with being*et through—it rained all the previous night and all that day—l can tell you they did look a siklifk I have a poor opinion of the Huns after this. We got the first line with hardly a casualty. Then the guns lifted for the third line, and then we fought for the second. Each man carried bombs on this occa- sion, and I can assure you soon drove the enemy out of this trench. What with bombs and cold steel—well, they didn't like it. I was surprised at the difference in the men' we found in this line. They were great massive brutes and be- longed to the Prussian Guards. The first lot were Bavarians. It was a bit of sport in this line. Fifty gave themselves up, so we sent them over the top back to our lines. The Germans opened a rapid fire on them when they saw they were giving themselves up. "Every Man for Himself."</p>
        <p>"Prior to the bombardment my pal and I had to lay a wire to the German barbed wire. While the boys were fighting for the third line, we stepped back and got the reel of wire. We thep opened communication with our original trench, telling them how we were doing. We also had a lamp on the parapet, which proved very useful when our wire was broken. The boys got the third line after a bit of a struggle, as the Huns were thick here, and so was the mud. Our boys then reversed the parapet and kept them back. There were bombs flying in all directions, both from us and the Huns. I can assure you these are deadly. things. It was getting close on dinner-time, and we were expecting reinforce- ments, but we were still waiting. About two o'clock the Runs seemed to creep round us from either side, bombing and sniping at us all the time. Many of, them also wore khaki caps and coats. Of course, this puzzled our boys, and for a time we didn't know what to do. The next thing we saw was that we were nearly cut off. It was then 'we were given the order to retire, every man for himself. We got back to our original lines 4. but had we had reinforcements we could have held on for ever, but it was not to bet</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>An Anxious Two Months.</head>
        <p>An Anxious Two Months.</p>
        <p>It •is three weeks since Mrs. Roughley re- ceived official news that her son had either been killed or taken prisoner. A month previously she had been unofficially informed that he was missing. She was told that on the 25th Sep- tember the South Lancashire bombers assisted to take three trenches, from which they were eventually driven by the Germans. "Your son," ran the letter, "was one of the bombers, there being three of them in the front line. The Germans ouTflanked us, both left and right, and the order was given for us to retire as quickly as possible. Of the three bombers, two—Privates Gleaver and Bowles— managed to escape. From this report it -would appear. that Private Roughley was either killed or taken prisoner." All this anxiety has now been set at rest in the saddest possible manner. Private Roughley was 22 years of age. He was a member of D Company, and attended as a boy the Hegthside School. He is a married man—having been married during a brief leave of absence on the eve of his departure for France. His widow lives in West-street. He was in the Territorials about three years, and was employed as a fireman on the London and North-Western Railway. Mrs. Roughley has two other sons on active service—Tames Percy Roughley, who is a wire- less operator, in the Navy, and George Greenall Roughley, who is in the 2nd South Lancashires.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>PRIVATE J. MOORCROFT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE J. MOORCROFT.</p>
        <p>Only At The Front About A Fortnight.</p>
        <p>A letter written by the regimental chaplain, the Rev. M. P. G. Leonard, conveyed to Mrs. Moorcroft the news that her husband, Private John Moorcroft, of the King's Own Royal Lancaster Regiment, and elder son of Mr. Herbert Moorcroft, of 104, Gorsey-lane, had been killed in action. The letter intimated tha, Private Moorcroft was killed instantly by a shell, whilst in the front trenches. "Joined Up" in the Early Days of the War. Private Moorcroft, who was 27 years of age, had, for the last two, or three years lived at Irlam, where he had been employed by the Partington Steel and Iron Co., Ltd. He enlisted about twelve months ago, but had only been at the Front about a fortnight. He was educated at the Silver-street School.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>SAPPER E. CHILTON.</head>
        <p>SAPPER E. CHILTON.</p>
        <p>"An Enterprising Fellow, And Plucky ! "</p>
        <p>Through the medium of a letter written to her by Second-Lieutenant J. L. Adams, coin- manding a section of the Royal Engineers, Mrs. Chilton, of 35, Allen-street, on Thursday learned that her son, Sapper Edwin Chilton, also of the Royal i;ngineers, had been killed at the Front.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant's High Tribute.</p>
        <p>The letter ran as follows:</p>
        <p>"You have probably heard already, through official journals, of the death of your son Edwin. May I just write a few words to say how sorry we all are and how much we shall miss him. He was an enterprising fellow, plucky, a good worker, and a favourite among his mates. On Thursday last we were doing some particular work in the firing-line, when a falling bullet hit him near the heart. He died in about two minutes, quite painlessly, I believe. We buried him on Friday in a British Military Cemetery." "Joined Up " Early On.</p>
        <p>Sapper Chilton, who was 23 years of age, attended, as a boy, the Wycliffe School. He enlisted in September of last year, having been previously employed as a plater by Messrs. Pearson and Knowles. He .worshipped at St. Paul's Church, and was a member of Mr. Wilkes' Bible Class, which is connected with the church. A brother-in-law of the dead man, Private Leigh, is in training with a new battalion of the Ring's (Liverpool) Regiment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>MISSING.</head>
        <p>MISSING.</p>
        <p>PRIVATE W. BIRD.</p>
        <p>Not Heard 01 Since The Great</p>
        <p>Advance.</p>
        <p>Mr. Henry Bird, 70, Liverpool-road, has been informed that his son, Private William Bird, of the machine-gun section attached to the Ving's Own Scottish Borderers, has been missing since the great British advance of September 25-27. The news was first conveyed in a letter from Lieutenant C. N. Crawshaw, whilst an official notification from the War Office has also since been forwarded to the father.</p>
        <p>Private T. Farrington, a " pal " of the miss- ing soldier, who has himself been wounded, and is at present on furlough, states that he has not seen any sign of Private Bird since before the commencement of the big charge, when they both clasped hands and wished each other "good luck." . Private lUrcl, who is 11 year 4 of age, was</p>
        <p>he has written to his mother to the effect that he is progressing favourably.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <p>ledneated at Heathside• School, Warrington, and before joining his Majesty's Forces was employed by Messrs. Monks-, Hall and CO., Ltd., in their tube department.. Coming of a patriotic family, he has a brother at present in the Navy, who has seen service off the West Coast of Africa, as well as taking part in engagements in the Dardanelles. Further news of the missing man is anxiously awaited by his relatives, who are conflicted with doubt xespecting his where- abouts'.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>PRIVATE HARRY POWNALL</head>
        <p>PRIVATE HARRY POWNALL</p>
        <p>Lost gight Of Since' September 25th.</p>
        <p>Private Harry Pownall, of the Somerset Light Tnfantry, who, before enlistment, lived with his sister, Mrs. Taylor, at 7, Raddon Court Cottages, Latchford, has been officially posted Is "missing." The notification bearing' in- 'ormation to this effect, reached Mrs. Taylor on Tuesday of last week, in the form of the follow- :ug notice from the Exeter Record Office:— " I regret to have to inform you that a report has been received from the War Office to the -fleet that No. 15460, Private H. Pownall, was --&gt;osted as missing after an engagement with he British Expeditionary Force on the 25th -ptember. Should he subsequently rejoin, or ,ther information be received concerning him. •uch information will at once be communicated f-o you."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>Keen On Football.</head>
        <p>Keen On Football.</p>
        <p>Private Pownall enlisted in August of last -oar, having been previously employed by rossrs. S. Reynolds and Co., tanners; Latchford. s a boy he attended St. James' School. ' Ithough not himself a player, he took a big :nterest in football.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>WOUNDED.</head>
        <p>WOUNDED.</p>
        <p>RIFLEMAN W. SHAW.</p>
        <p>lilt 'Alen Putting OuiTjie Entanglements.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. James Rough, of 2, Marbury- street, Latchford, in addition to receiving news of the wounding of their nephew, Private Thos. Cark—reported elsewhere—have also heard that their other nephew, who resided with them, Rifleman W. Shaw, has been wounded in action. In a letter dated October 15th, he writes : "ft was on October Ist that I was hit. We were barb-wiring in front of our bombing-post. We had just completed twenty-four hours in the trenches, and had missed the big charge at Loos. They had us going up in reserve. It is a pitiful sight to see Ypres, it is one mass of ruins."</p>
        <p>Progressing Satisfactorily In English Hospital.</p>
        <p>Rifleman Shaw is attached to the Bth Battalion Rifle Brigade, and is at present lying in the University College Hospital, Gower-street; Lon- don, W.C., where he is progressing very satis- factorily. He enlisted at the commencement of hostilities, and has witnessed many stirring engagements. Like his cousin—Private Carr— he was employed at Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co.'s works. He was a member of the Vicar's Bible Class at St. James' Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>PROGRESSING FAVOURABLY.</head>
        <p>PROGRESSING FAVOURABLY.</p>
        <p>Latest News Concerning Driver A.</p>
        <p>Cox.</p>
        <p>As we reported in our last issue, news has reached Mrs. Cox, 107, Oldham-street, Latch- ford, to the effect that her youngest son—Driver Albert Cox, of the Royal Field Artillery, has been wounded in the left leg. It has been sub- sequently learned that he has been admitted into a hospital in Scotland, from which place</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <p>DRIVER ALBERT COX. Wounded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0164" n="art0164">
        <head>PRIVATE THOMAS CARR.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE THOMAS CARR.</p>
        <p>Hit In Dardanelles Fight And Now In England.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. James Hough, of 2, Marbury- street, Latchford, have received word that their nephew, Private Thomas Carr, of the Royal Munster Fusillers, was wounded in action in the Dardanelles on August 21st. Private Carr landed in England a few days ago, and is at present an inmate of the 2nd Eastern Hospital, Brighton, where he is progressing favourably. It now appears that not only was the. Private wounded, but that he has also been suffering from dysentery, and was in hospital in Malta for some time.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Miracle a Man Escaped.</head>
        <p>Miracle a Man Escaped.</p>
        <p>The last letter received from him with the exception of a postcard from Brighton, was written on October 11th, and in it he stated "Things are very quiet here at present. I think it is the calm before the storm, for if I am not very much mistaken there will be a 'bust up' here befoce long, and something will have to go. I never told you how I received my wounds. We were advancing up a broad IT- shaped valley. The shrapnel and bullets were as thick as rain. It was a miracle how a single man got through without bezng hit. A shrapnel shell burst at my feet. The full force of the explosion must have caught my rifle, for it went spinning out of my hands, I don't know where I I was blind for a few minutes. My eyes were filled with dirt, and believe me, they were minutes of agony.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>Thought He Was Blind.</head>
        <p>Thought He Was Blind.</p>
        <p>You can imagine my feelings lying there thinking I was blind. Presently one of my eyes opened a little; then the other—and then I scraped the dirt off my face and went nearly mad with joy when I found I could see all right! Up I jumped, and started to make my way to the hospital, when I met a doctor, who gave me ray first dressing. Then I had a sur- prise. My leg and hand were a bit bad, but my face, which I thought the worst, had not even the skin broken. Anyhow I am all right now."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>An Old Volunteer.</head>
        <p>An Old Volunteer.</p>
        <p>Private Carr is a single man, and was educa- ted at St. James' Sohool, and was also a mem- ber of the First Warrington Company of the Church Lads' Brigade attached to St. James' Church. Previous to the outbreak of war he had had 16 years' military experience with the old Warrington Volunteers. He comes of a fine patriotic family, and at present has a brother, Private J. Carr, in the Dardanelles, and four cousins in his Majesty's service. Ho was employed by Messrs. Fletcher, Russell and Co., Ltd., and he is well-known and respected alike by Juasteri ad fellov worlzeru.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>STORIES FROM THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>EX-POLICE INSPECTOR</p>
        <p>Who Can Keep Pace With The Youngest Of Them.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable (Mr, Martin Nicholls) has received the following really stirring letter from ea-Inspector George Phillips, who, although over 53 years of age, is at the Front doing his bit, and incidentally doing his best: to shame the young "slackers" who still stand aloof :</p>
        <p>"Somewhere, 12/11/15.</p>
        <p>"Dear Sir,—Doubtless you have often won- dered how I was faring in my new pilgrimage. I'm not allowed to divulge names of places and localities, but we've been where the fighting has been fiercest ever since the war started. I'm 'in the pink' up to date, and have so far only received a hit on the shoulder blade from a fragment of German shell at , which quickly healed. Our battalion has been very fortunate in regard to casualties, having had comparatively few killed and wounded. We are at present having a rest after our work in the trenches, and are waiting eagerly for the order to advance once more. I've done my full share of forced marching with a full pack on, but sometimes I have to admit that I'm net so young as I used to be; however, I can keep pace with the youngest of 'ern. I sincerely hope you and yours are enjoying the best of health, and that the comrades with whom I served are in the same category. Please give my kind re- gards to Mr. Fairharst, and thank him very much for his kindness in so regularly forward- ing my pension, and at the same time remem- ber me to all old friends and comrades. I should be glad of a line from someone with whom Lserved in old times. The weather here is awfully wet and cold, and the light is fading, so good-bye, sir, and, good luck. "GEORGE PHILLIPS, Sergt."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>Real Patriotism.</head>
        <p>Real Patriotism.</p>
        <p>Ex-Chief Inspector Phillips, who served in the local police force for over 18 years, being granted a pension in 1907, having served for nearly seven years in the Ist Royal Dragoons, joined the Warrington Borough Police some 26 years ago. At the end of 18 years' service he was granted a pension. At the outbreak of war he at once offered his services, was accepted,</p>
        <p>and is now serving in the King's Liverpool Regi- meat as a sergeant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>LIEUTENANT MILN.</head>
        <p>LIEUTENANT MILN.</p>
        <p>Old School Friends Meet In Gallipoli.</p>
        <p>The mails from Gallipoli have recently been considerably delayed, but writing from some- where in the Peninsula on September 22nd, Lieutenant Gordon Miln, of the Cheshires, says: "We moved up to the firing line on Monday night, a different firing line from those we have been in previously. Curiously enough, I met an old King's School boy here, R. N. Foster, a Second-Lieutenant in the Cheshires, whom I had not seen since he left school in 1907. In fact, I had the pleasure of handing over our own palatial dug-out to him for himself and the other officers left in his company. I would have written yesterday, but there are only two officers now to do the work of our company, as our third officer is doing, sniping work with the batalion. Cullimore, with his machine gunners. is in the middle of our company firing trenches, and is consequently a welcome addition to our little mess, so that there are four of us to sit down to meals together to produce each our own little contribution in the way of luxuries from home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>Mr .Turk Didn't Mind.</head>
        <p>Mr .Turk Didn't Mind.</p>
        <p>"Last night we had to be content with a piece of cake, and cocoa, in lieu of dinner, as there was practically no wood on hand to do cooking with. To make up the deficiency in fire- wood, I had the pleasure of sending three men out to saw an old tree in front of our treach and hauled the branches in. Mr. Turk didn't seem to mind, but I do not know what he thought was happening when the men carried them up the trench to the cookhouse. It looked like a. small shrubbery walking up the trench in the bright moonlight. We are again not very closs. to the Turkish trenches-300 yards away on an average, but, of course, snipers come much nearer to catch the unwary. We had a Attie shelling this morning, as Mr. Turk seemed to object to a party of us who were digging. I suppose he saw the earth being thrown np. Anyhow, he got a shell very close to where we were working. In fact, I just walk,si round and found their shell ease in the trerrit and several shrapnel bullets lying about. I brought the case to our dug-out for general in- spection, as it is a trvi7l smal in. one than is generally used. In the company here with us there are four officers to share the watch, which only gives us two hours' watch each between 5.30 p.m. and 4.3') a.-a.. but then one lcus further slegp when sime...me comes and wakes one up by mistake."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>Turkish Joke.</head>
        <p>A Turkish Joke.</p>
        <p>Writing later, on Fepteraber 26th, he states:— "The men have just received a bumper parcel of ` fags ' from Miss Brown, of St. Mary's Schools. I believe a Mrs. Moore was the kind donor, and as each man received about 20, the Army is very happy, especially as we also had fresh meat servedout to-day. The Turks imagined they were being funny this morning. About 6 a.m. there was a great shout of ' Allah !' ' Allah !' on our right, which is their battle cry, of course. Everyone got ready to meet a Turkish charge, artillery having already bom- barded, but no Turk appeared, so we only pre sumed it was a new form of Turkish humour to ' enliven the monotony of trench life. The country round about the trenches is very beauti- ful. Fig trees are plentiful, but no figs, and you can still see the stubble where the remains of this year's harvest have not been collected in. Although the weather is getting much. cooler, the flies are still a very great nuisance, especially about now (4 p.m.), when they come along to the dug-out from somewhere I know not. It does one good to read in the men's letetrs, which are censored, how they intend to stick to the business in spite of their .Itts ef illness, and how they all hope and pray that they will see their homes once more." Wheat Specimens.</p>
        <p>in a later communication, dated September 29th, Lieutenant Ililn says:—" Herewith specimens of Gallipoli wheat, which I picked about 6 a.m., just after we stood to arms for 11 hours. We shifted flown to the support line last night, and are now in a trench which cuts through cornfields. Lots of the straw is still stacked, and I do not know why the authorities do not collect it and nse it for the mules, as it could easily be carted away by night. I shall be on my own for a day or two, starting this afternoon, as I am taking half the company into another support line mid-way between us and the firing line. It is not far from here, however, and I shall probably be able to stroll down here and have meitls with the other two officers."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Quite Necessary.</head>
        <p>Quite Necessary.</p>
        <p>Mamma: "Myrtle dear, was it necessary to spend 15 minutes bidding Harry good-night F" "Yes, mamma; Harry stutters so when he is agitated."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>War Letters and Photographs.</head>
        <p>War Letters and Photographs.</p>
        <p>We invite our readers to submit to us letters received from their soldier friends. We shall also be glad to receive photo- graphs of soldiers who have been killed, wounded, mentioned in disnatches or other- wise become a source of interest to the public.</p>
        <p>The letters will be carefully edited so that nothing will appear in print that ought not to appear.</p>
        <p>The photographs, when suitable, will be published in "The Examiner," which has an established reputation as a picture paper.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="3" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>DANDY MYSTERY SOLVED.</head>
        <p>DANDY MYSTERY SOLVED.</p>
        <p>Judge Senlo Prisoner To Penal Servitude</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>FOR MOST IMPUDENT CRIME.</head>
        <p>FOR MOST IMPUDENT CRIME.</p>
        <p>At the Manchester Assizes on Thursday, before Mr. Justice Low,</p>
        <p>George Parkin Hall, alias Herbert Dandy, 36 years of age, labourer, was charged with com- mitting an offence against Sarah Jane Dandy, a married woman, of Clowes-street, Gorton, by personating her husband, Herbert Dandy. As previously explained in "The Examiner," the real Sergeant Dandy is a Lymm man, being the son of Mr. and Mrs. Frederick Dandy, of Newfields. ,He was born in Lymm. Sergeant Herbert Moston Dandy is 38 years of age. When eight he went with his parents to America, returning to Lymm three years later. After a year at the village school he became an apprentice fustian-cutter with the late Mr. F. 0. Moston, for three years; thence to Messrs. Moorhouse, ginger beer makers, Manchester; the Ashton Militia; and back eventually to Lymm on his former work. Next he joined the local Volunteers under Sergeant-Instructor Howarth, later also enlisting at Preston in the 2nd Loyal North Lancs. On the South African War outbreak he was at Malta, where he offered his services in the Malta Mounted Infantry. During this he gained the Distinguished Service Medal by helping to save a convoy. Only seven officers and one or two men survived this, the officers getting V.C.'s and the men D.S.O.'s. Private Dandy simultaneously was also promoted Sergeant.</p>
        <p>JUDGE AND THE PECULIARITY OF THE</p>
        <p>o'clock, promising to return the next day, but at 7 p.m. a telegram came asking Mrs. Dandy to meet the defendant. on the steps of the hos- pital. She did so, took him home, and he re- mained with her as her husband for some days. Then Mrs. Dandy became suspicious, and asked him why he was dressed as a private. He said he had broken out of Netley Hospital because they were going to put him in a padded room, and he took the first clothes handy. She asked him what had become of the abscess mark on his neck and tattoo marks. He answered that the first had been filled up and the *second burned out. On July 23rd she went to the hospital with him, and from something said by a soldier she occupied a separate room. Afterwards Mrs. Dandy sent for her brother in Yorkshire, and also for a sister, and both thought the defend- ant was her husband. They took him with them to Yorkshire for a few days, and after his return on Au,gust 9th Mrs. Dandy would not allow him to stay in the house until he had proved he was Sergt. Dandy. She went to the station to see him off, and there he was arrested by Detective Inspector Thomas, who afterwards tested his handwriting by an old letter from Se4Teant Dandy. Inspector Thomas, who had been making inquiries for the military authori- ties, was satisfied that the defendant's writing was not like that of Sergeant Dandy. question then arose, who was the defendant? and counsel said he would call the deferal,iiit's wife, Ada Hall, of Thirlow-street, Patricrat.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>CASE.</head>
        <p>CASE.</p>
        <p>The Judge, charging the Grand Jury at the opening of the Assizes on Tuesday, said the peculiarity of the case arose from the circum- stance that the prisoner, as was usual in charges or that nature, did not use force to Accomplish his purpose, but was alleged to have used arti- fice amounting to fraud. The woman's hus- band had gone away to the war, and it was said that the prisoner returned from the war and personated the husband. There were some re-</p>
        <p>markable features in the case, in that the hus- band had been absent a comparatively short time, something under a year, and yet his sister and other relatives seemed to have be- lieved that the prisoner was the husband. Be- yond question he lived and cohabited for some days and nights with the wife. Concluding, the Judge said he thought there was certainly ground in that case for a careful investigation by a petty jury. _ .</p>
        <p>The Grand Jury -retlinied-a true bill against Hall.</p>
        <p>COUNSEL'S STATEMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brocklehurst, who prosecuted, when the Case came forward on Thursday, explained that the charge was made under the Criminal Law Amendment Act. A man who committed the offence named against a' woman by impersonat- ing her husband was deemed guilty under the section, the consent of the woman being im- material. Mrs. Dandy was the wife of a ser- geant in the Bth Manchesters, who went out to Egypt last autumn, and she kept a baker's shop in Clowes-street. On the morning of July 16th a neighbour named Dutton brought the defend- ant into the shop. Mrs. Dandy asked who the man was, and Dutton said "Herbert." Mrs. Dandy said "Never." Explaining the defend- ant's appearance, counsel said that before enter- ing Mrs. Dandy's shop the defendant went into Dutton's shop and said, "How is Mr. Dutton getting on?" Mr. Dutton inquired, "Is it Herbert Dandy?" and the defendant answered "Yes." The defendant explained that he had got an hour off from Whitworth-street Hospital, and asked Dutton to tell his wife and children, but after some discussion he was induced to go himself, Dutton accompanying him. The wife was suspicious and said, "You have altered," and the defendant replied that he had lain for 72 hours without help on the battlefield, and that was sufficient to alter anyone. Mrs. Dandy began to press him about personal matters, and in answer to questions he said he had lost his memory. The defendant was , undoubtedly, Mrs. Dandy's husband. She sent for her hus- band's sister, and the sister took him to he Sergeant Dandy. The neighbours were simi- larly deceived. The defendant left at one</p>
        <p>with them. All this time she thought he was her husband, though he had altered. The neighbours appeared to think he was the real Sergeant Dandy. Witness said she sent for Mrs. Watson, Sergeant Dandy's sister, and she also thought the prisoner was the real Sergeant Dandy. She thought he was a wreck and cried bitterly. One of the neighbours, a Mrs. Row- bottom, who knew Sergeant Dandy well, took him back to the hospital. After she had met him at the hospital in response to the telegram, witness said they went home and lived as man and wife for a week. The prisoner made a " fuss " of the children, and everyone accepted him as Sergeant Dandy. Explaining the reason he was in the uniform of a private, the prisoner said he had broken out of Netley Hospital. The authorities there, he said, talked about putting him in a padded room. In coming away he got another soldier's uniform.</p>
        <p>• &gt;it, 4 ' B.r•Li ' ca- * ..'s;V..4'›fei '''''l4`7•4l;''''');l'i Mr;xtff,4F4.4t;-'''-.::::','-',-,:47'-\',:'-r-',;;:lljAiPfi,kt*-:3:ft , , , - ,-05,1•4,'A:',1,,42,it 1:41A*4:::- 3"4,,,,y4.11,,,ita1i- 0„'.;:.b.\,,!,,,,,,,41, '-:' -,,,t ~,,,,,,,,,vrr„ -1, • '-' - , ..‘ ;.:,,,,;,34404,..ic..,,,4„- ~" • t'..r.;;.%+, t,',&gt;,,,N ' C',..',-,C;F:11, ', - ' ', - AN44.t. s,',!';':'/;'&lt;'':;,•"''';',,,, 3&gt; ~,,,, :; ~t,,7;,,,,,,„:::47;r0„.:109;''104:"A,Vitil ~,,...4,,,,,,,is 1!::`5.iii:Y.1;F:3'..1,41111, ~ •-• ' , ',.:: , : - . : ' " ' %-:„eir7g2 . " :-..-:: ;,,,* .'.. - - ' . ''., . • ' , ~.',, ~:::,' -- , - •.., •-: :1;.:, ' ~ - . ~,..,, ~..,;,,,A,,,,-,,,t.: •-r..!,‘„, .:::-,..- '.. . , . ...,:„:77,.,......„.”...... ~ ~,. _ . 7. :. • • •</p>
        <p>THE REAL SERGEANT DANDY. Missing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>Ac le Kidney , ) is --ase</head>
        <p>Ac le Kidney , ) is --ase</p>
        <p>Quick Relief and Speedy Cure by Dr. Cassell's Tablets.</p>
        <p>"I used to suffer from frigttful grind- ing pain in the small of my back." says Mrs. Elliott,. of 6. Rues°ll-grove, Regent, Park-road, Birming- ham. "My head ached as though it would split, and I had no heart or energy for anything. When I got up in the morning I trembled from head to foot. I got little</p>
        <p>sleep .at niiht, and just felt worn out in mind and body. My akin was of a. sickly, unhealthy colour, and puffy rims formed round my eyes. I need to dread meal times coming round, for all food disagreed with me. Though I had the best medical advice. I got no better, but worse. It was very different with Dr. Cassell's Tab. lets; they cured me completely. and I shall never cease to praise them.'!</p>
        <p>es- • • , mrz. Elliott.</p>
      </div>
