      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>MRS. DANDY'S STORY.</head>
        <p>MRS. DANDY'S STORY.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Sarah Jane Dandy, who was neatly dressed in black, said that after her husband was gone she received letters from him from .Cairo, and the last she, heard of him was in May of this year. She .saw the prisoner out- side the shop on July 16th. She was too excited to notice his uniform. - At the time he arrived two ladies were in the shop and they helped him in. Neighbours got to hear the news and came in and cried over him. He shook hands</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <p>IE COUNTERFEIT SERGEANT DAN In Gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>Vanished Tattoo Marks.</head>
        <p>Vanished Tattoo Marks.</p>
        <p>He said his tattoo marks had been burned out in Egypt. She sent for her brother, Fred Butterworth, and when he arrived the prisoner took him to be a detective and threatened to throw him out of the house, saying "This place is mine." The prisoner did not appear to know him, and when witness said, "This is my brother," prisoner calmed down and shook hands with him. Her brother seemed to think he was Sergeant Dandy. ' • The next day witness's own sister came and prisoner cried, remarking to her, "Oh, Agnes, I am glad to get home." Her sister accepted him as,Sergt. Dandy. On the following Sunday the prisoner went with her sister and brother to Marsden, in Yorkshire, and stayed there until August 9th. She saw him at Exchange Station on his return, and she said to him, "You are not going to stay in my house until you have proved yourself to be Sergeant Dandy." Inspector Thomas came along, and witness suggested to the prisoner that he should either give himself up or go back to his regi- ment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>DEFENCE.</head>
        <p>THE DEFENCE.</p>
        <p>Prisoner And A Former Husband And Wife Trouble.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. Sandbach, who ap- peared for the defence, witness said she and her husband got on well together. There was once a little trouble about ahother woman with whom she saw him. "I pulled her hat off in the street."</p>
        <p>The witness said the prisoner told her about this incident, and that made her more con- vinced than ever that the prisoner was her, husband.</p>
        <p>Replying to the Judge, Mr. Sandbach said he was not going to suggest that the prisoner was the sergeant. Mr. Sandbach : Did you tell the prisoner that your husband treated yOu badly?—No; my hus- band worked six nights a week and was never at home.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sandbach : And you had cause for com- plaining that your husband was neglecting you? —I said I expect you will be different now you have come home:</p>
        <p>In answer to the Judge, the witness said the prisoner resembled her husband very much.</p>
        <p>Wife Breaks Down.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sandbach was proceeding to ask further questions as to the treatment Mrs. Dandy re- ceived at the hands of her husband, when the witness broke down, and sobbing hysterically, said: "It is a shame that this trouble should come upon me—an innocent woman. I thought. too much about him to think of anyone else. I have never known wrong in my life. I never thought of a man coming to me and saying he was my husband. Only to think that my poor husband is laid low! . . . I have not done any work since this man came to my house."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>OTHER WITNESSES.</head>
        <p>OTHER WITNESSES.</p>
        <p>William Dutton, a retired mechanic, informed the Court that it was he who first spoke to the prisoner in Clowes.43treet. The prisoner said: "Well, how is Master Dutton getting along? Still working, I see." Hall was leaning heavily upon a stick and dragging one leg behind him. He said: "It's Herbert of course. you know me. I am not supposed to be out." The wit- ner3s helped him into the shop, and he scrambled behind the counter.</p>
        <p>The Judge: Did you believe he was Sergeant Dandy?—l did. Edith Watson, Sergeant Dandy's sister, also gave evidence that when she saw the prisoner</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <p>Continued ill next co!umn.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
