      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</p>
        <p>- • 0 1 T 7 ---. , • 4. s, 1 1 ...., I v . - 4-, ~..,. - - t „ $11.• 11 •. . ._ - T -" IC T . .... _,...,7 31l EW _. : ; i N,... ! • kt: . .... - 'l.._. • -.1 - -',...</p>
        <p>LAYMIVI</p>
        <p>New Member of Education Committee.—Mr. T. W. F. Dixon, of "The Clifflands," Lymm, has been appointed to fill the vacancy on the local Education Committee caused by the resig- nation of Mr. H.'S. Saville.</p>
        <p>Lymm Recruiting Committee.—lt is pleasing to record that during the week there has been a decided fillip, quite a large number of recruits presenting themselves to go through the pre- liminary medical tests. Many expressed their willingness to enlist at once, whilst others wished to be placed in the Reserves.</p>
        <p>"Our Day" Proceeds: Substantial Amount Realised.—The Working Men's Committee of the British Red Crogs Society (Lymm Detach- ment) have this week forwarded a cheque valued £34 18s. Id. to Mrs. Watkin, being the net profits in the recent effort in aid of "Our Day." The committee and other helpers are to be congratulated in raising such a handsome total for this noble cause. .</p>
        <p>Corporal Ben Elliott Convalescent—Mr. and Mrs. S. Elliott, of Canterbury Cottage, .have this week received the news that their son— CorPoral Ben •Elliott, of the 2nd Scots Guards— has been transferred from the 2nd Southern Hospital, Southmead, Bristol, to the Beach Convalescent Home, Bitton, Glos., owing to him Making such rapid progress towards recov- ery. Corporal Elliott expects to be fully well again in a short" time and back in the firing- line.</p>
        <p>Concert for Red Cross Society.-7-An entertain- ment, which promises to be highly successful, judging from the programme, is to be given on Wednesday next in the Drill Hall, in aid of the- Lymm Detachment of the 8.R.C.5., the premier attraction being "The Gay Cadets" in their "Querieland" entertainment, produced by Mr. George Stubbs. Tickets may be obtained from Mr. A. H. Hudson, The Nook, Lymm, or from any member of the Working Men's Com- mittee. The list of artistes and other particu- lars will be found on another page.</p>
        <p>Sapper Joseph Clare.—Mrs. M. E. Clare, of Booth's Hill, Lymm, has this week received the news that her son—Sapper Joseph Clare, of the 3rd Section, Royal Engineers—has landed in England and is at present lying in hospital on the outskirts of Manchester, where he is mak- ing satisfactory progress towards recovery. It will be remembered that he was wounded by a piece of trench mortar, which went through his mouth and lodged in his cheek. His mother visiting him on Thursday last, found him in the best of health and in no way much worse from his recent thrilling experiences.</p>
        <p>had 6s. war bonus every week: In reply to the Clerk the applicant said that her income from her father, brother and lodger—was £2 13s. 6d. In addition to this, she had Bs. 6d. a week from her husband.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel said that the secret of this applioa- tion was that the defendant had recently been left £2O by an aunt, and the wife was deter- mined to get some of that. So far as the defendant was concerned he intended to keep the £2O.</p>
        <p>In the box, the defendant said that his average earnings were 31s. a week.</p>
        <p>After-- retiring for about five minutes, the Bench decided to increase the allowance to 10s. a week.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>PRIVATE ARTHUR THORNICROFT.</head>
        <p>PRIVATE ARTHUR THORNICROFT.</p>
        <p>Arrives In A Newmarket Hospital.</p>
        <p>Mr. and Mrs. Edwin Thornicroft, of Clay- terrace, have received the news that their son, Private Arthur Thornicroft, 10th Battalion, Cheshire Regiment, has landed in England and is at present an inmate of the Cheveley Park Hospital, Newmarket, Cambs. The hospital has been opened on the estate of the late Col. Harry McCalmont, M.P., the owner of some of the most famous racehorses of the last gen- eration, including Isinglass, the sensational Derby winner. Private Thornicroft was slightly wounded in the forearm on November 4th, but is now making satisfactory pPogress. He hopes soon to be able to visit his parents at home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>INCREASED ALIMONY.</head>
        <p>INCREASED ALIMONY.</p>
        <p>Wife's Successful Application To Magistrates.</p>
        <p>Florence Evelyn Williamson, of Gilberts'- terrace, Hall-lane, Partington, applied to the Bench at Tuesday's Police Court for the ~ariat- ion of an order made on July 15th, 1911. whereby her husband, Henry Williamson, bad to pay her Bs. 6d. a week. The application AN as made on the grounds of increased income. Mr. T. S. Steel represented the defendant.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Williamson Stated that at the time the order was made, the defendant's wage was 225. 6d. a week. It was now £1 9s. Gd. and he</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>DANDY MYSTERY SOLVED.—Continued.</head>
        <p>DANDY MYSTERY SOLVED.—Continued.</p>
        <p>he said, "Oh, Edith, I ani glad to see you."</p>
        <p>SAW SERGEANT DANDY IN EGYPT.</p>
        <p>Sergeant John Edwin Hursthouse, of the Bth Manchester Regiment, said he saw Sergeant Dandy on May 3, when Dandy was leaving Alexandria for the Dardanelles. The prisoner was not Sergeant Dandy, though there was a good resemblance between the two men. The</p>
        <p>prisoner was a shade shorter and his features were bigger.</p>
        <p>PRISONER'S WIFE AND SIX CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The wife of the prisoner, Ada Hall, told the Court that she was married to the accused in Lincolnshire, and there had been six children.</p>
        <p>Mr. Broeklehurst : In what year were you married?</p>
        <p>The witness could not remember, and caused much amusement by saying "Ask him" (the prisoner) "he is there." It transpired that they were married in 1899. Her husband, she added, enlisted in the 19th Battalion Salford Fusiliers, and she had received 10s. a week as separation allowance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>"MEMORY A BLANK."</head>
        <p>"MEMORY A BLANK."</p>
        <p>A remarkable story was told by Inspector Thomas, who stated that when he saw the pris- oner at Exchange Station, he asked him to ex- plain hi g- movements for a time before he saw Mrs. Dandy. Hall said: "My memory is a blank during that period." The prisoner ap- pealed to Mrs. Dandy, saying, "You are not going to have me locked up?" She answered that she was not. Thereupon the officer said, "It is not now in your hands." The witness asked him certain questions about his marriage, all of which he answered. He said he was married to Mrs. Dandy at Latchford, and that a child was born and christened there.. The witness said he was satisfied, after trying to find an abscess mark on the prisoner's neck, that. Hall was not Dandy, and he said he would lock him up. To this the prisoner remarked, "It is a fine thing for a man who has bled and fought for his country to be locked up."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>PRISONER NOT CALLED.</head>
        <p>PRISONER NOT CALLED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Sandbach did not call any witnesses for the defence, nor did he put the .lefendant into the witness-box. The question at issue, he said, was not whether the defendant had been guilty of a breach of morals, but wheth..r Mrs Dandy really believed at the time that he was her husband. Was the story told by Mrs. Dandy a possible story? She was married in 1905, and her husband had only .been away a year, and yet she mistook defendant for her husband. She could have found out the truth in five minutes if she had asked a few simple questions. There was no evidence that the defendant and Sergt. Dandy had ever met, and he submitted that Mrs. Dandy's story was such that no sensible man could believe.</p>
        <p>The Judge pointed out that it was certain the defendant must have been informed of his likeness to Sergeant Dandy. How did he find his way to the shop in Clowes-street? It was also evident that the defendant was a man of some resource. The real question was whether the wife was deceived. She appeared to be a respectable, industrious and hard-working woman.</p>
        <p>THREE YEARS' PENAL SERVITUDE. The jury, after five minutes' consideration, brought in a verdict of guilty. The Judge, in passing sentence, said: This is a most impudent crime. The Defendant: I was led into it. The Judge: You by some means or other we don't know-</p>
        <p>The Defendant: It was in a public-house--- The Judge: You got to know that you bore a strong resemblance to Sergeant Dandy, and, availing yourself of that information, you de- liberately perpetrated this crime. You must go to penal servitude for three years.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>THEFTS FROM RED CROSS HOSPITAL.</head>
        <p>THEFTS FROM RED CROSS HOSPITAL.</p>
        <p>Two Women Fined For "Most Wicked Thing." .At the Lymm Petty Sessions on Tuesday, be- fore Messrs. S. W. Gould (presiding), S. Daviet. and H. Holden, two women were summoned for stealing various articles from the Red Cros.., Hospital. In. the first case the thefts were of one bath towel, one tablecloth, one trayclott, and one roll of boric-lint, valued at 75.; in the other it. was stealing one roll of cotton-woo and one, roll of boric-lint, valued at 3s: 6d., from the same hospital.</p>
        <p>Some of the property was produced, and was identified by Miss Coleburn, the matron at the hospital.</p>
        <p>Sergeant Davies, who had charge of the case, visited the women accused, who admitted the offence.</p>
        <p>Mr. Giles Hunt, on behalf of the 'Red Cross Hospital Committee, said that the committee did not wish to press the matter. Addressing the defendants, the Chairman of the Bench (Mr. S. W. Gould) said that the magistrates felt that they could not deal with this case without a fine. The offence was most serious. The Red Cross Hospital was carried on by voluntary effort, and it was most wicked to take away property that had been given by the people of the district for the alleviation of the sufferings of wounded soldiers. Even taking into consideration the fact that the committee did not wish to press the matter, the Bench con- sidered that they were acting very leniently by imposing a fine of 10s. upon each defendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>DEATH OF A PROMINENT WARBURTON FARMER.</head>
        <p>DEATH OF A PROMINENT WARBURTON FARMER.</p>
        <p>The death occurred on Monday of Mr. John R. Newton, of "The Bent," Warburton, after an illness extending over a period of six months. He had been attended for, some time past by Dr. H. Thorp and two specialists, but the patient passed away as stated. The late Mr. J. R. Newton was a prominent farmer of War- burton, having an extensive connection, and was of a kindly gild affectionate disposition, being esteemed by a large number of intimate friends, both in his immediate vicinity and the surrounding districts. He was a member of the Lymm Education Committee, where his services in the future will be greatly missed. He was also assistant over- seer for Heatley and Warburton, where he carried out his duties with honourable satis- faction in this particular sphere of work. His political views were Conservative, albeit taking more than ordinary interest in the 'doings of both parties, unprejudiced so long as it was for the welfare of his country. Agricultural shows he was particularly fond of, and never missed attending the shows in the district. He was also a member of the Lymm Recruiting Committee, and lent himself whole heartedly to this cause. The deceased gentleman was 59 years of age. . As a tribute to his memory the flag of the Con- servative Club at Lymm flew at half-mast. The interment took place on Thursday at Warburton Church, in the presence of a large number of sorrowing relatives and friends. By request, there were no floral tributes, with the exception of a handsome wreath from his own family. The Rev. G. Egerton-Warburton administered the last rites, and the funeral arrangements were in the hands of Messrs. Clough and Sons, of Sale. The Lymm Education Committee wore repre- sented by: 'Messrs. S. W. Gould (vice-chairman). Mullard (clerk to committee), R. S. Gotobed, S. Davies and W. G. Groves; as well as Mr. H. S. Mercer, school attendance officer, and Mr. G. A. Cook, clerk to the Old Age Pension Com- mittee.</p>
        <p>A large number of farmers from the sur- rounding districts witnessed the burial, who felt keenly the loss they had sustained by the deceased gentlem'an's death.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>" Bucking Up."—At the Runcorn Board of Guardians on Tuesday, Mr. James Pearson, the Stockton Heath relieving officer, said that he had nothing to report. This statement was received with applause, and the Chairman (Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun.), observed that Stock- ton Heath was "bucking up."</p>
        <p>Whist Drive At The Adult School.—The mem- bers of the Stockton Heath Adult School held a whist drive in their rooms last Saturday even- ing. There was a good attendance of players. and the prizes were won by the following : Ladies: 1, Mrs. Mason; 2, Mrs. Lynch; gentle- men: 1, Mr. Isherwood; 2, Mr. G. Mann.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>WESLEYAN MISSIONS.</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN MISSIONS.</p>
        <p>Local Effort For Evangelising Foreign</p>
        <p>Lands.</p>
        <p>The Wesleyan Church Foreign Missions Anni- versary was held on Sunday. The special preachers for the day were Messrs. W. H. Kneen (morning) and J. Upson (evening). At the evening service a solo was rendered by Mr. Hetherington. A public meeting was held on Monday even- ing, when there were a good number present. Mr. P. Hickman was in the chair, and an eloquent address on the mission work in India was given by the Rev. T. M. Kerruish. , -The annual report of Miss Turner, the local secretary, was as follows: "I am pleased to tell you that Stockton Heath has made one or two advances during the past year. • They are certainly small, but we intend to work for a greater advance during the coming year. The Wesley Guild is our greatest help, for all our collectors are 'Guilders.' Besides the ordinary missionary work, the Guild has taken up the "Leagues of Ten." Two Leagues have been formed, one by Mrs. Cliffs and one by Miss L. Brooks. Another hopeful sign is that we have in circulation 28 copies of the 'Foreign Field,' an increase of six on last year. I am sorry to say that the collections at the services were down a little yesterday, but our box-holders have been more diligent than in previous years, so that we have an increase of £1 12s. 2d. proving, I think, that, we are not quite so dead as many people think. Someone heard the Rev. Percy Ineson say the other day that he was going to 'make things hum' at Stockton Heath. If that is so, we intend to ' hum ' also." The balance sheet set forth the following items :—Sunday's collections, 4:2 6s. 51d.; Sunday school collec- tion, Bs. 64d.; subscription from Mr. and Mrs. Bishop, £2 25.; from Mr. Kneen, 65.; from boxes, £6 12s. 6d.; from Dr. •Stephen's Medical Missions, £1; from collection taken at the meet- ing, £5 19s. 34d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>MILK FLOAT AND CYCLE.</head>
        <p>MILK FLOAT AND CYCLE.</p>
        <p>An Unsuccessful Claim For Damages.</p>
        <p>A claim for £2 15s. damages was made at the Warrington County Court on Thursday by Henry Cotterall Revile, of 19, London-road, Stockton Heath, against James Whitlow, farmer, of Garland Hall, Crowley.—The :le-</p>
        <p>fendant was represented by Mr. R. Ridgway Bennett (instructed by Messrs. Wood and Lord, Manchester). The plaintiff said that on Thursday, June 211 h, at 8.45 a.m., he was .wheeling his bicycle acrcss the road from his apartments at 19, I.ondon-road, Stockton Heath, when acci- dentally fell with his machine: A milk-float was pp roach in g from the opposite •firl,mot. It was driven by a boy, who, though he had every opportunity of stopping his hone drove straight over the length of the..ccle. The boy was asked for his name and addies.4, but refused to give either, and drove away. On Jlily f.`9th, the plaintiff was crosSing the Northwich-roac. Swing Bridge when he saw the boy in the milk- float The boy recognised him, began to flog his horse and galloped away. Evidence supporting this stateineut was given by Mrs. Reville, the plaintiff's wife. Edwin Baines, of Arley, Northwich, the boy .who had charge of the milk-float, maintained that he had no time to stop his horse alter the cycle fell over. Judgment, with costs, was given in favour of the defendant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
        <head>CIIESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</head>
        <p>CIIESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT.</p>
        <p>Stockton Heath Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for week commencing 22nd November: Monday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45. Friday.—Drill at Headquarters, 7.45. The Detachment is open to men of military av who have been attested, and to youths unOer military age desirous of obtaining prPliminary instruction in drill, &amp;c. Application is cordi- ally invited by the Officer Commanding on either of the above nights. By Order, F. WARBURTON. Officer Commanding.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0200" n="art0200">
        <head>GRAPPENHALL.</head>
        <p>GRAPPENHALL.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE VOLUNTEER REGIMENT</p>
        <p>Grappenhall Detachment.</p>
        <p>Orders for the week commencing November 21st, 1915 :</p>
        <p>Tuesday, 23rd November.—Squad drill, mus- ketry; fall ,in at headquarters at 7.40 p.m. sharp. Saturday, 27th November.—Company drill (in uniform); fall in at headquarters at 3.25 p.m. sharp. Orderlies.—Section-Commander C. F. Read, Private 0. Lofthouse. NOTE.—A list of the men selected for each visit to the range for musketry will be posted weekly at Headquarters. Should any man find that the date for which he is selected is incon- venient he must immediately inform his Sec- tion-Commander.</p>
        <p>By order, P. A. FROST, O.C. (Platoon Commander). Headquarters, 14th Nov., 1915.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0201" n="art0201">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Still They Come.—The latest recruits to the great cause are Joseph Scrutton and Charles Midwood, R.A.M.C. Grouped and returned to employment until called up, Ernest Foster, Ernest Glover, John Tunstall, Leonard 'Fowles, Harry Goulden, Harry Jones, Marshall Whit- field, and James Ainsworth.</p>
        <p>Canvassers Wanted.—Canvassers under Lord Derby's recruiting scheme are wanted for the Penketh and Cuerdley districts, and volunteers are requested to communicate with the hon. secretary, Mr. Griffiths, Council School, Pen- keth. Volunteers for canvassing should be above the military age, or if under, should have been rejected from military service.</p>
        <p>Co-operative Concert.—A grand concert was given on Thursday evening in the Wesleyan Schoolroom, under the auspices of the War- rington Co-operative Society's Educational Com- mittee. The artistes, who are well known and just as keenly appreciated wherever they go— Miss Elsie Hewitt, Madame Lily Fairhurst, Mr. W. Rigby and Mr. W. W. Whittle (humorist)— entertained a well-packed room to a series of songs, duets and recitations, to their great de- light. During the interval the Chairman, Mr. George Faulkner, spoke a few earnest words on co-operation and the great benefits attached to it, both from the workers' and members' stand- point.</p>
        <p>Wonderful Missionary Story.—On Wednesday evening in the Wesleyan Chapel the annual missionary *meting was held, when the Rev. 0. Griffin, late district superintendent of Lagos, West Africa, told a wonderful story of mission- ary work and enterprise during the eighteen years he sojourned there. One thing he espe- cially impressed upon all temperance workers was to put the utmost pressure upon the Gov- ernment to prohibit the exportation of spirits, chiefly "trade gin," to the natives of that region. Speaking from personal experience, the lecturer said that the Yorubas were the most honest people on earth, for a native, wishing to dispose of any produce or goods, needed only to place them alongside the , highway, with price 'attached, to find the full value, on return- ing, lying where the goods had originally lain. A series of lantern slides gave the finishing touch to a master's hand at description. Mrs. Wheatley worthily fulfilled her mission in the chair.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0202" n="art0202">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>Bishop And Dead Soldier's Relatives.—At Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians it was announced that Bishop Chavasse, of Liverpool, had kindly consented to visit the Workhouse to-day and administer the Sacrament of Confirmation to two hospital patients. "Aye," remarked Mr. Mather, "Bishop Chavasse is one of the most thought- ful men in the Chu' ch. When he was in Padgate recently his Lordship heard that a local man had been killed in action in France, and that the news had been received that morning. The Bishop went down to the house and tried to console the sorrowing relatives. We ought to feel both obliged to and proud of a Bishop like that." (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Missionary Week.---The annual missiouary SENT ViCC6 were held on Sunday. the morning preacher being the Rev. A. J. Morten, and the</p>
        <p>best judges of the use to which the lists may be put. The matter has been placed before the Local Government Board for decision."</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Mather: It says very distinctly that the overseers are going to use their.own discre- tion as to whether they publish the list or not. Mr. J. Billinge: We have local knowledge; we know beet</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0203" n="art0203">
        <p>evening preacher the Rev. 11. T. Dixon. In the afternoon there was a short address to the chil- dren on missionary work in China, the speaker showing many interesting curios representing gods, &amp;c., which are worshipped by he heathen in that vast empire. Mr. J. L. Fryer occupied the chair. On Wednesday Mr. W. J. Bate gave a short paper on missionary work in India to the members of the Junior Guild. On Thursday evening the Rev. Oliver J. Griffin, chairman of the Lagos District,W. Africa, lectured to a large audience. He gave some idea of the distances and time it takes to travel round the variots districts, and also the vast numbers of people they have to deal with. Missionaries were in- adequate. There were over 40,000,000 people in the interior of Africa who have not yet heard the Gospel of Christ. Ile spoke of the intense religious feeling of the natives„and stated that their honesty was most pronounced. He also said a great deal about the customs, habits and beliefs of the people. He wound up with several anecdotes showing the necessity of supporting missionary work. Alderman Bennett occupied the chair, and ' the Rev. A. J. Morton read the report, which showed an amount of over £36, which had been raised during the year for missionary work by the Wesley Church at Padgate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0204" n="art0204">
        <head>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Ratepayer And War-time Economy On Roads.</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Antwis presided over a meeting of the Runcorn Rural District Council, held on Tuesday.</p>
        <p>WILL ROADS" DO" UNTIL END 0/WAR?</p>
        <p>The Clerk (Mr. W. E. Hough) read the following letter from Mr. R. Shepherd, of Park- side, Ashton, Preston Brook:—" In the interests of the nation I think the time has arrived when you, as a public body, should discontinue the making of any more new road improvements during the period of the war. The men so employed are urgently needed to assist in the country's wants in producing food and war munitions, which are necessary to shorten and win the war. The outlay of the money and the material saved will, by ceasing non-produc- tive work, be a great national asset in assist- ing the Government to finance the war; more particularly if that lead is followed through- out the country. May I plead to you to set the example? Your roads are in good repair and will stand over until the end of the war— and after, if necessary. In addition to the new work-gang you can release other workmen. It is only necessary to attend to the most urgent work, such as keeping the water courses and catch-pits in order; sanding gradients in frosty ,weather, &amp;c. Lead the way; the nation wants your help and courage!" The matter was referred to the Highways Executive Committee.</p>
        <p>THE STEAM ROLLER-AND THE WAR!</p>
        <p>The Council resolved , that the Executive Highway Committee should be asked to con- sider the advisability of suspending the work of the road roller until the late spring of 1916; that the roller be stored for that period; that the men now attached thereto be allowed to offer themselves at works making munitions of war, and that such men be reinstated when the roller is again used.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0205" n="art0205">
        <head>AUTUMN EPIDEMICS.</head>
        <p>AUTUMN EPIDEMICS.</p>
        <p>Referring to various epidemics in the dis- trict, Mr. Woodward pointed out that the Council's hospital had now 42 patients in it; thirty-four of whom are suffering from scarlet fever and the remainder from diphtheria.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0206" n="art0206">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS.</p>
        <p>War Will Not Rob Poor Of Christmas Cheer.</p>
        <p>A meeting of the Runcorn Board of Guardians was held on Tuesday at the Dutton Workhouse, Mr. C. E. Linaker, jun., presiding.</p>
        <p>OLD AGE PENSIONERS " HIT " BY THE WAR.</p>
        <p>Mr. G. W. Yates, the hon. secretary of the Runcorn Civil Distress Seruting Committee, wrote that at a recent meeting of his committee, it was proposed that the Guardians be approached on behalf of the old age pensioners in the district, with a view to getting the pen- sions supplemented. Several cases had been brought before the committee, who submitted them to the authority who distributes the Prince of Wales' Fund, but it appeared that they were Powerless to grant relief. This class seemed to be the most affected by the present conditions and the cost of living made it very 'difficult for some of them to make ends meet.</p>
        <p>Nhe Clerk (Mr. W. E. Haugh) said that this letter suggested that the old age pensioners should be helped out of the rates. Specific cases should be reported to the Guardians. He also pointed out that if people were granted poor relief they would be disqualified as old age pensioners.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0207" n="art0207">
        <head>CHRISTMAS "AS USUAL."</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS "AS USUAL."</p>
        <p>The House Committee rec.°. memnded that the usual Christmas a extras " should be allowed to the inmates and the staff. The committee also desired to place on record their apprecia- tion of the six years' service performed by Mr. Parsons as house master. Mr. Parsons has joined the Army.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0208" n="art0208">
        <head>Our Policeman.</head>
        <p>Our Policeman.</p>
        <p>Lady (to policeman on duty): "Oh, have you seen the Zeppelin? Which way did it go?" Policeman (in best official manner): "Up the street opposite, Madam, and first turning to the left."—" Punch."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0209" n="art0209">
        <p>" PARISH RELlEF—Continued.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0210" n="art0210">
        <head>"PARISH RELIEF,"</head>
        <p>"PARISH RELIEF,"</p>
        <p>Should Names Of Recipients Be</p>
        <p>Disclosed ?</p>
        <p>GUARDIANS v. OVERSEERS.</p>
        <p>At Wednesday's meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians the Rev. A. M. Mitchell proposed that the resolution passed at a pre- vious meeting to refuse to supply a list- of per- sons in receipt of parochial relief to the over- seers of the township of Newton-in-Makerfield; be rescinded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0211" n="art0211">
        <head>WAS IT A MISAPPREHENSION?</head>
        <p>WAS IT A MISAPPREHENSION?</p>
        <p>The reverend gentleman thought that the re- fusal had been made under a misapprehension— a misapprehension that the list was to be made, more or less, public property. The overseers, lie contended, had a perfect right to know how every penny of the money raised in their re- spective areas had been' expended. It did not suffice that they should merely know the gross amount; they ought to be in a position to give to those to whom they were responsible the details.. In a word, the overseers were respon- sible for the raising of the money, and they had an unalienable right to know how much of such money had been disbursed under the direction of the Board of Guardians. *lt had been decided by the Board that the overseers had no right to the information in question, and he submitted that that was quite a wrong inter- pretation of the law on the matter. The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley): If you chal- lenge my ruling, that I shall not allow ! If you are making the statement as an exposition of a point of law, you are quite wrong. As an individual, of course, you are entitled to an opinion, but I would point out that the matter is the subject of an appeal to the Local Govern- ment Board. If they decide against me, there's an end to the matter. But so long as the matter .rests in the stage which it has now reached, my ruling holds 'good.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0212" n="art0212">
        <head>"A WICKED OLD CUSTO?A."</head>
        <p>"A WICKED OLD CUSTO?A."</p>
        <p>• The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I must enter my protest against the expression which fell from your lips a fortnight ago, and which I very much regretted. It was to the effect that the action_of Mr. Billinge, and myself in saiTorting the Newton-in-Makerfield overseers in their ap- plication meant that we were to have the re- sumption of a wicked old custom. I think those were your words. The Chairman: Yes.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell: I admire 14ain. strong, direct language, but I think that in this case it was out of place. We none of us want a resumption of that which you so graphically described as a wicked old:cuStom. But I should like to point- out that we had no idea in our minds that the names of the people who receive relief should be published, or exhibited in such a position that they could be read by every- body attending church or chapel. The reverend gentleman added that it was laid down by regulation that the lists asked for in this case were to be supplied, not for exhibition in church porches, but supplied to the overseers. "who shall lay the same before the next vestry meeting . . . and preserve the same with the other parish papers." If they presumed to exhibit the list in the church porches and to resort to that "wicked old custom" they would be acting in a manner that was ultra vires. and could be " called over the coals," and sharply rapped over the knuckles by the Local Government Board. Mr. E. L. Bent seconded. He thought that the overseers were entitled to be supplied with the information, to the satisfaction not only of the vestry meeting, but also of other people.</p>
        <p>The Chairman :That's the whole point—" of other people."</p>
        <p>WHY THE INFORMATION IS NEEDED.</p>
        <p>Mr. Bent: We overseers are appointed by the local council, and when they ask us certain questions we find that we cannot answer them, because this information that we seek is with- held. It is for their own private- use and in- formation that the overseers want the list, and I contend that they are perfectly entitled to it. Mr. J. Billinge:failed to see how the Board could, so to speak, revoke an order of the Local Government Board. The Union accounts were, every half-year, open to • inspection by rate- payers, and it was quite within the province of " that august body to which. Mr. Bent belonged —the Newton overseers—and that little body at Ha.ydock " to send some ratepayer down to live on the clerk's premises for three days and ex- amine every page of the entire accounts. (Laughter.) _</p>
        <p>Mr. G. Mather: Won't that suggestion solv.a the problem? Mr. J. Billinge : If that should fail they have other facilities. The Chairman: What is asked for now is that the previous resolution should be rescinded. Mr. G. Mather: I say, appeal to Ctesar, anti let Ctesar give his decision: (Laughter.) The Chairman: The Board gave their deci- sion at the last meeting, and now they are being invited to go back on it. ' The Rev. A. M. Mitchell's proposal was de- feated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0213" n="art0213">
        <head>OVERSEERS' VIEW.</head>
        <p>THE OVERSEERS' VIEW.</p>
        <p>Later on in the meeting the Clerk (Mr. A. Bottomley) read a letter from the clerk to the overseers in question, in the course of which the following phrase occurred: "My overseers submit, with great respect, that they are the</p>
