      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0220" n="art0220">
        <head>•. • N 3S WORL I. ~••</head>
        <p>•. • N 3S WORL I. ~••</p>
        <p>intimate acquaintance with the social life and condition of the people.</p>
        <p>The Lady Mayoress of London. The new Lady Mayoress of London takes the keenest interest in all that concerns the British soldier, When war 'broke out Sir Charles and Lady Wakefield offered their sea- side home at Hythe to the Government, locating themselves in the gardener's cottage near at hand in order that they might take a personal interest in the sojourners under their hospit- able roof. Lady Wakefield is a great traveller. She has twice been round the world, and knows almost every country of Europe. She and Sir Charles were. in Japan when that country was at war with China, and again 'during the Russian war. Lady Wakefield is an adept with her needle, and is particularly interested in ell charitable Works; but more especially in those that concern the babies of to-day. Both hus- band and wife have (lone much for the Rel CrosS and for hospitals, and Sir Charles is president of the City of London League of Mercy.</p>
        <p>Spanish for German. After the Franco-Prussian war of 1670-71, the French people determined to study the German language, in order better to prepare themselves for the " ravanche," which it is to be hoped they will .soon attain. French families sent their sons and daughters to Germany to study the people and their unwieldly tongue; perhaps we should do well to follow their example by continuing to study. .German, in order to beat them in the Coining commercial war. The choice of Spanish in the place of German is, nevertheless a good one, since that language is one of the finest of the Latin tongues. Charles V. spoke German to his dogs, French to his friends, Italian to his wife, and Spanish to his God. Winter Coats and Skirts.</p>
        <p>The newest fashions show a distinct tendency to flare much less than during the summer. There is in fact, a certain straightness in line, which may be noted on coats as well as on dresses. The latter are still full, but there is no bunchiness; all hang straight from the waist. Coats may in some instances flute over the hips, especially towards the back, but as a general rule they follow the lines of skirts. Winter coats and skirts are particularly neat, they are also practical and.sensible, neither too short nor too wide. Some of the new models in frieze and</p>
        <p>serge, suggest a military severity and stiffness in their cut and lack of adornment. The coats are rather short, quite straight, and button to the chin. They are finished with small collars and cuffs of fur. Russian styles are most suitably trimmed with astrakan fur, which on a grey material is most attractive. The buttons also are covered with the same pelt and are very large. Black astrakan -fornks a most suitable trimming for navy serge, whether used for a simple.frock or a tailored suit. ' This Season's Overall.</p>
        <p>The overall has quietly and gradually made a place for itself in the retail stores and •shops. For some years it was a negligible garment, by no means attractive, as designers and maim- facturers alike paid little heed to it. The war has, however,, brought this useful article into prominence, its utility and convenience as a protective garment earning for it. a well- deserved popularity. Taste and ingenuity have lately been brought into operation, with the result that the models prepared for this season's wear may well be Mistaken for the ,daintiest of morning gowns. They are fashioned on the lines of the new coats, and coat frocks, are belted at the waist; and adorned with capaciou3 pockets of many shapes which are 'variously trimmed. There is little of the pinafore about them, and they all fasten in front. All have long sleeves finished with a closely fitting cuff, protecting completely the dress beneath. Some have detachable collars of silk ,or velvet, and the buttons are invariably more or. less orna- mental. Dresses and Blouses Fasten Behind.</p>
        <p>The Princess frock finds great favour in Paris as well as in London. It is made quite wide at the hem, but fits smoothly over the hips. Magyar or Raglan sleeves are generally pre- ferred for this style of dress, but some models show puffs at the shoulders, and under these are sleeves of lace or chiffon. • Belts, often very elaborate, are worn with these dresses, and it of suede are usually beautifully embroidered, and the buckles are embossed. Sashes that cover _the hips tightly and lend a slenderness to the figure, often take the place of the 6elt. It is said that in order to ensure a: perfect fit, the blouse or dress with which a high vollar is worn, must fasten behind. This is indeed un- fortunate, since in these busy times .inly the simplest and easiest of fastenings are advisable. Princess- dresses, however, so far fasten in front, as do the greater number of blouses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0221" n="art0221">
        <head>CHAT and GOSSIP.</head>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>War Changes.</p>
        <p>London is normally a world containing many worlds, but the war has had a considerable levelling influence on its social and domestic</p>
        <p>life. At first the Metropolis .took the war almost lightly, but the awakening came with the Zeppelin raids, the stringent regulations as to darkened streets, and the present and final recruiting;campaign,• all of which are palpable and unavoidable evidences of the seriousness of the war. City and West End workers are anxious to get to their homes in the suburbs early in the evening. London 'managers scarce7y know how to meet the situation. Matinees are the rule, and at some theatres the evening per- formance commences iat ' 5.30. Some of the Christmas pantomimes will be given at noon and 4.30, thus reversing the old order Of things —dinner first and theatre afterwards. Many of</p>
        <p>the professional and well-to-do families • are, however, giving up late dinner, and substituting a light repast that involves little if any special cooking. Early hours, servant difficulties, and the need for economy,. are all having a marked influence on social life in London. •</p>
        <p>• The Emperor of Japan. ' The education of the Emperor and, Empress of japan, who have recentlY celebrated their coronation, was conducted on totally different lines from that of their predecessors. The Mikados are considered to hays() a divine origin, and hitherto have been educated in splendid isolation from their subjects, but the late Emperor placed his heir and only surviving son in the Peer's School. lie was given a separate palace, provided with special facilities for exer- cise, and for leading an-ppen-air life. Military subjects attracted him from his earliest years, and he is an expert in all matters relating to war. He is neither an Oriental nor an Occidental, but a little of both, for he was born :and brought up in the transition period of Japan's history. The Emperor is a democrat, and his marriage strengthened his tendencies in that direction, as the Empress Sadoka has an</p>
      </div>
