        <p>*I) +11) 4:4 •:"I:"X4+ .4•••) +444.0:44......4.11446.444...44.4*..........41:•++.41.4) o+44+ 4)**4.******•:•*•::**.:•**4* *oo4,oo*.llo4..te.)**oo4o.4".o*****o.*** 004:600.0**4***-2•44. 44+ 4.4) it ii</p>
        <p>Mothers of England! Be proud of your sons As they gO forth to ,fight; Give them a cheer, loud let it ring, For they go forth to defend the right. Mothers of England! Be proud of your sons As forth to the war they go; They are not cowards, but Britishers true, Each one determined to conquer the foe.</p>
        <p>Mothers of England Weep not for your sons Who go. the honour of England to keep, Though the parting be hard, and your hearts . be sore, Do not weep, dear mothers, for them do not weep. God gave His dear Son for you, and you . Your sons must freely give . For England's honour and glory, And that we at home in peace may live.</p>
        <p>Mothers of England! Pray for your sons, And for all who khaki wear, That God will bless and protect them: Make this your daily prayer. They need your prayers, dear mothers, As they fight on the battle field. And will you not pray daily That God will them from danger shield?</p>
        <p>Mothers of England! Be proud of your sons, Be proud of your soldier sons. Mothers of England! Weep not for your sons Weep not for those behind the guns. Mothers of England! Pray for your sons; Pray for them to God on high; • And hurrah ! for the British soldier, Who goes to coll.:fuer:or die! I;.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0224" n="art0224">
        <head>IR-TINIE FOOD PRICES.</head>
        <p>IR-TINIE FOOD PRICES.</p>
        <p>41 Per Cent. For A AVorit</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>Retail prices of food On November 1 (states the Board of Trade "Labour Gazette") were higher than a month earlier by less than 1 per cent. Taking the country as a whole and mak- ing allowances for the relative importance of the various articles in working-class household. expenditure, the average increases in the retail prices of food since the beginning of the war may be put at 41 .per cent. This figure relates to food only, and in estimating the Increased cost of living, this increase must not be applied to the total family expendittfre but only to that proportion which is expended on feed. Taking the prices in Berlin in July, 1914—the last returns before the 'commencement of the war—as a basis, the advance in retail food prices in September, 1915, was over 78 per cent,. as against 75 per cent. in the preceding month. In Vienna it is calculated that in the aggre- gate food prices in. August, 1915, were about 4 per cent. 'higher than in the preceding Month and about 86 per cent. higher than in July, 1914. The general level of food prices in Vienna would appear to have been about 88 per cent. higher in September, 1915, than in September,</p>
        <p>Her Conclusion. "I suppose you consider my hauteur mere affection," began Miss Parvenue. "Not at all," Miss Bright hastened to say. " Your scornful expression is quite natural." " Yes, I suppose you lived for a great 'many years in the neighbourhood of your father's glue factory."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0225" n="art0225">
