      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0226" n="art0226">
        <head>WORKHOUSE CHRISTMAS.</head>
        <p>WORKHOUSE CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>Modified Bill Of Fare For</p>
        <p>Inmates.</p>
        <p>THE " BEER QUESTION."</p>
        <p>The sort of war-time Christmas fare to be given to the inmates of the Workhouse was the subject of lengthy discussion at the fqrtnightly meeting of the Warrington Board of Guardians, held at the Workhouse on Wedlieiday flaunting. The matter arose on the report of the House Committee, who stated that the Master's list of estimates for Christmas extras had been sub- mitted to them. In view of the urgent appeal of the Government for national economy and having in mind existing circumstances, they recommended that the usual grant of extras to the officers be discontinued for this year. They, however, recommended the grant of Christmas extras to the inmates on a modified scale.</p>
        <p>As an indication of the economies effected, it might be mentioned that, whereas the Master received 500 rounds of beef; the committee con- sidered 350 rounds would be sufficient.</p>
        <p>There were also, on the list received by the committee, vafious reductions in the quantities of other fare'to be provided, sultanas and grapes being entirely struck out of the list. Last year 30 dozen pint bottles of beer were provided, and the modified estimate now recommended provided for "beer as required, not to exceed one pint to any inmate." Again each inmate, it was recommended, should receive one orange and one apple instead of ,two oranges and two apples, as usual. The Rev. A. Mitchell asked, a question as</p>
        <p>to the quantity of beer which it was proposed to allow. to the inmates, and was told that the quantity per head was tfie same as in previous</p>
        <p>or a beer? I don't see anything wrong in that, but, on the contrary, I think the committee are proposing-to do the proper thing by allow- ing the inmates, on this particular occasion, to enjoy their Christmas dinner." Mr. Dale said that his point was that the beer was to be brought within the reach of those who, in the past, had shown that they could not resist it. - -</p>
        <p>Mr. j. Billing° contended that by cutting out the beer the Board would not be inflicting any material loss upon the inmates, especially when it was considered that it was proposed. to cut out all the officers' extras. The amendment was defeated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0227" n="art0227">
        <head>OBJECTED TO TEMPTING THEM!</head>
        <p>OBJECTED TO TEMPTING THEM!</p>
        <p>Mr. T. Dale said that the beer allowance•was the only item on the list to which he took' ex- ception and he proposed, as an amendment, that it be not allowed to the inmates. He agreed with all the other items in the list, for he liked the idea of the inmates .having a good repast at Christmas. He had no objection to the roast beef and pluni pudding and soda water, but when it came to beer—well, he thought that there would be many whom they would be entertaining on Christmas Day who had, un- fortunately, been brought there through their intemperate habits. lie, personally, knew some who would not be there save for their intem- perance—for their over-indulgence in strong drink. To offer cheer to such people—people who had so strong a liking for intoxicants, but who had, to some extent, been weaned from their uncontrollable desire whilst they hail been in the hands of the Guardians—was, to his mind, to subject them to sore temptation; in fact, it would be bringing them once more into contact with their gravest foe—the foe who had already vanquished them once. It would be, morer cruel than kind; it would, in his opinion, be like pouring oil on smouldering embers, for whilst they might satisfy the appetites of the diners with the beef, the vegetables and the pudding, was it conceivable., that they would satisfy their latent craving foi• arink with a pint of beer? "Treating," it was said to-day, was a great curse, and were the Guardians, as a public body, justified in offering to " treat " the poor people who had been brought under their control—brought under their control in so many cases, because in the past there had been too much "treating " and "standing treat." They were told, in these times, that they must economise, and he did not know that they could begin to economise in a better direction than by stopping the supply of that which was entirely unnecessary. TEA PREFERABLE TO BEER, SAYS REV. GENTLEMAN.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell seconded the amend- ment. Beer had been allowed in the past, but they ought not to take the past as being the ideal for the present. Circumstances to-day were decidedly exceptional, and beer was cer- tainly an item in the menu that could be dis- pensed with, and no inmate would suffer par- ticular loss. "As a total abstainer for the last twenty-five years," added the rev. gentle- man, "I can certify that a cup of tea is emi- nently preferable to a glass of beer; though I quite agree that, unfortunately, our tastes don't all run in the same direction." (Laughter and "Hear, hear !") Miss Broadbent thought that the inmates might, with profit, be encouraged to follow the King's example. Mr. W. C. Brooks challenged the statement that the majority of the inmates of the Work- house were brought there through the mis-use of strong drink. Personally, he would not like to slander in that manner the poor people to be found in the Poor Law institutions. Mr. Dale: I did not say all</p>
        <p>"NO HOLDING-OUT OF 'THE GLASS.'"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0228" n="art0228">
        <head>OFFICERS' CHRISTMAS EXTRAS.</head>
        <p>OFFICERS' CHRISTMAS EXTRAS.</p>
        <p>The Chairman (Mr. A. J. Jolley) then moved, as a further amendment, that. the minute which proposed to debar the officers of their custonr= ary Christmas extras, be not approved. The total expense involved in giving the officers that which they usually had was, relatively, very slight; in fact, the sum of £l5 would cover it, both at the Workhouse and at the Padgate Cottage 'tomes. Mr. W. 'Flanagan seconded, favouring treat- ing the officers as formerly, and pointing out that when the cost was spread over a Union of the size of the Warrington Union it was a very trifling matter. He thought the stoppage of the usual Christmas extras would be a slight upon the officers, who had, of late, put in extra work without extra remuner4ion.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0229" n="art0229">
        <head>IF THE OFFICERS WERE ASKED --</head>
        <p>IF THE OFFICERS WERE ASKED --</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell took exception to Mr. Flanagan's statement that the passing of the amendment would he a slight upon the officers; indeed, he thought that, if asked, the officers themselves would express a desire that the usual extras should not be allowed under all the circumstances. If he were one of the offi- cers he knew very well that' he would decline to have any part, share or lot in such fare this year. He thought the proposer and the seconder were. overlooking thee fact that the indoor officers had practically been receiving an augmentation of salary during the year, owing to the substantial increase in the price of the provisions which had been allowed them. The indoor officers had been getting that advantage, whilst the outdoor officers had, as it were, been having to help to pay the piper; in fact; they had—it might be said—been having a decrease in their salary, owing to the added cost of living. He thoughts that at the present junc- ture it was up to tho officials to remember the circumstances of the times, and the special call which there was to everyone—public officials, perhaps; more than any others—to show that they were ready to deny themselves, and to do their little bit in the way of economy, so that the icountry , might have more money available for the prosecution and speedy termination of the terrible and very cruel war now raging. He was confident that if the officers were consulted on the subject, one by one, that the Guardians . would find that the vote would be against ' Christmas extras. The members of the Board approved highly of the work done by their officials; but, lie thought, they ought not to hesitate to ask them at the present critical time to set an example by denying themselves at Christmas time the usual extra grant. He thought the officials ought to take the step suggested voluntarily; without any action on the part of ?he Board. "What it is proposed to give here," said the reverend gentleman, holding up a printed form, "is such RR will not grace the King's table this coming Christmas- tide, and I think it will he a .disgrace to the Board if the extra fare suggested is allowed. Just read it—eight turkeys, twelve pounds of sausages, six bottles of essence, four pounds of Jordan almonds, and so on</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0230" n="art0230">
        <head>"ESSENCE OF THE ROOT OF EVIL."</head>
        <p>"ESSENCE OF THE ROOT OF EVIL."</p>
        <p>A member playfully wondered whether the reverend gentleman was under the apprehen- sion that the six bottles of essence referred to contained something alcoholic. The Rev. ,A. M. Mitchell answered that he did not, and added: "But in some bottles there is the essence of the root of evil, is there not?" _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr. E. L. Bent thought that if the officers were approached they would consent to the withdrawal of several of the things which figured in the proposed list of extras. Mr. J. Billinge did not think it should go forth that the officers of the Union were work- ing night and day. "We are," he said, "thor- ough Trade Unionists in Warrington; we don't work our servants twenty-four hours a day. The Clerk might be content to work long hours; but he was an extraordinary person. (Laugh- ter.) Not long since the Board appointed three officers and they worked on eight-hour shifts. Mr. Flanagan delighted the Board with a choice Irish "bull." "When," he said, "we say that a man is working night and day we don't mean that he is working night and day as well." (Laughter.) The amendment was defeated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0231" n="art0231">
        <head>LOCAL ACADEMY.</head>
        <p>LOCAL ACADEMY.</p>
        <p>Excellent Exhibition Of Art</p>
        <p>Students' Work.</p>
        <p>THOSE ON THE LINE."</p>
        <p>On the small side, maybe, but certainly on the good side! Thus, in a sentence, One may crudely sum up his impressions of the exhibi- tion of work done by students of the Warring- ton School of Art, which, this week, has been held in the Art Gallery of the Municipal Museum.</p>
        <p>'What the exhibits, perhaps, lacked 'n num- ber—for Warrington is quite a large-sized town! 7--they made up for in interest, and, included among them, was work of the most highly creditable order—work ,vhi.-11 more than fully justifies the existence of the rate-supported in- stitution which, by fostering and directing the artistic . proclivities of the students who have come within the scope of its operations, is, maybe slowly, but as surely as slowly, building up a movement which in the cuture will be intolerant of unsightliness. And then - , But that's a long story!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0232" n="art0232">
        <head>Many Local Artists at the Front.</head>
        <p>Many Local Artists at the Front.</p>
        <p>In previous years the exhibition of students' work has been held in the Art School itself. This year, owing to it being housed in the Art Gallery, it is enabled to be of longer duration, thus' giving more op- portunities to more people to go and see what local painters and pencillers can do. The exhibition is smaller this year than in previous years, largely because some thirty of the students have gone to fight for their country. It's a pity they have not all joined the same section, or the townspeople would be able to boast quite A Warrington Artists' Corps. Among the thirty or so who have "joined up ' are some of the most advanced and promising of the students on the books, and that fact might reasonably have been expected to have a deteriorating effect on the quality of the current exhibition. Of course, it has had some effect, but so well have the students who remain been brought on, under the able headmastership of Mr. Clarence Mawson, that the exhibition is but little behind that which would have been held had the bright-particular brush-and-palette men not "downed" their colour tubes and picked up the rifle and the sword. On the whole the ex- hibition is one of which the talented " head " may well feel proud—and he 1/41oes, and so does so much of artistic Warrington as has yet in- spected the display.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0233" n="art0233">
        <head>Pictures That Have Won Praise.</head>
        <p>Pictures That Have Won Praise.</p>
        <p>Some of the pictures have gained commenda- tion from the Board of Education, and several have been exhibited in galleries at Liverpool, Hull and Doncaster, for, it should be remem- bered, that the exhibition does not entirely con- sist of pictures painted during the past year only, but is intended to be a representative col- lection of selections from comparatively recent artistic productions done by students of the school.</p>
        <p>Among the outstanding exhibits are a number of clever still life studies in water-colour, most striking amongst them being those contributed by Miss Pearson, Miss Lowe, Miss Clare, Miss Crompton and Mr. R. A. Collinge. Mr. Sydney Clatworthy, an old pupil of the school, and now a Press illustrator in Fleet- street, contributes a clever drawing to the black and white section; to which, also, Miss Oakley and Mr. F. Cooke send admirable studies. Ex- ceedingly promising still-life efforts in chalk are forwarded by two junior students—Harold Cross and ,Stanley Birrell—and Messrs. H. Dell and R. E. Poniton are responsible for sepia studies of more than average excellence. Mr. Edgar B. •Allen exhibits several beauti- ful specimens of design in material including inlaid work, tooled leather work, &amp;c.</p>
        <p>Two Brilliant Studies.</p>
        <p>The headmaster is represented by two bril- liant studies from his own brush, one depict- ing Messrs. Crosfield's wharf at Bank Quay— an unlovely subject most vigorously treated— and the other the old Warrington Bridge, now gone for ever and living only in the memory, or in drawings, paintings and—picture post- cards.</p>
        <p>Yes, altogether the exhibition is decidedly in- teresting and promising, reflecting the highest credit upon the talented principal, under whose good guidance so much of it has been done, and demonstrating beyond doubt that there may exist much of the genuine artistic spirit and love of the beautiful even in so prosaic and work-A-day collection of dispiriting bricks and mortar and unsightly chimneys as unlovely Warrington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0234" n="art0234">
        <head>THOS. snzA</head>
        <p>THOS. snzA</p>
        <p>.</p>
        <p>M DOUGALLS ikSELF FLIUR vtisis • SAVES YOU TIME, TROUBLE AND MONEY. TRY IT TO-DAY</p>
        <p>Johnny came home late from school one day. His -mother asked him what had kept him so long. "Oh, teacher kept me for something I did not do," he answered. ‘.‘ What was that" asked mother qnicUy. "My lessons said Johnny. .</p>
        <p>Mazie : I did; but he didn't take it as I thought he would.- - He called, said I had done. just right, that it was all for the best' and that he was convinced we never could be happy to- gether, anyway. You don't suppose I was going. to stand for that kind of talk, do yon? I wept one little tear—and the next minute he had my head on his. shoulder, was calling me his darling and telling me he couldn't live without me!</p>
        <p>,:,.tr,,,,..:..,.," ~;,i,. ; ~:.:,it.;.ip</p>
        <p>4110140</p>
        <p>Mr. Brooks: You said the. majority. As a matter Of fact, the majority are brought here through unfortunate circumstances over which they had no personal control. Mr. Pale, the speaker added, argued that it would be very unjust to offer the inmates beer, quite overlook- ing the fact that they would have the choice between beer and other drinks. "There is," said Mr. Brooks, ." no holding out of 'the glass' in any tempting manner. Whatever .the cause that brings a person here, he would simply be asked whether he would have a mineral water</p>
        <p>y Alaid, of Symins-ton's, says )1 making delicious coffee is Symington way. kl in a cup of boiling water there you are." raCTON CO., EJinburjh and '</p>
