      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0235" n="art0235">
        <head>riAit •wt ;• , •1t • • 1</head>
        <p>riAit •wt ;• , •1t • • 1</p>
        <p>(Regd. Trade lllarh.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0236" n="art0236">
        <head>lIONIE NOTES.</head>
        <p>lIONIE NOTES.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes And Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>A' Plum Pudding Recipe. , For the benefit of our lady readers we give them the best recipe we know of for their Christmas plum pudding. Take three-quarters of a pound of flour, two heaped-up teaspoonfuls of Borwick's Baking Powder, two ounces of bread-crumbs, one and a half pounds of suet, two pounds of raisins, 'one pound of currants, ten ounces of sugar, two ounces of almonds, ono pound of mixed candied peel, salt and spice to taste. Mix the ingredients well together, and add six eggs, well beaten, and three-quarters of a pint of milk; divide in two, and boil eight hours. Tomato Pie.</p>
        <p>To make a pie is one of the best Ways of cook- ing this favourite vegetable. Take a pound Of good tomatoes and a small Spanish onion, cut all into slices, the onion as small as possible. Butter a pie-dish, sprinkle the bottom With breadcrumbs, on them put a leyr cf tomats then a . little of the -onion, scatter more breadcrumbs, and continue in this way till the dish is full, the top layer being of crumbs. Melt loz. of butter, pour over the tomatoes, &amp;c., and bake slowly until thoroughly cooked, prob- ably three-quarters of an hour. Serve hot. .</p>
        <p>Fairy Puddings. • Required: 2oz. of castor sugar, 2oz. of flour, 2oz. of butter, two yolks of eggs, one white, one lemon-rind, half a pint of milk. Thickly grease some small dariole moulds or cups. Melt the butter in a saucepan, stir in the sugar. and milk. Add this slowly and smoothly to the flour. Then add the beaten yolks and grated lemon-rind. Beat the white stiffly and stir it in lightly. Half fill the cups with this mixture, and bake in a quick oven about 20 minutes, or till firm and a pale brown. Turn out gently and serve at once, or they sink down. Raisin Loaf.</p>
        <p>Ingredients: ilb. of taisius (stoned and Chopped); noz. of margarine; teaspoonful ,of powdered spice; Ilb. of brown sugar; 1 Cup- ful of treacle; ipt. of boiling water; 12oz. of flour; 1 teaspoonful of carbonate of soda. Cream the margarine with the sugar in a deep basin, and stir in the treacle; melt the soda in a little of the hot water, and add this to the mixture. Stir in the rest of the water, beat all, together thoroughly, and then sift in the flour, spice, and raisins, a little of each at a time. Line a tin with greased paper, pour in the mixture, and bake in a fairly hot oven for about three-quarters of an hour.—" Ironic Cookery." Hashed Beef.</p>
        <p>Fry a small onion,, cut in thin slices, in butter; when it begins to colour stir in a tablespoonful of flour, then add a cupful of stock, pepper and salt, a . small pinch of powdered sweet herbs, and a little tarragon vinegar. When the sauce has boiled for 4 minute, or two.. strain it into another saucepan.- When cold, put in the beef cut in thin slices (if roast beef, all outside.parts must be trimmed off), set the saucepan by the side of the fire for the contents to get gradually warm; when nearly,so, a fair allowance of sliced</p>
        <p>gherkins should be added. The longer the pro- cess of warming the better the hash will be.</p>
        <p>Sardines.</p>
        <p>A tempting dish for a poor appetite may be prepared with six or eight sardines, loz. of but-</p>
        <p>ier; onions, a little white sauce, loz. of grated Parmesan cheese, loz. of anchovy butter, toasted bread, bread-crumbs, and seasoning. Trim off the tails of the sardines, peel and chop the onions finely, put them in a small saucepan with enough water to cover them; boil up quickly, strain, .add the butter, and cook for a few minutes; add the sauce and cheese. Cut the toast into pieces a little larger than the sardines, spread with anchovy butter, place a sardine on each piece of toast, spread thickly with the sauce, season with pepper and cayenne; sprinkle over some bread-crumbs, put in a hot oven for a few minutes till warm through. Serve very hot.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0237" n="art0237">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>Those Female Relations. It is the opinion of those who know all about kin, remarks a Pittsburg paper, that if a thorough investigation were made it would be found that the charge that Ere tempted Adam originated with some of Adam's female rela- tions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0238" n="art0238">
        <head>FEAR AND SICKNESS.</head>
        <p>FEAR AND SICKNESS.</p>
        <p>The One Who Laughs At Illness Apt To Live</p>
        <p>Longest.</p>
        <p>The greatest enemy to life is fear, says a noted Chicago clergyman. It is not only akin to death, but is the breath, influence and fore- runner of death. So it is to ,be fought at all tithes with all one's might. Fear is the ally of every malady. Whatever illness besets a man is twice as bad if he is afraid; and if he laughs at it one can carry about and live comfortably many a year. with a deadly disease. So that if a physician could enter all his sick rooms and give some physic to purge away anxiety he might well lose his other medicines.</p>
        <p>The imagination, which is commonly prosti- tuted to frighten little ones, might just as well be utilised to strengthen them. For insance, in- stead of telling them to be good and not to cry lest the bogey man gets them, or the vampires, goblins', and spooks snatch them, it would be more conducive to their peace and happiness if we suggested to them, that in all dark places are the good angels; and that the unseen power?: that move by night are friendly, and that the world we live in is crowded 'with invisible, kind- hearted helpers! The child must imagine some- thing„ and how much he has gained if he is led to fill his, fancy world with friends and not enemies!</p>
        <p>_ • The _child ought to learn that fear is some- thing to be ashamed of. Quite the contrary is often the case. We use fear as a means to in- culcate morality. This is ignorant, unpsycho- logical, dangerous and even wicked. No parent should play upon the fear motive. To develop it is to strengthen that which is, most destruc- tive of any nobleness of character.</p>
        <p>_ To reward a youth for doing a thing because he was afraid not to do it is to pay a premium on moral cowardice. To threaten a child ever and under any circumstances is criminal.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0239" n="art0239">
        <p>the nearest object, and at once breaks through its covering or "mask." The brilliant wings of gauze are unfolded, and,a new world of warmth and light opens before it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0240" n="art0240">
        <head>RETORT UNCOURTEOUS.</head>
        <p>THE RETORT UNCOURTEOUS.</p>
        <p>Two men were hurrying along, and met at the corner of a street, only to coil* and knock their heads. "Why don't you look where you are going?" said one. "I was just going to say the same," said the other, "for you made my head ring." "Your head ring?" "Yes." "That shows it's empty." "Didn't your bead ring?" said the other. " No," was the reply. "Then that shows it's cracked."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0241" n="art0241">
        <head>WORKING SONG.</head>
        <p>WORKING SONG.</p>
        <p>This is hardly topical, is it, with the rain and the Snow and the wind and the hail on our door- step, so to speak, but it is a pretty little poem, don't you think? Come', little maids, your gardens make And plant them full of flowers, And the Run will shine, aml the winds will blow, And the good sky send the showers. ,</p>
        <p>Come, little men, now shoulder arms And off to the gardens go; Plant your seeds, but look out for the weeds, Like noble knights of the hoe.</p>
        <p>Come, little birds, now build your nests And sing us a • pretty song, For the grass is green And the flowers bloom And the sun shines all day long.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0242" n="art0242">
        <head>" CATCH " YOUR FRIEND.</head>
        <p>" CATCH " YOUR FRIEND.</p>
        <p>Here are a couple of riddles, nephews and nieces. Learn them off and you will be able to " catch " your boy and girl friends I.—l am taken from a mine and shut up in a wooden ease, from which I am never released; and yet I am used almost by everybody. An. : A lead pencil. 2.—A house fall and a yard full, but you can't catch a bowl full. Answer: Smoke.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0243" n="art0243">
        <head>JOHNNY'S ANSWER.</head>
        <p>JOHNNY'S ANSWER.</p>
        <p>lIINTS ON HEALTII.</p>
        <p>Useful Information In Popular Language.</p>
        <p>The Hair in Health. Everybody's head, as a rule, should have a thorough washing and brushing once a week with warm water and good soap. I make no exception for ladies, who, however, have far more trouble over this operation Chan the mere male, and to whom, therefore, corresponding sympathy must be extended. Yet the accumula- tion (a) of scurf or dandruff (epidermic scales), (b) of fatty secretion from the sebaceous and sudoriparous glands, and thirdly (c) of dust and dirt particles in and about the roots of the hair is such that it is never safe ,to leave the hair unwashed more than a week or ten days at the outside. Neglect is punished by premature loosening of the hair, by disease of the skin, or by the intrusion of vermin which then find an admirable breeding place and multiply exceed- ingly before their presence is detected. Many , people find it advantageous to dispense with soap altogether, and in place to use the egg. You break two or three eggs, and knead the contents in a basin; then work this through your hair thoroughly, perhaps with the addition of a little warm water. Then you Wash out (also with warm.water) and dry. Nothing more effectually removes scurf; and you have the additional gain Oi the hair becoming glossy, instead of dry and harsh, as it is apt to become after • soap.—" Preston Guardian." • Muddling. Not to every woman is allotted the blessed gift of order and organisation. gome there are who • are born methodical, but the muddler is none too rare. She is in fat in the majority. The unmethodical appear to be pathetic victims of their own mismanagement. It is they them- • selves who usually suffer most. Almost always their families escape the consequent discomfort and 'confusion because muddlers are, generally speaking, wonderfully good-natured, self-forget- ting, unselfish, easygoing people: They con- trive somehow or other to work domestic mat- ters round in the long run, so that, as they quite joyfully declare, "it all comes right in the wash." Well, I'm sure I hope it always does, and will, come right in the wash, only I do think the wear and tear unnecessarily heavy. . . . Why should a woman's working day be such a world without end? The wear and tear of constant' labour leaves its mark upon her features, and it leaves its mark upon her mind as well. She feels aged and weary when she might feel young and fresh, and on the well-established theory that all work and no play makes Jack a dull boy, this fatal panning out of domestic duties, muddling, dawdling. dreaming, is damaging alike to mind and man- ners. Could you not, dear muddler, adopt just a few definite. home rules in the management of things domestic, and so save yourself from growing faded, worn, jaded, and uninteresting? Too often has it been said that woman's work is never done. It is the expression of an idea which might Well be trampled down if muddlers would study method. Meanwhile ,you scramble on for days, weeks, months, years, you scuttle through life in a haphazard fashion, and to quote the words of coster notoriety, "you've only got yourself to blame." The pity of it.— Marie Blanche, in "Good Health."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0244" n="art0244">
        <head>LAND OF LOST TOYS.</head>
        <p>THE LAND OF LOST TOYS.</p>
        <p>It is getting near Christmas, isn't it? Soon we shall have the shop windows putting on their festive season garb 'soon, too, you will he think- ing about those presents which make the Yule- tide festival so jolly and so anxiously looked forward to by you young folk. The following verse has a Christmassy ring about it, hasn't it?</p>
        <p>Last Christmas, when Dorothy woke in her bed, She found a large stocking hung close to her head; And over its top, as though over a wall, There peeped the blue eyes of a beautiful doll. Then; searching around here with cries: of delight, She found other treasures soon pepping in sight; A work-box, so useful in making repairs; A tea-set for dolly, and table and chairs. But that was last Christmas, and tell me, oh, where Is Dorothy's doll with the beautiful hair?</p>
        <p>Her dresses grew old, and she wandered one day Where the tea things, all broken, had gone with the tray; The table and work-box soon followed, I know, For dolls in Lost Toy-land are able to sew. _</p>
        <p>There sometimes in fancy I see her at work, Repairing the damage her mistress would shirk. Beside the lost tfible she sits on a chair,</p>
        <p>The tea things, once broken, are mended witl care, And dolly will lay them so neatly; you see She's fond of inviting the fairies to tea. So here, her own mistress, she's happier far Than any poor dolls in the nursery are.</p>
        <p>What Happened. Daisy: So you're wearing Gerald's ring again I thought you sent it back to him and told bin he needn't call again—it .was all over betweei you.</p>
      </div>
