      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0250" n="art0250">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Tho Dad as Chum.</p>
        <p>• Corporal Tattersall, the Rochdale soldier. who presided at the opening of the Winwiek Soldiers' Home and :Institute last week, after- wards set out for Serbia, accompanied by his only son. I have conic across a few similar yaws. I knew such a pair who went with the Territorials to the Dardanelles. There they were separated, and the elder was wounded and shipped off to hospital, whereupon the dutiful son wrote-home: " I hear that the old cock has got hit, hut don't worry; it's nothing serious, and you can het he'll be round again directly as lively as ever."</p>
        <p>Brothers in Action. Since the last issne I have met three " Ter- riers</p>
        <p>" invalided home from the region just indicated. One, a nephew of mine; dropped in unexpectedly on Saturday night. He had been in the thick of it alongside his brother. The latter is wounded and, in hospital at 7rlalta. He himself was buried with . some others through the trench being blown in. His ribs were crushed and a broken one pierced_ the lung, hut, sent from Malta to a London hospital and thence to a Blackpool convalescent camp, he seems likely to make a good recovery. I know two other brothers belonging to another regiment out there, and have lately had a little talk with the older. He said to me: " The only thing that both- ered Me when •we landed on the Peninsula was how Jimmy would behave in a bayonet charge. You know, he's only a youngster, and I had persuaded him to enlist with me, and I wondered if he'd funk. Well, the test- ing time came, and when we jumped into the enemy's trench Jimmy . couldn't help being sort of affected when he saw the Turks drop- ping. Pointing to a heap of them he said to me Oh, look here.' We were just Making for another lot, and I said, No, you musn't look there; you must look yonder. If you stop looking there you'll.soon be among them.' And Jimmy bucked up and did wonders—he did really; I never feared for him after that:"</p>
        <p>Vegetarianism. "Simple Simon's" letter, with the reason- able suggestion that we should all resolye upon a: non-flesh diet on two days of each week, set me thidking of experiences of the cult in the byzmes.l, in one district in which I lived some years, an earnest and determined apostle of vegetarianism converted m'actically the whole village in which he made his home for a while. On obtaining an appointment as traveller for a wholesale house dealing in vegetarian food, he removed from those parts, whereupon, alas I the sow that was washed. re-' turned to her wallowing in the mire—in a word, that weak community became a village of backsliders. What poor mortals we are even in this respect—the great majority of us, I mean; of course, as all readers of this paper are constantly reminded, there are some I very remarkable exceptions. I was, thinking, however, of one who had lapsed from the true faith, and .yet was constrained to accept an invitation to in side at a lecture on vegia- tarianism. Naturally curious to hear my,,, good friend's explanation, I attended, and I confess I was somewhat taken aback when he told the audience that he was sorry to hate had to return to flesh diet, but he had been ill and weak and his doctor's orders left him no alternative! Talk about giving the show away! J couldn't help a feeling of pity for the lecturer, because the chairman had appar- ently recovered his health after modifying the exclusively -vegetarian diet that had confess- edly, impaired it. Temperance Sunday. This was pretty generally observed in the district, and I am in hearty sympathy with the movement. , Vt'hether we can live in health and comfort without flesh meat or not —and I agree that we might try it a couple of days 'a week at least—l think most people would be better without alcohol than with it, and that in any ease it would. be patriotic on the part of the generality to abstain during the period of the war. Of course, my sugges- tion refers mainly to the respectable moderate drinkers. Excess.</p>
        <p>, As for such folk as were drinking and fight- ing in the lodging-house the other day, there can be but one opinion. That carouse ended remarkably like Hans Breitmann's party when "Der gompany fighted mit dable-legs Till der gonstable made ,'em Atop." And yet—and yet—as soiiieone has -truly enough observed, while there, are vicious habits which imply a nature so inherently had that their victims, even if reclaimed, are never likely to become very useful citizens, the par- ticular vice now in question is of the charac- ter of a disease or a madness, and may be found in the best natures. There is no evil more degrading to its victims, more cruel in the ravages it commits upon family life; yet, like any other disease, it is to be found among men' of sterling character, and, like most other diseases, is open to physical remedy. 1 ,cannot pursue this further at present, but shall be glad to do so on some conve4ient opportunity.</p>
        <p>Tea and Its Terrors. Those who in these times abstain from in- toxicants do well; but, as Was pointed out in last week's " Hints on Health," it is not to be supposed that they can safely drink tea to excess either. I remember reading in one. of the reports of the Registrar-General that the increase of insanity in :Scotland was due not to whisky-drinking, but to-over-indulgence in tea. I fancy Ireland will rank next for con- sumption of the "cups that dicer," etc. We do not forget about Russia, but the Russians drink it weak with a little lemon in it. As to what appeared last week under the heading of " Tea and the heart," I read somewhere a while ago that the main canse of indigestion is expessive indulgence in tea. The late Sir Andrew Clark said that fire o'clock tea was more responsible than anything else for the ailments of the West End.. I know of a man who suffered so acutely from dyspepsia as to have come to the conclusion- that his heart was soriously affected. Inquiring as to his habits of life, a doctor whom he consulted elicited that he mias a tea drinker. " Try coffee instead," said the doctor. He did so, and soon became an- other man, with no more apprehension of heart disease. Still that paragraph last week bade us 'ware coffee also. Well, each must decide for himself and herself: sug- gest that the danger lies in excess. A Short Article. I may note by the way that during the struggle over the liquor laws in New York State, Mr. William North wrote the shortest " leader "I. ever read or heard of. It ran " We had far rather see the whole world get drunk of its owm free will than one man kept sober by compulsion." He had been in- structed to make his article " short and very much to the Point." Time Bible.</p>
        <p>The advertisement of next Thursday's meet- ing of the Warrington auxiliary to the British and Foreign Bible Society set me thinking of Robert Stephens, who divided the Bible into verses. As was pointed out on the occa- sion of the formal presentation of a copy of the Authorised Version of the Scriptures to King George, his Testament was published in 1551, and the first I,7atglisli New Testament in h iel the example was followed came out six years later, being published in Geneva. The first complete Bible with the text in verses was the Geneva Bible published in 1.560. 'rho division. into chapters or sections had been made in the manuscript Bible in the thirteenth century. One of the most remarkable un- used editions of the 1311,1 e was that pub- -I,shed by Hills and Field in 1653, and was called the Pearl Bible, because of the _small type in which it was printed. It abounded in errors. A manuscript 'account says they num- bered 6,000. Sterne, " a solid scholiiici' -found 3,600 faults in it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0251" n="art0251">
        <head>EXPLOSIVES.</head>
        <p>EXPLOSIVES.</p>
        <p>0....r5v0r -</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0252" n="art0252">
        <head>Scientific Side Of Modern Warfare.</head>
        <p>The Scientific Side Of Modern Warfare.</p>
        <p>"LIT. &amp; PHIL." LECTURE.</p>
        <p>Harmless-looking little bottles full of pale- coloured liquids were handed round at the meeting of the Warrington Literary and Philo- sophical Society, in the Cairo-street School, last week, and as they passed from (Ale to another the Lecturer, whilst assuring - his audience that there was no danger,- with grim humour at the same time issued a caution against dropping them—"for one neVer knows !" The lecturer was Dr. E. Frankland Armstrong, the bottles contained specimens of chemical liquids which are employed in manufacturing the deadliest explosives in use in the present war, and the title of the lecture—which was listened to attentively by a goodly-sized audi- ence—was "Modern Explosives."</p>
        <p>"CRIMINAL FOLLY OF THOSE IN HIGH</p>
        <p>PLACES."</p>
        <p>In the course of his lecture the Doctor traced the evolution of artillery ammunition from the smoky black powder used in the Crimean War to the devastating cordite of to-day, and, inci- dentally, referred to the important part which gun-cotton plays in the manufacture of the deadly modern explosive. In view of the im- portance of cotton in the production of this explosive they must all, 'Dr. Armstrong said, realise the absolute criminal folly of those in charge in high places in the country in allow- ing the Germans to have the access they had had to cotton for so long. They must realise also the extraordinarily appalling ignorance of their leaders and of their Press in quarrelling month after Month as to whether cotton was or was not necessary to the manufacture of propellant explosives. It was absolutely essen- tial, and there were thousands of men in the country who could have answered that ques- tion at once.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0253" n="art0253">
        <head>From Medicine To Murder.</head>
        <p>From Medicine To Murder.</p>
        <p>The Lecturer went on to point out that .gly- cerine, which in peace times was most used as a medicament, was required in enormous quan- tities in war time for the manufacture of cordite. The only way to make glycerine was by saponifying fats. That was the method adopted by the soap-maker,- who used the fats for the production of soap and sold the glycer- ine to makers of explosives. The one material above all others which Germany could not produce. in her own country, and of which she had the greatest shortage, was fat of all kinds. Without fat Germany could not get glycerine. Even to-day they were allowing large quantities of fat to go from this country and her colonies into Germany. The position with regard to fat was just as serious as it was with regard to cotton. They could starve Germany inure easily by preventing her from getting cotton and fat than by withholding any other materials. The Deadly Shrapnel Shell.</p>
        <p>The Doctor went on to refer to the great changes which had taken place in gun con- strtiction consequent upon the introduction of cordite. Smaller chambers were needed and the guns, could he made lighter. For instance, the naval 15-inch guns of to-day, charged with. 400 M. of cordite, had a muzzle velocity fifty per cent. greater and fired a far heavier pro- jectile than the old 110-ton guns, using 960 Th. of prism powder. Speaking of high explosives, the Lecturer said that shells were of two kinds —thse shrapnel shell for use against troops and the high explosive shell. The shrapnel shell consisted of a hollow steel cylinder, packed with bullets, and containing a bursting charge in the base. A time fuse was fixed in the nose, which was capable of being so regulated as to explode the charge in the air at any desired Point. The high explosive shell was required to detonate with the maximum possible con- cussive power So as to shater everything with- in reach. They were made of forged steel with comparatively thin walls, and contained a heavy charge of high explosive, generally cast into the shell in pt fused condition. • Armour- piercing shells were of stouter construction, so as to be able to penetrate, .and had a nose capped with •soft steel to prevent -the shell shattering on-impact. The raw materials from which high explcsives were made, it was curious to note, were without exception substances which in times of peace were regarded as quite innocuous, most of them being derived from coal-tar. The war bad familiarised them with a hitherto' comparatively little-known coal-tar product—toluene—which when nitrated formed trinitrotoluene, or T.N.T. T.N.T. gave out great volumes of black smoke on detona- tion; hence the names "Jack Johnsons," "Coal Boxes," &amp;c., with which the troops have christened them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0254" n="art0254">
        <head>Germans' Devilish I ngenuity.</head>
        <p>Germans' Devilish I ngenuity.</p>
        <p>In conclusion, Pr. Armstrong said that some of the German bombs, as used in Zeppelin raids, were marvels of ingenuity. One detonator set off another, until the fittll high explosive charge was ignited. The incendiary bombs consisted of an.,outer skin wound round with tarred rope and containing a charge consisting of a mixture of aluminium dust with oxide of iron. This mixture was known as thermite, and in peace time was largely used for welding rails and repairing castings where intense local heating Was required. When it was set burn- ing the aluminium combined with the oxygen which it took from the .oxide of iron, and an enormous amount of heat was developed. The bombs also contained phosphorous. which Was converted into vapour and burned with a poisonous flame, giving rise to poisoned burns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0255" n="art0255">
        <head>WANTED : A MORAL EXPLOSIVE !</head>
        <p>WANTED : A MORAL EXPLOSIVE !</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett proposed a vote of thanks to the Lecturer, and, after congratulating him on the manner in which he had handled a difficult subject, said that the horror of the present situation was that the stientists of this country were bound, as a patriotic duty, to devote their activities to the devising of scientific means even more devilish than those employed by their adversaries; 'there Seemed to be no way out. It was, hewever, possible that out of this evil permanent good might come. If the War did not rouse the world to see the utter folly, the atrocious, appalling waste and the wicked cruelty of such a method. of settling disputes, nothing would. it had occurred to him that it would be an extraordinarily good. thing if they could have, a sort of moral explo- sive', something to shake the people out of their stupid apathy. The eloquence of the preachers, the prattling of the politicians, and the hysterics of the newspapers, all seemed to fail. Was any man in the world able to invent a moral explosive? -If so, it would be a 'greater achievement than any-thing that had ever been done by even the greatest chemists. (Hear, hear.) Air. J. 'S. Broome seconded and Mr. 0. Loft- house supported. ,</p>
        <p>WHEN THELWALL MADE, GUN-POWDER. The President (the Rev. if. V. Pigot) men- tioned that there 'was once a gun-powder mill •at Thelwall, the site of which was still known. flow long ago the mill was abandoned he did. not know, Another fact which had brought the, subject home to this neighbourhood was that Colonel Lyon's father, to the great regret of his friends, 'met his death when engaged oil some interesting but dangerous experiments with explosives. Mr. S. P..Sileock asked whether it was impos- sible to make explosives without cotton? Dr. Armstrong answered that cotton at pre- sent was absolutely essential for the manufac- ture of propellant explosives. They could make them of other materials, such as wood pulp, &amp;c., hut that would necessitate an alteration of the whole science of artillery. Clever as the Germans were, he did not think they could possibly do that. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0256" n="art0256">
        <head>Tit For Tat.</head>
        <p>Tit For Tat.</p>
        <p>" Cl• ric,,. darling," mnarked Archibald 'to the ten st,tne of sweet feudally which nestled agal nst his manly bosom, " tam- ihat are engaged, lily angel, we should have na secrets from each other."</p>
        <p>" N. lovey," purred: " Well, then, Illy sweetest, do Please MI !no how old you pre." " With . pleasure," cooed the sweet young thing. " But, first. dearest, please tell me just how much you get a week. 1 have often won- dered."</p>
        <p>Archibald did a bit of rapid headwork. Ilk mind ran ahead into the future.</p>
        <p>" Forgive me, Clarice. darling." he ex- claimed, as he softly pressed a chaste salute on her snow-white forehead. "it was .none of my busineu to a* such a que4tion."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0257" n="art0257">
        <head>EXAMINVE, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 191.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINVE, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 191.</p>
        <p>ELIGO 7NI.</p>
        <p>Curious Facts About A Tiny North Sea island.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0258" n="art0258">
        <head>GERMAN GIBR!\LTAR.</head>
        <p>THE GERMAN GIBR!\LTAR.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR fvIOUNFIELD.</p>
        <p>Even on large scale maps Heligoland is represented by a mere dot. Had it been situate off the coast of Ireland or Scotland none save the local fishermen would have known its existence or given it a name. But Heligoland—Helgoland some have itstands like a sentinel opposite the mouths of the Elbe and Weser, and seems destined to be- come, if it is not already, one of tile most famous bits of land in modern history. In view of the fame-that it already enjoys and the prominence. it must inevitably gain in the near future, we propose to tell- our readers something of its history. _</p>
        <p>It stands about- 28 miles from the nearest Coast line of Germany, though from the nearest port it is a rather longer journey than this. First named Fositelandt, and after- wards Amelandt, it became Heligoland, which is said. to mean Holy Island, and to echo a time when it was sacred to the worship of the Saxon god Phoseta. Another explana- tion makes it Helgoland, after a king Helgo who held it fer a time.</p>
        <p>of the inhabitants. Two thousand British subjects lived on -the island, and these were handed over to the mercies of German mili- tarism without the least consideration for their wishes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0259" n="art0259">
        <head>TWELVE CENTURIES AGO.</head>
        <p>TWELVE CENTURIES AGO.</p>
        <p>We may conveniently open our story at a date 1,200 years ago, when a pious Northum- brian monk stumbled upon it in the course of a missionary journey. Willibtord had entered the monastery at Ripon as a child, and in manhood became a zealous pioneer of Chris- tianity. He embarked upon a mission to the Frisians, but was driven by bad weather to take refuge upon the island. It was then called • Fositelandt, and in size and shape re- sembled the Isle of Man. .1t is to be feared that Willibrord lacked the tact which his task required. The natives were pagan, yet kindly, and might have given a sympathetic hearing to a more patient preacher. They had superstitious ideas about cattle, am!, re- fused on any account to kill them. Willi- brord thought it wise to show his contempt for pagan notions and killed several beasts. They would only draw water from their well (,there was only one on the island) in perfect and religious silence, making it a religious act. The missionary preached and. baptised at the mouth Of the well, to the hbrror of the natives. Fearful lest he should awaken the wrath of evil spirits, they took up weapons against the Christians. One was martyred, and the -saint and his companions fled.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0260" n="art0260">
        <head>MONKS, MISSIONARIES AND LEGENV3.</head>
        <p>MONKS, MISSIONARIES AND LEGENV3.</p>
        <p>In the year 785, two generations later, an- other missionary, 'Lurdgar, appeared upon the island and proved much more successful. The inhabitants professed Christianity And built some humble churches, which remained until washed away by the sea in the 18th cen- tury.</p>
        <p>In 1530, when Luther's doctrines were stir- ring Europe. a Protestant .monk landed and sought to win the inhabitants to the new teachings. Feeling ran high, and the apostle of the Reformation was Hung over a duff into the sea. Legend has it that when night came the figure of the monk appeared on the Neistach Rock and preached to the terror- filled natives.in a voice that echoed through the island. They were rimpressed, and Pro- testantism gained a permanent place. But lest the island should forget its guilt it is said that even yet when a storm rages and lashes the sea to fury the voice of the mar- tyred preacher is heard amid the wailing of the wind. and his. figure can be seen with a warning band upraised upon:the rock..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0261" n="art0261">
        <head>BATTLE OF SEA AND LAND.</head>
        <p>THE BATTLE OF SEA AND LAND.</p>
        <p>So much for its religious history. It, will be convenient now to tell of the outward changes which have reduced the large island to its present minute proportions.‘ • There is evidence that t \wive centUries ago it was larger than. the Isle of Wight, and in shape very much like the Isle of Man, or perhaps Ireland. But the fate of other North _Sea islands (notably the lands of Earl God; win) has befallen,it. It has become a prey to the restless waters. Two hundred years ago the island had assumed the shape of an hour-glass. Then in 1720 the sea , broke through and left two small islands with a tide-swept channel between: Ancient churches and traces of earlier days were de- voured by the waves, which year by year, wore down the hrumbling rocks. Those who will turn up Blackie's " Gazetteer " or 1855 will learn that " the sea is preying so fast upon the island that it will probably at no distant periqd be reduced to a more shoalior hank." From a goodly land 'like the Isle of‘ Wight the merciless waters have worn it down until its length is, but a mile and its greatest breadth not a third of a mile.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0262" n="art0262">
        <head>TAKEN BY A BRITISH ADMIRAL.</head>
        <p>TAKEN BY A BRITISH ADMIRAL.</p>
        <p>Tt was in 1807 that this vanishing bit of land came to he Na British posSession, cap- tured by Admiral Macnajnara Russell from the Danes during the. unsettled Napoleonic .period. The despatch which the Admiral sent home, is worth looking up. However little the. island may have been esteemed in later years, it is clear that its captor had a fairly accurate idea of its value. He wrote on September 6th, 1807:</p>
        <p>Having -found that Lord Falkland had, with his usual zeal find promptness, sum- moned the garrison on the 301 h ultimo, and that his proposals were rejected by the gover- nor, I was making my arrangements to storm. him with the marines and seamen of the squadron if he did not instantly surrender, for at this time the value of tke island is immense. At 6 p.m., however, he sent out a flag of truce desiring that an oftlcer might he sent in the morning to treat on articles' of capitulation; and I accordingly at daylight yesterday morning despatched Lord Viscount Falkland and Lipid D'Auvergne (first of the ship) for that service. At. 2 p.m. the deputa- tion returned with the artiqles of capitula- tion, which I immediately ratified. With a small expense this island may be made a little Gibraltar, and a safe haven for small cfaft even in the winter; it is a key to the rivers Ems, Weser, Jade, Elbe, and Eyder, the only asylum ,at present for myr cruisers in these seas.</p>
        <p>A " little Gibraltar " it was destined to be, but not for Britain.</p>
        <p>LORD SALISBURY'S BLUNDER.</p>
        <p>It remained a British possession for 83 years,/ when Lord Sali3bury handed it over to .the Kaiser. It was given ,to soothe the feel- ings of Germany and reconcile them to the renunciation of Uganda and the establishment of an English protectorate over the Island of Zanzibar.</p>
        <p>• The official announcement said : " Subject to .the consent of the British Phrliament, Eng- land cedes to His 31ajesty the Gorman Em- peror this island of Heligoland. A date will Pie fixed for the introduction of universal ser- vice and the German customs laws, and the present inhabitants will have for a certain time the right to assume English no it is interesting to turn back to the news- papers and magazines of the period to take note of the expressions of contemporary opinion. Few seem to have attached much value to Heligoland. None seem to have had any vision of what the island might become in the hands of Germany. The Government, knowing how little was left, and how quickly erosion had taken place, probably thought that the island would soon cease to exist; But even assuming the political value to be small, they acted with cruel disregard of the feelings</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0263" n="art0263">
        <head>SMALL NATION GIVEN AWAY.</head>
        <p>A SMALL NATION GIVEN AWAY.</p>
        <p>They were few in number, but there was a principle at stake, and it is painful to reflect that the Heligolanders were treated with such cynical disregard. The " Pall Mall Gazette" sent over Miss Friederichs as a special com- missioner to ascertain . the fcolings of the islanders, and those who care to turn up the files of that paper will find a very caustic but well-deserved rebuke to Lord Salisbury's Gov- ernment. The feelings of the people were summed hp by a Heligoland dame in a single sentence. " You may give away .a cat or a dog," she said, " but not a whole people." Yet that was what was ibeing done They were a .small people, it is true, but they were given away—rtraditions, history, churches, and two thousand souls, in a document of fifty-two words. Those who deshie to calculate the cost of this blunder have but to •realise how differ- ently the events of the past year would have flowed if this " little Gibraltar," " the key to the rivers .Ems, Weser, jade, Elbe and •Eyder," had been in British possession.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0264" n="art0264">
        <head>GIBRALTAR INDEED.</head>
        <p>A GIBRALTAR INDEED.</p>
        <p>The German Government were not long in making use of Heligoland as a naval station. Operations through twenty-five years have changed the peaceful island into a powerful military fortress. - An extensive harbour for .submarines was made. The shaky cliffs have been made over again in concrete, and huge batteries hide behind them. With the com- ing of the airship Heligoland became a Zep- pelin base, and five of the largest ships can find shelter there. Millions have been spent on Heligoland, and its system of batteries, guns and gun shelters is said to be the most complete in the world. There is another and pleasanter aspect of the little island. It is -a pleasure resort, as well as a fortress. As far from the main- 'land as the Isle of Man from Lancashire, with the broad sea on every side, and within sight of the. vessels that enter and leave Hamburg, it offers a fine and pleasing panorama. The gently-sloping and sandy beach offers the finest bathing in the world, and children paddle and prattle under the nozzles of guns which wait to spread frightfulness for twenty miles around.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0265" n="art0265">
        <head>QUAINT RELICS AND SURVIVALS.</head>
        <p>QUAINT RELICS AND SURVIVALS.</p>
        <p>The only relic of the past is a quaint seven- teenth century church. It has a modern tower built by a Heligolander who made money as a shipbuilder in Bremen, but the church remains scarcely changed after two and a half centuries of use. The islanders of that date, remote from the world and out of touch with ecclesiastical fashions, framed and adorned the church accOrding,' to their .own ideas. % The panels of the gallery, which runs round three sides of the 'building, are filled with strange, Biblical pictures, one of which represents the devil with a wooden leg. The seats are of the old family kind, each one being a family freehold. The people regard their pews as tl6y would their home, and furnish and decorate them to their indi- vidual taste. To look down from the gallery is to see multi-coloured wooden -little wilder- ness, the spaces as clearlyT divided as the squares on a chess board,' but painted in white, red, green, blue or -yellow,, according to the owners' ideas. Each pew bears the owner's name in blaek, and inscriptions tell the dates 'of the deaths of •previous owners. A man has the power to bequeath his seat to any member .of his family, just as he would his house or other property. - -</p>
        <p>_ On each side of the communion table there is a private box glazed with blue glass, and suggesting a. small omnibus. One is the offi- cial seat of the magistrate and the other of the governor of the island. Close by hang some time-glasses, so very few of which have survived in our own land. These sand-glasses measure hours, half-hours, and quarters. The parson of old time solemnly -turned the glass as be began to preach, and the running out of the sand told him that his hour or half- hour was 'up. Other curioSities are models of ships, which hang frola the roof, and which, though they look very much out of place to the landsman, would apPear fitting ornaments to fishermen and children of the sea. A relic of English ownership survives—Or did until the war--,--in the form of an English sermon preached .once a month.</p>
        <p>PRAYERS FOR WRECKS.</p>
        <p>It is said that until fifty years ago the church had a. survival much more curious than any we have mentioned. RegularlY, Sunday by Sunday, a form of petition was read which prayed, that 'wrecks might occur on their coast. The islanders from time immemorial plun- dered wreekCd vessels, and their piety and prayers (it is a failing which no enlighten- ment has quite removed) took form from their wishes. Like the., people of Cornwall, they saw no harm in smuggling and looked upon wrecks as special providenees. They placed false lights to betray ships, to the rooks, and gave God thanks for mercies received when' ships and seamen lay broken on the beach. They were good Christians, as they under- stood Christianity, and were never absent from church—unless there was a wreck to be plundered on ,the shore. But after a lighthouse had gleamed out for two generations and gallant Heligolanders had learned to man a_lifeboat, it seems to have dawned upon someone that prayers for wrecks could be conveniently left out. And left out they were.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0266" n="art0266">
        <head>TEN-MINUTE SERMONS.</head>
        <p>TEN-MINUTE SERMONS.</p>
        <p>Army Chaplain Speaks Of His</p>
        <p>Duties.</p>
        <p>The duties .of an army chaplain formed the 'subject of an interesting lecture given by the Rev. 'Cation J. T. Mitchell (rector of Waver- tree) at a Chapter ,of the Clergy of• the Rural Deanery of Winwick, held at the Church House,; Bridge-street, Warrington, last Iveek. Speaking of his experiences whilst a chaplain at one of the home camps, (-anon Mitchell said that no• chaplain was alloweet to address the troops for more than ten minutes at a parade service. De emphasised the need of forceful preaching, ;iiying that a chaplain must have something new to say and that hc discourses must be extremely vivid. Concluding, the speaker said that he felt it to \be, an immense privilege to be allowed to speak to men who. althouglA going out to meet danger and possible death, showed a remarkable eheerfuNess, and were full of determination to do their duty whatever happened. AN IMPORTANT SUGGESTION.</p>
        <p>:rho question of church reform was dealt with by Mr. H. Singl4on Cooper, the. organis- ing secretary of the Church Reform League. Ile declared that there was a necessity for making an alteration in the existing method of legislation. iii:; suggestion was that the Church, through its own representative body, should have the power of initiating leghtlation which should lie on the table for a prescribed period, and if .no address in opposition was presented to the Crown, it would automatically become law. .Mr. Cooper also spoke on ques- tions of patronage, finance and dilapidation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0267" n="art0267">
        <head>CHRISTIAN NAMES.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN NAMES.</p>
        <p>Their Origin And Their Meaning.</p>
        <p>By C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Last week we had a talk about surnames. This week we will consider Christian names. Perhaps these ought' to have come first in order, because they were in being first. We have in the old Rolls of the Graveships and Court Rolls.of Wakefield Manor lists of names which suggest many interesting points. These Rolls, which go back to the thirteenth .cen- tury, consist of skins parchment stitched to- gether, measuring, when unrolled, thirty or forty feet. They are written partly in Eng- lish and partly in abbreviated Latin, just as the readiest- word came to the pen of the writer. Graves or Groves probably meant those who had charge of the affairs of the Manor. There is the Saxon word Gerefa, in- dicating one in authority, from which it prob- ably sprang. In connection with the Saxon tuna or settlements the Greave occupied very much the same position as is now. filled by our Town Clerks, he was the business man of the community. ' Of course, there was the head man of the tun-ships, or town-ships, over him, but he did the work. In some settlements we read of special greaves in Charge of the moors and of the dykes, moor-greaves and dyke-greaves. The office appears to have been of a voluntary character, and in the old Manor rolls we have records of those who on election refused to serve being fined (see below). We hive the Scotch official called greeve still in use in connection with the man- agement of estates. and corresponding to our English term bailiff. The Same term Grave is used in connection with the Wink Tribu- nals of 'Westphalia (see introduction to Scott's "Anne of Gierstem"), in which it .appears to apply to the clerk of the court.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0268" n="art0268">
        <head>AN ANCIENT COURT ROLL.</head>
        <p>AN ANCIENT COURT ROLL.</p>
        <p>The following names from the list of Graves, and from the Court Rolls of the above Manor, show that while some of the parties named possessed double name, others, especially in the early period, had to be designated by their place of residence, ,their occupation, or parentage :- 1272. 'Henry fil John de Rastrik. 1284. Robert de •Thackmache(r). 1284. Thos. Hogson. 1285. Hugh' le ,Tinker. 1285. Henry le Pynder. 1297. Petre de Sutteelyf. 1297. Ad del Locwod. 1297. John de Holeway. 1297. Richard de TOlehyll. 1297.. Soureby, Geppe he forester. 1297. 'Richard le Bagger. 1297. Will le Pinder de Staynland. % 1297. Petre Suthelife. 1306. Alcok Jo Waynewrth. 1306. Roger flu John Molend and John de Sunderland paid us each fine for not serving as grooves, as elected. Iqo7. John de Stone 1309. Simon 1311. John flu Walter de Northowrom. 1335. Matthew de Ouendene. Henry by the Broke. 1340. Jahn I Matthew de Totehill. 1342. John le 'Milner. . 1341. Richd. flu Jordan le Pynder. 1349. Roger Tonkin. de Rastryk. 1350. John de Whithill. 1351. Roger Cowhird. 1352. John del Botherod. 1356. John Wilkynson. John de Botheroide.</p>
        <p>1357. John Strong. 1360. John del Botherode. 1365: Henry flu Stephen. 1368. John de Wyluby. 1369. Elias de Northend. 1370. John do Bratwhait. 1372. John Synison. 1382. John Jenson Sinison (alias John Sim son). 1418. John Weloweby. 1429. Hugh Fox. 1449. John attownend. 1468. John Townende.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0269" n="art0269">
        <head>1482. Ric. Boythes and Thomas ffurness.</head>
        <p>1482. Ric. Boythes and Thomas ffurness.</p>
        <p>It will be observed that the list, from which we have taken only a few 'names, covers a period of two hundred years. In the full list it is noticeable that at the beginning of the period there were few who possessed definite surnames, while towards the end almost all have double names. We may look upon it therefore that this was the period When sur- names were generally adopted, although it does not cover the whole period. Many names now in use can in these lists be recog- nised, although the spelling is slightly differ- ent. The saint) name is spelt in several differ- ent ways. For instance, the names of Bother- ode and Russworth have. each five variations in their spelling. It is said that in those times few knew how to spell their own name, and fewer still those of their neighbours. It is possible that the clerk being aware that such names were spelt in different ways deter- mined to chronicle them all.</p>
        <p>Coming now to Christian names, we may roughly class them under the four principal divisions—those of Teutonic, Latin, Greek, and Hebrew origin. These. however, by no Means cover the whole. We have, for ex- ample, names from the Old Norse and the Celtic, but of these there are not many.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0270" n="art0270">
        <head>TEUTONIC ORIGIN.</head>
        <p>TEUTONIC ORIGIN.</p>
        <p>These are mostly Saxon, with a sprinkling, of Danish. We have the names Albert (all bright), Alfred (all peace), Baldwin (a bold winner), Charles (valiant), Cuthbert (noted splendour), Edgar (rich spear), Edmund (pro- tector), Edward (valuable keeper), all good Saxon names.</p>
        <p>Egbert (bright), Everard (firm), Ferdinand (risking life), Frederick (peaceful ruler), Geoffrey, Gilbert, Godfrey (God's peace), Henry • (home ruler), and its feminine, Hen- rietta, or Harriet; Herbert (bright warrior), Hubert (bright mind), Hugh (mind), Hum- phrey (prop of peace), Norman (northman), Ernest (eaglestone), Bertha (bright or famous). The name Gilchrist is of interesting origin. It means the servant of Christ, Gill meaning servant, Gilroy red servant. This, however, must be of Celtic origin. We have the gillie or servant in the Highlands, where Gaelic is spoken.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0271" n="art0271">
        <head>LATIN ORIGIN.</head>
        <p>LATIN ORIGIN.</p>
        <p>Of the names that conic to us from a Latin source we have Anthony (worthy of praise), Augustus (venerable). This was a supple- mentary name of the first of the Imperial Ctesars. The month of August was so called is a compliment to Ctesar Augustus. Clement means merciful, hence clemency; feminine, Clementina. Constantine (firm), Felix (happy), Lionel, Martin (warlike), Oliver and its feminine Olivia, Valentine (healthy), Vin- cent (conquering), Beatrice (making happy), Clara (famous), Julius and its feminine Juha, Ursular (bear), Mathilda rith its diminutive Maud (mighty battlemaid).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0272" n="art0272">
        <head>GREEK ORIGIN.</head>
        <p>GREEK ORIGIN.</p>
        <p>Among the names derived from the Greek, either directly or indirectly, we have the fol- lowing : Alexander (helper of Inez)), very com- mon in Scotland, with its contraction Alec+ or Sandy, or, as :English people always call the latter, Sawney ; Andrew (courageous), Scot- land's patron Saint; Christopher (a Christ bearer).</p>
        <p>George would seem at first sight to be of Saxon origin, but it is Greek, and signifies husbandman. Virgil' s writings on husbandry are called his " Georpr,ies." • Hector (stout de- fender). Theodore, with its feminine Doro- thy by transposition, signifies the gift of God. Giles, at one time a common name in rural England, means a little goat or kid; possibly was first used by some rustic mother in old Greece, who gave as a vet name to her child the name of kicl. Erasmus (amiable), Eugene</p>
        <p>(well born), Eustace (happy in harvest), Nicholas (victory of the people), Philip (lover of horses). _ _</p>
        <p>Amongst our female names of Greek origin we have Agatha (good, kind), Agnes (sacred), Caitherine (pure), Helen (light), Helena, Mar- garet (a pearl), Phyllis (greenleaf or bough), and Sophia (wisdom).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0273" n="art0273">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0274" n="art0274">
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="1" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0275" n="art0275">
        <head>HEBREW ORIGIN.</head>
        <p>HEBREW ORIGIN.</p>
        <p>From the Hebrew we derive many of the best known Christian names, and many of these have been used as surnames. These, however, did • not appear until the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries.</p>
        <p>There are only two men answering to the Scriptural name of John in Doomsday Book, and one of these was a foreigner. During the sixteenth century, at the time of the Puritan and other movements largely religious, Bible names were very popular. We had Puritan maids in plenty, with such names as Patience, Faith, Hope and CharitY: I have before no a few pages of a register of a. Friends' Meeting Houae. There are in it thirty-one names of females. Of these twenty-five are Scriptural, and, Hannah, Mary, Ruth, Sara, and Judith predominate. It is difficult to know why so many Quaker maidens' should he called Judith. I must con- fess that I did not know where this lady lived in the Scriptures. 1 found a Judith in the Apocrypha, but she appears to have had a rather mixed reputation. I then discovered that Esau's wife was called Judith. I sup- pose .the Friends, and,many others, entertain a secret regard for, and sympathy with, the disinherited son; hence some have given the name of Esau's. wife to one of their children. Curious it is there is not a single Rachel, the name of ,Jacob's wife, in this list.</p>
        <p>One family having exhausted in their sons the four Evangelists, Matthew, Mark, Luke and John, on the arrival of the fifth wanted to call him Acts, but were dissuaded. Another went to the Old Testament, and alighted upon the word Alas, whom they believed to be the brother of the prophet, " Alas 1 my brother." It is recorded, but scarcely believed, that parents brought their child to the font and asked that he should be called Beelzebub, but this was too much for the parson, and the name was changed. We have amongst our Hebrew names Adam (red earth), Abraham (father of a multitude), Isaac (laughter), Benjamin (son of the right hand)," Daniel (the judge), David (beloved), Gabriel (hero of God), Jacob (supplanter), Jeremiah (exalted of time, Lord), Jonathan, Matthew, Samuel (heard of God), and Thomas (twin). Among the feminine names we have Hannah (gracious), Johannah, Joan (the Lord's grace), Mirian, Ruth and Sarah. Some of Cromwell's Ironsides had to endure Scriptural terms, such as " Fight against Sin," " Grace Triumphant," etc.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0276" n="art0276">
        <head>NEW BOOKS. •</head>
        <p>NEW BOOKS. •</p>
        <p>Tho Progressive Movement. By B. P. Do Witt. London: Macmillan and Co., Ltd.</p>
        <p>This is another volume in that excellent " Citizen's Library " edited by Professor It. T. Ely, and published by the Macmillan Com- pany,. of New York. It deals, of course, with political conditions in U.S.A. It is a study of a niovement which is not confined to any one party. • The progressive movement, as Mr. De Witt says in his preface, is broader than the Progressive party, and, in fact, than any single party. . It is the embodiment end expression of fundamental measures and prin- ciples of reform that have been advocated for many years by all political parties. The book is divided into four parts dealing respectively with "Thu Origin and Development of the Progressive Movement," " The Progressive Movement in the Nation," "The Progressive Movement in the State," and "The Progres- sive Movenient in the City." Mr. De Witt treats of the many reforms that the Progres- sive Movement seeks to effect, many of them, we think, applicable, or at leastadaptable, to our own conditions. Mr. De _Witt is very sympathetic in his treatment of the tentative solutions which the Progressive Movement offers to the difficult problems of sOCial life. He sees that the effect of the war may be " to divert the attention of people from progres- sive reforms for a time. But, sooner or later, that attention Will return, more earnest and more intense, and the principles of the mo ement will receive a new emphasis and a wider application." England is now at con- siderable disadvantage, compared with America, in this matter. Saddled with the enormous debt which the war will entail, ham- pered with the special problems which will arise when it is over, we shall not be in the Position to experiment as fully on the lines suggested hy Mr. De Witt as we should have been able to do had there been no war. No one can surmise when those measures that, two years ago, we hoped to have had m operation by now, vill once more become ques- tions of practical politics. But all the more desirable is it, in our opinion, that suli books as this of Mr. De Witt should circulate widely among our -people that they may become acquainted with the political and domeqcic problems of other countries—so similar, yet with certain important differences to our own _and the measures proposed for solving them. We cordially recommend Mr. De Witt's work.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0277" n="art0277">
        <head>PORRITT AND RATHBONE. Warringtonians Meet Accidentally In Ontario.</head>
        <p>PORRITT AND RATHBONE. Warringtonians Meet Acciden- tally In Ontario.</p>
        <p>In the ForeSt, (Ontario) Free Press for September 23rd we read the following interest- ing account of the meeting of two former War- rington residents:— Sampling Forest Peaches.</p>
        <p>Jackson Rathbone, who is well-known as an expert in the steel trade at Sheffield, Englanc and •,also at several centres of the crueible steel industry in the United States,. spent Sunday. in Forest. Mr. Rathbone, who has been living for the last four or five months at Sarnia, came to town to spend Sunday with Edward Porriik, of Hartford, Connecticut, who during the last few days has been a guest of " The Free Press " Office, Forrest. Messrs. Porritt and Rathbone whose home folks were for over twenty years neighbours in A rpley-street, Warrington, Lan- cashire, met quite by accident on the Recreation Pier on the St. Clair River at Sarnia, on Hie !previous Sunday, September 12th. Mr.. Porriti left Warrington in 1880. Mr. Rathbone .left there to go to Sheffield seventeen years ago. Tlw visiting Englishmen, in company with the Editor of the " Free Press," spent a good part of Sunday morning in the famous peach orchards of Martin Sadler. Mr. Rathbone photographed the heavily-laden peaeh trees. He also had a more immediate and more per- sonal use for some of Mr. Sadler's finest hand- selected precinct.- The peach orchard party returned to the Editor's home on. Albert-street, by way of the flax mill. As Mr. Rathbone was crossing the Indian compound—where the flax company houses its Chippeway workpeople, and their numerous and bonnie pappooses—he casually remarked that he " rather lik Forest—especially the Sadler peach orchards.'</p>
        <p>63 Stockton Heath</p>
        <p>The County Motor Co. Stockton Heath Garage, Warrington.</p>
        <p>4 L</p>
