      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0280" n="art0280">
        <head>CHRISTMAS NUMBERS.</head>
        <p>CHRISTMAS NUMBERS.</p>
        <p>WAR TIME HAS ONLY ENHANCED THEIR ATTRACTIVENESS.</p>
        <p>War-time conditions have but enhaue3,l the attractiveness of the Christmas. Nuicbers, which are now steadily making their appearance. For the most part, war subjects are well mixed With subjects less grim and more cheerful, while • the all-absorbing topic is treated in the best possible spirit. S. PEARS' TWENTY-FIFTH ANNIVERSARY. " Pears' Annual " for 1915 is. the 25th issue of a Christmas number that jumped into popu- larity with the, first number, and has maintained its position in the first rank of such publica- tions ever since. For the whole twenty-five years this bright anti cheery Christmas visitor has been welcomed in hundreds of thousands of British homes. The present celebration number is of outstanding 'interest anti value, giving as its literary fare a collection of stories by eminent writers, while, on the art side, on which it has always made an especially high appeal, its attractions are of a very liberal and distinguished order. There are six presentation ptes, the largest being a superb mezzogravure "The Beauty of the Glade," by. A. Dampier May. There are beautiful reproductions in colour of paintings by .T. Shaw Crompton, Arthur J. Blsley,, R. 1., and R. Anderson, and a pair of coloured Bartolozzi ,prints after famous pictures by F. Wheatley. Further art features are four coloured pages illustrating Old Christ- mas Carols. The tales by Max Pemberton, J. Cutcliffe Ilyne, Barry Pain, W. 14. George and J. J. Bell, all of which' are fully illustrated 'in colours and tints by well-known artists, are fully up to the high reputation which this annual has ,so long enjoyedr. The whole is enclosed in a picturesqUe Chrstmassy cover in colours after a drawing by I rank Dadd, R.I. "Pears' Annual" for 1915 is in every sense worthy of the success it so bounteously cele- brates. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0281" n="art0281">
        <head>"THE QUEEN."</head>
        <p>"THE QUEEN."</p>
        <p>" The Queen" double Christmas Number has otice again a unique Feature, this time in the shape of a beautiful coloured picture on canvas, " The Foster Alotlisr." by Arthur J. Elsliv. After nearly eighteen months of war it is natural to turn for relief from the continued study of horrors to which we have be- come accustomed to simplicity and nature, awl this is why the proprietors present this year with the Christmas number a picture of country life, one Of the most charming which. the celebrated artist, Mr. Arthur J. Elsley, has ever painted. It serves to remind us that, while Europe is distracted, the sweet work of Nature goes 'on without interruption. As is usual. "Tile Queen" prents first-class fare on both its literary and artistic sides.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0282" n="art0282">
        <head>" HOLLY LEAVES."</head>
        <p>" HOLLY LEAVES."</p>
        <p>" Holly Leaves," the Christmas number of " The Illustrated Sporting and Dramatic News," true 'to its martial -spirit even in the most peaceful of years, gives a large photo- gravure supplement, a reproduction of Lady Butler's painting " Halt!" the original of which is now in possession of H.M. the King. No reproductions of this splendid picture havo hitherto been obtainable and, although the photogravure now offered is sold at a nominal price, the print has, we believe we may venture to say, been turned out in a style which rivals highly-priced engravings, does every justice to the original painting, and is admirably suited for framing. "PUNCH ALMANACK."</p>
        <p>This Almanack deals almost exclusively with the war, but it does so in " Punch's " inimit- able style. The Germans are made the butt of most telling witticisms, and if ridicule really killed there would not be a -German left soon to continue the struggle for the l'ttherland. There are jokes pictorial and verbal at our own expense; but " Punch " tells its sad news merrily throughout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0283" n="art0283">
        <head>" WINTER'S PIE."</head>
        <p>" WINTER'S PIE."</p>
        <p>We are glad to hear that that ever-weleomc annual, "'Winter's Pie," will be published on Monday, the '22nd inst., and, judging by the following list of eminent authors and artists who have contributed to its pages, it will un- doubtedly come as a cheery gleam to those on active service, in hospitals and camps, or at lome. There are stories by G. B. Burgin, Egerton Castle, .Dion Clayton Calthrop, the late Walter.- Emanuel, Athol Forbes, Captain Harry Gra ham, Kehl° Howard, Arthur Lambton, Sir Henry Lucy, William Le Queux, Lieut.-Colonel Newham-Davis,- Barry Pain. J. C. Percy; Mos- tyn 'l'. Pigott, W. Pett Ridge, Ethel and Adrian loss, George lt. Minis, J. Ashby-Sterry, and Katharine Tynan. Drawings are contributed by. Mabel Luck Attwell, Bateman, Lewis Daumer. George 'Belcher, Lieut. I. 0. 0: ,Beuttler, E. Blampied, A. S. Boyd, 11. M. Brock, Rene Bull, Fred Buchanan,. DudleY Buxton, Leo Cheney. Hilda Cow ham, Charles Crombie, Hesketh Daubeny, Harold Earnshaw, Charles Folkard, P. Fraser, Frederick Garnett, Charles Grave, John HasSall, Charles Harrison, W. K. Basel- den, Chris Heaps, Thomas Henry, P. B. Hick. hug, E. -Huskinson, Charles Ince, Harry Low, Savile Lumley, Wilmot Lunt, Mac Wilson, A. Wallis Mills, Will Owen, Charles Pears,, L. Raven Hill, Frank Reynolds, Harry Rountree, W.-Heath Robinson, Rilette. Byam Shaw. J. A. Shepherd, E. H. Shepard, .0. E. Studdy, Lance Thackeray, H. Thorpe, Godfrey Wilson, Law. son Wood, and Starr Wood.</p>
        <p>In this issue will appear Mr. W. Pett appreciation of the late Mr. W. Hugh Spottis- woode,' with a .copy of his latest portrait "Winter's Pie" is published by the Sphere and Taller, Ltd., and obtainable at all book- sellers and bookstalls, Is. net.</p>
      </div>
