      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0286" n="art0286">
        <head>FARM AND GARDEN.</head>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>TOP-DRESSING WHEAT IN AUTUMN.</p>
        <p>As a means of increasing the supply of home- grown wheat, a return to the old practice of top- dressing the wheat crop with nitrogenous manures in autumn may be recluimended. Long ago, LaweS and Gilbert, as the result of their first twenty years' experieti: at Rothamsted, 4ame to -the conclusion that with land of poor Air moderate quality an increase of from 5 to 6 bushels of wheat per acre, might be eipected from the application of lcwt. of sulphate of .ammeinia in autumn. This conclusion had reference to quantities of .13 and 861 b. of nitrogen per acre (equal to the amounts present in dressings Of 2cwt. to . 4cwt. sulphate of .ammonia) and to soils which without a nitro- .genous'- manure were capable of yielding from 18 to 28 bushels of wheat per. acre. In the Board's " Journal " for September, Dr. E. J. Russell, the present- Director of Rothanasted, pointed out that for the long period of 61_ years A dressing of ammonia salts equal to about 2cwt. sulphate of • ammonia has increased the wheat crop by 8.7 bushels and that double the dressing. has produced 17.6 busheLs; this i•icrease is at the rare of nearly 4, bushels of 'wheat per cent of sulphate of am- monia. For reasons which need not be discussed here, there have been very few 'experftments on Ihe autunin top-dressing of wheat, and there is no extensive series of tests on land under ordinary rotation by which we can check these estimates based on the per- manent wheat plots at Rothamsted.</p>
        <p>KEEP THE WHEAT PLANT GROWING.</p>
        <p>A forecast of the season cannot be made, but a study of the.effects of weather on 'field crops and of the influence of past seasons on the yield of wheat furnish hints for those who are en- deavouring to grow a,heavy crop in 1916. An examination of the Rothamsted records shows that the finest wheat crop ever grown there was that of - the year '1863. The character of the weather of this harvest year was :—November cold and dry, December and January mild and moderately moist, February and March mild and dry, April very dry and warm, May and June cool with a sufficiency of rain, and July dry and bright. Commenting on this wonderful *heat crop, Lawes and Gilbert Say "the.. extra- ordinary result was due to the almost unchecked grolith from the first appearance of the plant above ground up to the-time of harvest rather than to any extraordinary -characteristics Of the Season?' This statement supplies a hint for the farmer who desires to produce a bumper crop Of wheat; it is obvious that in 1916 "keep the plant growing" should be his maxim.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0287" n="art0287">
        <head>WINTER FEED FOR DAIRY COWS.</head>
        <p>WINTER FEED FOR DAIRY COWS.</p>
        <p>The cost• of keeping a cow in full milk per week during the winter depends on the supply of home-grown foods available, and the outlay incurred in purchased feeding-stuffs. Hay and grass are the natural foods of the cow, and when winter is open ana there is some grass left on a sheltered pasture, she can be wintered at. a small 'cost. Some cows are large eaters, but, on an average, from 25 to 301 b. of hay will suffice- for the daily wants of an animal of this class. It is becoming more and more the prac- tice of eowkeepers to rely on other foods as well as hay, and to make up a ration which, though costing less, is equally if nor more effective. One cannot lay down rules to meet every case, but probably for general tie noth- ing would answer better than equal parts of hay and oat straw chaffed, mixed with a sprink- ling of brewers' grains and a bushel of crushed oats per week. The cows, if kept constantly .in the house, would be fed three times daily, the meals being placed at equal distances apart dur- ing the day. The brewors' grains should be. sufficient to damp the mass, of chaff and corn; and would be fed at the morning and mid-day meals, while at night the animals could recei,,e a feed of hay or ,oat straw. If a. plot of autumn cabbage could be grown it should be passible to 'procure at least one-half of the cows' food in a green -state until nearly Christmas, ex- cept when the land is frost-bound. In this case the brewers' grains would be dispensed with. Another Plan would be to use purped swedes' during the early part of the winter, and mangelS from February onwards, to be succeeded with rye in April.—Staffordshire, in "Farm and Rome."</p>
        <p>THE ENLISTMENT OF FARM WORKMEN.</p>
        <p>The President of the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries desires to call the special attention of farmers and agricultnrists generally to the new arrangements made under Lord Derby's' 'recruiting scheme whereby men can either enlist for .immediate service. with the Colours, or can be attested, grouped according to their age and .condition, i.e., married or single, passed at once into Section B, Army Reserve, and returned to their civil Occupation. Lord Selborne strongly advises skilled agricul- tural workers whose labour is really indispens- able on the farm, but who yet may feel a strong</p>
        <p>impulse to join the Army that they should not attempt to enlist for immediate service, but that they should be attested and passed into the Reserve, and he recommends farmers and all who have influence over such men to persuade them to adopt this course, while encouraging the immediate enlistment of men who are not in- dispensable. Indispensable men so attested will never be called up for service without an oppor- tunity being given through the local tribunals set up by the Local Government Board for con- sideration whether they ought not to be retained on the farms, and meanwhile they will be en- titled as soldiers in the Reserve to wear a khaki armlet which will be given to them by the Military Authorities. The matter is of urgeut importance in view of the Martinmas hirings when men will be for the moment unemployed, and when special efforts will doubtless be ma de by the recruiting officers to obtain recruits for the Army.</p>
      </div>
