      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0290" n="art0290">
        <head>TURKEYS FOR CHRISTMAS.</head>
        <p>TURKEYS FOR CHRISTMAS.</p>
        <p>Turkeys intended for killing at Christmas, says "Middlesex," in "Farm and Home," should receive special treatment during the latter half of November and the first three weeks of December. Upon the management during the last five or six weeks of a turkey's life a great deal depends, and if the birds are to realise the best prices they must be suitably and generously fed. Turkeys take about five weeks to fatten, so that towards the end of the third week of November the process should com- mence. The birds should be confined in large roomy sheds, well lighted and ventilated, where they will remain the whole. time. A good form of house is one having the side facing south, I enclosed with wire-netting only, so that whilst the birds within the shed are well protected and warm, there is a plentiful supply of pure, fresh air. Perches about a couple of feet from the ground should be provided, and the floor should be well covered with peat moss litter or a similar material._ A variety of foods may be used for the purpose of fattening, some of which tend to the production of the finer quality flesh than others. The best is undoubtedly ground oats, but being a somewhat expensive food, it only pays to use when a first-class trade is being supplied. The ground oats should be mixed with sweet milk, and milk instead of water given for drinking. Other meals employed for the purpose are barley meal and middlings, either singly or mixed together in equal pro- portions, which form good quality flesh, but not of the same texture or flavour as the ground oats. One part barley - meal and one part potatoes is a cheaper mixture, and is suitable for producing birds intended for a second-class market. Soft food should be fed to the turkeys in the morning, and grain in the afternoon, pre- ferably that which has been soaked in water for a few hours. This has the effect of making it more easily digestible. The best grains are wheat, bailey and oats. A supply of fresh water, grit and green food should at all times</p>
        <p>be available for the birds, without which they will never thrive well.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0291" n="art0291">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>DEEP CULTIVATION.</p>
        <p>This is important and is all the more notice- able in a dry season. Compare the crops grow- ing on the soil that has been dug only one spit deep and those from ground that has been deeply trenched and well manured. Now that many of the breaks are vacant, trenching should be carried out. Many people make the mistake of bringing the bottom soil to the top, the result being poor crops. The reason is obvious, as what in many cases has been brought to the sur- face is crude soil with no life in the shape of vegetable• matter in it. The young plants struggle on till they reach the good soil once on the .surface, but which has been buried in the bottom of the trench. By far the best plan is to leave this crude soil where it is, fork it up, then lay on this a good dressing of manure, put- ting the top soil over this. Given this treat- ment the young seedlings get a good start and very soon their roots find their way to the manure and the loose soil, and in consequence do not feel the effects of a dry season.- Ground thus treated should not carry the first year after trenching any top-rooted vegetables, as it is known that the roots become forked and their value thus considerably reduced.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0292" n="art0292">
        <head>SOIL TRENCHING.</head>
        <p>SOIL TRENCHING.</p>
        <p>Winter is the time for trenching and deepen- ing the soil, and all vacant plots should have attention. No hard and fast line can be laid down about bringing up any of the subsoil, all depending on its character and the labour available. Every bit of waste matter should be collected and worked up into compost and all prunings and old bushes being burned and mixed with the soil as the trenching proceeds. One often sees weeds being trenched in, but this is a mistake unless they can be buried very deeply. Far better throw them on the fire heap and so destroy all seeds. Once the practice has begun of thoroughly turning soils topsy-turvy from some 21 feet to 3 feet deep then splendid crops follow, not merely because the roots are better able to go down, but also because when deep down they are less influenced by summer heat and drought than those which are near the sur- face.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0293" n="art0293">
        <head>CLIPPING SHRUBS.</head>
        <p>CLIPPING SHRUBS.</p>
        <p>At this season of the year, when gardens are being cleared of the summer bedding plants and tidied up for the winter it is distresving to see how the many beautifnl evergreens we now have are being hacked about. It is of course neces- sary to clear off all decaying plants and the over-luxuriant shoots of climbers, but why mutilate evergreens that are so cheerful in the winter? Take the case of the many varieties of euonymus we have. HOW often does one see these trimmed with the shears into mop-headed specimens, devoid of all beauty? If it is neces- sary to restrict them to any specified height or size it could as easily be done with the knife thinning out the shoots and leaving the younger shoots with the foliage intact. This mania fo.• clipping is copied by nearly all the owners of small gardens, who think it is the correct thing. Such mutilation destroys the beautiful form that these shrubs would assume if left to grow naturally Unfortunately, it is not only evergreens that are treated in this barbarous way, as one all too often sees some of our most beautiful flower- ing shrubs including the lilac so mutilated that in their season they haVe no flowers, and people wonder why this is so.—Wick. in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0294" n="art0294">
        <head>FOOD PRODUCTION.</head>
        <p>FOOD PRODUCTION.</p>
        <p>How Country Children May Help. ,</p>
        <p>Children living in country districts can assist in increasing the homA production of food in many ways. At present, if their parents keep pigs .or other live stock, they can help to reduce the cost of feeding by collecting acorns and horse chestnuts, rough grass, &amp;c., for use as fodder. They can also help to economise the use of straw for litter by collecting dry bracken, grass, leaves and reeds. Suggestions for the use of these materials are given in leaflets issued by the Board of 'Agriculture 'and Fisheries, viz., Special Leaflet No '9 (Acorns, Horse Chestnuts and Beechmast), Special Leaflet No.. 10 (Pig- keeping for Cottagers and Smallholders), Special Leaflet No. 34 (Autumn and Winter Fodder), and Special Leaflet No. 38 (Use of Bracken as Litter). Copies of these leaflets may be obtained free of charge and post free on application to the Board, Whitehall-place, London, S.W. Board. of Agriculture and Fisheries, Whitehall-place, London, S.W., , 15th November, 1915.</p>
        <p>POULTRY FOR FRENCH FARMERS.</p>
        <p>A gift of over 1,500 head of poultry to the farmers and peasants of the devastated areas of France is being consigned this month by the Agricultural Relief of Allies Committee.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0295" n="art0295">
        <head>TIGON CAPSULE&amp;</head>
        <p>A TIGON CAPSULE&amp;</p>
        <p>- Dr. LERMONT'S scientific preparation gives instantaneous relief in all Bladder and Urethal trouble. ,Contains no Cubeb or Sandal Wood Oil, thereby avoiding that objection- able nauseous repeat, and more effectively killing the goenococci germs. All sufferers from such complaints are advised to take this remedy. To be obtained only from the Sole Agent in Great Bi:itain, post free, in sealed boxes, 2/6 and 3/6, from A. DARCH, Chemist, 84, PRAED STREET, LONDON, W. ,</p>
        <p>On the Lovely-lane ground, before a small number of spectators. The game commenced very evenly and chiefly in midfield. Whenever either team made an attempt to attack their opponents' goal, the backs proved very safe,</p>
