      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0296" n="art0296">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 1915.</p>
        <p>good passing, took the ball into the vicinity oi their opponents' line. The Bank Quay lads, however, played a great defensive game. aud kept the foe at bay. At length the Sac/.d heart full-back received the ball, and with a good - punt cleared his lines and again removed nay to midfield. Here a number of scrimmages -wore conteste,pl, the ball at last coming out to the Sacred Heart half-back, who inaugurated a good passing movement, which resulted in the ball being transferred to St. Mary's line, and a good try being scored at the corner. The goal- kick was a failure. From the restart the " Hearts " attacked 'strongly, but glorious chances were missed through each individual trying to do too much on his own, instead of playing the combined game and passing the ball out, as did the opposing team. The Sacred Heart were penal- ised and a good goal was recorded against them. The final score read :—Sacred Heart, 1 try (3 points); St. Mary's, .1 goal (2 points).</p>
        <p>and for some time they easily held the for- I wards at bay. Rain began to fall rather heavily I and play became very uninteresting, and was mostly along the touch lines. As the interval was drawing near the Athletic rallied and made a strong attempt to capture the Albions' citadel. From a throw-in Edwards received the ball and opened the score for the Athletic. Half-time: Athletic, 1 goal; Albions, nil. The Athletic, with the wind in their favour, started .in brisk fashion, and their extreme wing players got in some good centres. For a foul throw the Athletic were given a free kick, and the Albion goal wits placed in danger. After a scrimmage in their goalmouth the ball was forced out for a corner kick, the latter being cleared. Kay made a grand individual run for the Albions, and his final shot hit the Athletic crossbar. Play was quickly transferred to the opposite end, where Payton scored the second goal. fter exciting play, Edwards scored a third goal with a low shot. The Albions now played more Strenuously, and succeeded in re- ducing their arrears. The . remainder of . the game was chiefly in the Albions' half, and time being called, the result was: Bewsey Athletic, 3 goals; Bewsey Albions, 1 goal.</p>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH SCOUTS v. HOWLEY</p>
        <p>UNITED.</p>
        <p>On the ground of the former at Higher Wal- ton'. The United, with the advantage of a strong wind, keenly attacked, but owing to the good defensive play of. Foster, the danger was cleared. Then the home team broke away. Kenwright, making a good individual run, placed the United goal in jeopardy, and when Solan received the ball the United goalkeeper rushed out to clear, with the result that Solan was injured and did not play in his usual style afterwards. From the goal kick the United again made an attack on the Scouts' citadel, and their centre-forward passed to Potter, when well placed. The latter scored after twenty minutes' play. The game was new very keenly contested, but the Scouts failed to get on equal terms. Half-time: United, 1 goal; Scouts, nil. In the second portion the Scouts were the first to show up prominently. Tl*y. made strenuous efforts to make the scores equal, but the United defence were in fine form, and stub- bornly resisted all attacks, and gradually drove the Scouts back. In the closing stages both teams renewed their attacks, but the defence proved superior, and no further goals were scored. Result: Howley United, 1 goal; Stock- ton Heath Scouts, nil.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0297" n="art0297">
        <head>4- 4- 4. 4. 4... 4- 4. 4. 4.. 4. 4. 4. 4.. 4.. 4. 4.. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. • • • THE FOOTBALL FIELD * .</head>
        <p>4- 4- 4. 4. 4... 4- 4. 4. 4.. 4. 4. 4. 4.. 4.. 4. 4.. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. 4. • • • THE FOOTBALL FIELD * .</p>
