      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0298" n="art0298">
        <head>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</head>
        <p>INTERMEDIATE LEAGUE.</p>
        <p>TABLE UP TO NOVEMBER 13th, 1915</p>
        <p>(INCLUSIVE).</p>
        <p>Points P. W. L. D. F. A. P. . 8 7 1 0 63 26 14 . 8 6 1 1 115 9 13 . 8 4 2 2 43 41 10 . 8 4 3 133 31.9 . 6 4 2 0 50 15 8 . 6 3 1 2 25 17 8 . 7 3 2 2 37 25 8 . 9 3 6 0 22 55 6 . 8 2 6 0 33 42 4 . 8 2 6 0 22 71 4 . 8 0 8 0 131 20 0</p>
        <p>Heathside Parochial Rylands's St. James' Silver-street Bolton St. Thomas' St. Mary's Sacred Heart Beamont Whitecross</p>
        <p>LAST SATURDAY'S RESULTS.</p>
        <p>EL Thomas', 7 points; Parochial, 0. ltylands's, 3; Bolton, 3.. Ileathside, 3; Whitecrtiss, 2. Sacred Ileart, 3; St. Mary's, 2. Silver-street, 11; Beamont, 0.</p>
        <p>RYLAN DS'S RECS. v. BOLTON OLD BOYS. On the Gorsey-lane enclosure, before a very poor attendance 'Viands's won the toss and commenced with the wind in their favour. The Bolton team had at once to defend their lines owing to the fine form of the Recs.' forwards. The Bolton fullrback relieved by a huge punt, which sent the Recs. back to their own half, • where a series of scrunimages took place, Bolton being penalised for offside tactics. ,Edwards was forced to carry over, but nothing ensued from the scrimmage. Rylands's kept Bolton penned in, and a little later Edwards, taking a flying kick when pressed, clean missed and Ryiands's would have scored had not the water impeded their progress and thus ,given the Bolton defence time to recuperate. Soon after Rylands's again missed an opportunity; their forwards took the ball away and after getting clear kicked too strongly, and the ball rolled dead. Bolton now had a spell at Rylands's end, but were soon forced back and were a little later penalised for offside, Archer failing to give his side the lead. After some scrappy play A. Jones made a fine effort for Bolton, beating man after man before kicking; Archer just arriving in time to make dead. This inspired Bolton, and play till the interval remained at Rylands's 'line, but no score was recorded. Interval: No score.</p>
        <p>On resuming Rylands' came 'away and for a short period kept Bolton busy, but Edwards relieved by a judicious kick. From a pack about midfield, W. Jones secured and passed over to his brother, Bate and Wright also handled, the latter being forced into touch near the corner. A little later Archer, the Rylands's back, secured about the twenty-five line, and after run- ning strongly, found touch at the corner. From a scrum on the Bolton line, Mulvey, of Rylands's, dashed over for a try, the goal-kick failing. Restarting, Bolton forwards dribbled well and Price almost scored, but Archer saved finely, picking up from the feet of the opposing forwards he found touch about midfield, where play remained for some time until A. Jones, picking up in the loose, made a good run, and when met by Archer kicked and just scored at the corner, no goal resulting. Bolton now kept play at the railway end, and in quick succession Cooper almost forced over and A. Jones almost scraped in at the corner. Shortly before time Rylands's were penalised but Bate missed the goal-kick, the game ending :—Rylands's, 1 try. three points; Bolton, 1 try, three points.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0299" n="art0299">
        <head>Featutes of the Game.</head>
        <p>Featutes of the Game.</p>
        <p>The features of the game were the great amount of work Rylands's forwards got through and the fine full-back play of T. Archer;_ tae Bolton half-backs (who completely beat Rylands's half-backs) and the defence of their full-back.</p>
        <p>There was not much in the two teams, Bolton, if anything, were more methodical, but vary, little football could be expected under the circumstances. Of the backs, Archer was the better. He kicked and fielded finely, while Edwards was uncertain and a trifle erratic, trusting too much to fly kicking when pressed. The three-quarters were pretty equal, but at half the brothers Jones easily outclassed Sloane and Littlewood. In the forward rank Rylands's were slightly better. Their forwards heeled lUt well and thus gave their backs opportunities which, however, were not materialised, -while in the loose they adapted themselves better to the conditions than did the Latchford pack. • Prior to the start the Bolton team asked that the match might be abandoned because of the [amount of water on'the ground, but the referee replied4hat the game would be played</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0300" n="art0300">
        <head>SILVER-STREET v. BEAMONT.</head>
        <p>SILVER-STREET v. BEAMONT.</p>
        <p>Silver-street kicked off before a fair attendance of spectators, and at once niade for their opponents' line, but the Beamont lot by a good bit of play drove them back Both teams payed a great gara3; in fact many of the pass- ing movements were worthy cf our pre:nier team. Silver-street started a fine passing move- rc..ent. From the scram the ball travelkti from one player to the slier at a great pece, and Ratcliffe completely broke away, but was beaten for speed. At this stage both teams were play- ing on the offensive, but the dgfence won each time, the ball travelling from one end to tha other very quickly. Silver-street were playing the better game". Beamont kicked off but Silver-street now had the wind in their favour. They soon- began some fine passing movements, which kept Bea- mont, on the defensive. From a 'scrummage Callon gave to Radcliffe. The ball quickly re- turned to Callon and that player gained a splen- did try. Ratcliffe took the kick and was suc- cessful in placing a fine goal. Silver-street were now overplaying their opponents, and fraa some more fine passing Barlow received and com- pletely left them standing and scored a good try, the goal-kick failing. Soon after Hewitt received and scored again, Silver-street proving I winners by 1 goal 3 tries, 11 points, to nil. •</p>
        <p>PAROCHIAL'S DOWNFALL. Defeated By Lads From Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>Parochial met with their first defeat of the season on Victoria Park, where they were de- feated by St. Thomas', and, in consequence of this, lost their position \at the ;head of the League. They now occupy second place, Heath- side being in the premier position. The match was witnessed by a good number of spectators and proved most exciting. The result was totally unexpected even by the most ardent supporter from Stockton Heath. The records of the two teams ,during the season were such that only one result could be predicted, namely, a win for Parochial. But, as. usually happens, the unexpected happened, and St. Thomas' won by iheeclear margin of 7 points to nil. Though they have lost their first position, the record of the Parochial team. is worthy of notice. During the season they have scored 115 points, .whilst only nine have been registered against them.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0301" n="art0301">
        <head>HEATHSIDE'S NARROW WIN;</head>
        <p>HEATHSIDE'S NARROW WIN;</p>
        <p>The mateh. between Ileathside and White- cross, on Victoria Park, proved to be—contrary to expectations—a very tight affair, as may be judged by the fat that the "wooden spoonists" were only one point to the bad at the close .of hostilities. The game was brimful of excite- ment throughout, neither teams seeming to tire. From the kick-off Heathside attacked, but the Whitecross lads put Up a fine defence and pushed them back again. A number of scrummages were fought out in mid-field, which resulted in some good passing movements, all of which, however, came to nought. Heathside were again soon on the attack, and after a bout of flossing a good ,try was•recorded. The goal- kick was a failure. Stung by this reverse, Whitecross attacked strongly, and when in a good .position were awarded a free-kick. A goal was recorded, and no further scoring taking place Heathside retired victors by one try (three points) to one goal (two points).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0302" n="art0302">
        <head>SACRED HEART BEAT ST. MARY'S.</head>
        <p>SACRED HEART BEAT ST. MARY'S.</p>
        <p>This Auatch was played on the Wilderspool ground. St. 'Mary's kicked off and .for a time attacked most strenuously. They were, how- ever, gradually driven back, and play was for a time Confined to midfield. Eventually the " Saints " broke away again, and, by dia of'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0303" n="art0303">
        <head>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</head>
        <p>MATCHES FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>At Wilderspool.</p>
        <p>Parochial v. St. Mary's. Referee, Mr. J. Cooper. Kick-off 2.30 p.m. Sacred Heart v. Beamont. J. Beardsall. Kick-off 3.30 p.m.</p>
        <p>In Victoria Park. No. I.—St. James' v. Rylands's. Mr. G Crozier. Kick-off 3 p.m.</p>
        <p>No. 2.—St. Thomas' v. Silver-street. Mr. R. Williams. Kick-off 3 p.m. • . - No. 3.—Heathside v. Bolton. Mr. W. Bibby. Kick-off 3 p.m.</p>
      </div>
