        <p>HEATHSIDE JUNIORS v. SANKEY JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>Victoria Park, 2.30 p.m. Heathside: T. Cotterill; J. Dolphin, H. James, A. 'Lassall and J. Hinchliffe; J. Delooze and F. Hughes; C. Perks, P. Richmond, J. Worrall, P. Bradburn, J. Armstrong and R. Bate. Reserves: A. Healy and J. Bate. Sankey: T. Coleman; Davies, Wright (capt).. Wilson and Jackson; A. Clark and W. Weston; Hares, T. Clarke, W. Dunning, Hartlands, J. Lea and Corbett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0305" n="art0305">
        <head>COMING LEAGUE MEETING.</head>
        <p>COMING LEAGUE MEETING.</p>
        <p>There will be a meeting of the League on Thursday, December 2nd, at half-past seven in the Pavilion on the Football Ground. The b isiness will be important, and delegates are specially requested.</p>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION. The matches played on Saturday resulted as follows: Dewsbury, 7; Hull Kingston R., 0. Leeds, 15; Brumley, 3. Hull, 26; Batley, 5. Halifax, 10; Hunslet, 6. Featherstone, 10; Bradford, 8. Leigh, 5, St. Helens Recs., 0. Rochdale H., 8; Oldham, 2. Salford, 18; St. Helens, 5. Huddersfield, 17; Broughton R., 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0306" n="art0306">
        <head>ASSOCIATION.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION.</p>
        <p>STOKE RESERVE VISIT GREAT SANKEY</p>
        <p>TEAM.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and Distriot League ca Saturday, the Paddington United had as visitors the British Alumi•ilum F.C. The visitors were not fully represented, Banner being a notable absentee. The home team had much the best of the game. and ran out, easy winners by five clear goals.</p>
        <p>Most of the League clubs were nut engaged last week, but the Howley iTnited team, who last week played a very fine game against St. Elphin's, the leaders, succeeded in going cne better this time, and administered lefeat to the Scouts on their own meadow. This was a surprise to most of the other members of the League, but the United were smarting under the heavy defeat they sustained from the Scouts earlier in the season, and they were determined to have their revenge. To win by the only goal which was scored early in the first half, is sig- nificant proof of a hard and determined struggle. The United are to be congratulated on their success.</p>
        <p>The Latchford Cadets and the Padgate C.L.B. should have met last week, but qwing to some misunderstanding both teams were waiting for each other on their own grounds.</p>
        <p>There was a keen game in the rain and slush at Lovely-lane between the Bewsey Athletic and the Bewsey Albions. Only one point was scored in the first half, and that was gained by the Athletic. In the second portion they had more of the game, and scored two further points, and towards the close of the game the Albions made a final rally and succeeded in penetrating the Athletic's defence. At the call of time the Albions had to admit defeat.</p>
        <p>Last week the Great Sankey Club had the Stoke Reserves as visitors to Hood-lane. The ' home club were not able to . play the team as selected, and had Millward and Connor, who usually occupy positions as forwards, operating as full-backs. The forwards were shuffled about, and Foster, as an extreme winger, never does himself justice. The play did not reach a Nery high standard, and in some respects it was dis- appointing to many present. The home team failed to score from two, penalty kicks, and on the first occasion the kick had to be taken a second time, Foster having the unusual experi- ence of missing both attempts, and Aspinall the second. The visitors were handicapped by the uneven surface of the greund, and Brindle had many goad shots volleyed at him, which he dealt with successfully. Dennis, in the visitors' gOal, was well protected by a pair of good backs. Apart from the penalty kicks the game can be described as fairly equal, and no goals were scored.</p>
        <p>We are informed that Glover, Foster and Kelley, of the Great Sankey team. have en- listed, the two former in the Royal Garrison Artillery.</p>
        <p>R. Daniels, of the Latchford Cadets, has joined the Territorials.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON &amp; DISTRICT ASSO-</p>
        <p>CIATION JUNIOROLEAGUE.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY ATHLETIC v. BEWSEY ALBION</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0307" n="art0307">
        <head>TABLE TO DATE.</head>
        <p>TABLE TO DATE.</p>
        <p>Goals. P. W. L. D. F. A. P. St. Elphin's Stockton Heath Scouts 7 5 2 0 17 11 10 Fairfield United Res. 5 4 1 0 21 _7 S</p>
        <p>Howley United Bewsey Albions Bewsey A.F.C. Orford Juniors Latchford Cadets Mersey Juniors Padgate C.L.8.6 0 6 0 6 50 0</p>
        <p>fight in the evening somewhere in the Higher Walton neighbourhood. Next week teams representing the same Corps will .be engaged in League strife, when a very keen game is expected.</p>
        <p>TEAMS FOR TO-DAY.</p>
        <p>BEWSEY v. ORFORD JUNIORS.</p>
        <p>At Longford-road. Bewsey : IT. Bradbury; J. Stephens and T. Blease; J. Kirk, F. Roylance and D. Davies; N. Pearson, F. Payfon, B. Dickenson, J. Small- man and E. Edwards. Reserves.—J. Dribble and A. Ellis.</p>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE V. WIDNES ATHLETIC.</p>
        <p>At Victoria Park. Kick-off 3 o'clock. Emmanuel Institute: T. Knight; E. Crozier and S. Hughes (captain); F. Parr, E. Hardy and W. Ayres; forwards (selected from) Win. Eaton, F. Stubbs, T. Hughes, A. Oakes, W. Eaton and W. Booth. Team meet at the Institute by 2.15.</p>
        <p>ELMWOOD CONGREGATIONALISTS v. PADDINGTON. Elmwood Congregationalists: F. Roberts; H. Oxley and Jos. Phcenix; J. Mellor, H. Hazel- dine (captain) and H. Clayton; W. Littlewood, E. Kendrick, W. C. Ashton, W. Franklin and A. Holt. Reserves: W. Gibson and J. Molyneux. All players, meet at Elmwood Avenue at 2.15 prompt. CAIRO-STREET UNITARIANS v. ST. JOHN'S CHURCH. At Victoria Park. Kick-off 3 p.m. Unitarians.—S. Hayes; J. Hallam and W. Har- kinson; J. Whittaker, Whitlow and H. Hill; R. Mellor, Maney, T. Pilling, G. Bailey and A. 11. Mather. Reserves: J. Bailey and H. Ffalford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0308" n="art0308">
        <head>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</head>
        <p>FRIENDLY MATCHES.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY v. STOKE RESERVES. On the Hood-lane enclosure. There was a good crowd present when the following players turned out:— Groat Sankey: Brindle; Connor and Millward ; Millington, G. Whitfield and Ditchfield ; Aspinall, T. Whitfield, Siddall, Illidge and Foster.</p>
        <p>_ Stoke: Dennis; Bentley and Brooks; Hawkins, Clarke and Perry; Tompkinson, Ellis, Cooper, Greaves and Clayton. Referee, Mr. H. Smith, Latchford. The home team won the toss and Cooper set the ball rolling for the visitors,, with the win,' at their backs. The home team were the first to show signs of advance, when a mistake by Aspinall allowed Clarke to clear, and the visi- tors , became dangerous. T. Whitfield cleared. The visitors returned to the home goal when, Clayton centi)ing well, Thompson caused Brindle to,handle. The home forwards got away; and when in a dangerous position,' Midge was brought down. Foster taking the penalty-kiCk, hit the• upright, and the kick had to be taken again owing to some infringement. This 'time Dennis brought off a good save, but had to concede a corner-kick, which proved fruitless. The 'visitors' defence now got relief, and a visit was paid to the home goal, where IClayton tested Brindle with a hot shot, which was safely cleared. A free-kick against the visitors was cleared by Hawkins, and Clayton, gaining possession, raced away, and from his centre Ellis shot over the bar. Shortly after. T. Whitfield gave away a corner, which was got away. After even exchanges the home team gained ground by a throw-in. Dennis was called upon, but he proved safe. Then Tomp- kinson made a good run on the right, beating several opponents and causing Brindle to exert himself to save his goal. The home team played more strongly and Dennis had to save twice in quick succession, and directly after the home team were awarded another penalty for a foul on Whitfield. Aspinall took the kick and' shot too high. A visit to the home end saw Connor clear well, and when the interval was called play was in the front of the visitors' goal, but no goals hail been scored. The second half opened in favour of the home team, Aspinall being prominent on the right. Then even play in midfield followed, when Siddali beat Bentley for the ball and shot just wide of the goal. A misunderstanding between Millward and Ditchfield nearly let the visit()) s through, Brindle getting the ball away. Aspinc,ll raced away to the opposite end and forced a corner-kick, which was not improved upon. The game was again keenly contested in midfield, and the visitors got the best of the exchanges. Brindle had to run out of goal to clear. Then a, hot ado&amp; on the visitors goal was success- fully got away, but the home forwards soon returned and Dennis again proved too good for the opposing forwards. Tompkinson made a splendid run on the 'right and sent a lightning shot in, which just went past the wrong side of the post. The visitors were now pressing strongly, and the home defence showed up well. G. Whitfield passed neatly to Siddall, who beat</p>
        <p>he backs but failed to ;core. A few minutes</p>
        <p>Inter Dennis again saved well with several opponents close on him. The remainder of the game was evenly contested. The visitors were best served by Dennis, Bentle'y, Clarke, Tomp- kinson ad Clayton, and the home team by Brindle, G. Whitfield, Ditehfield, Aspinall an d Siddall. Result :—Great Sankey, 0; Stoke Reserves, 0.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0309" n="art0309">
        <head>CAIRO-STREET UNITARIANS v. WIDNES ST. AMBROSE.</head>
        <p>CAIRO-STREET UNITARIANS v. WIDNES ST. AMBROSE.</p>
        <p>At Victoria Park on Saturday. Both teams were strongly represented. Wid- nes pressed at the start; and showed better combination than the home team. After ten , minutes the visitors took the lead, through their centre-forward. This reverse roused the, Unitarians, and Mellor completed a brilliant run with an accurate centre, which Mather converted. The home forwards continued to. attack and Mather added 'a second. Half- time: Unitarians, 2 goals; St. Ambrose, 1 goal. The second half opened briskly, and both goals had narrow escapes. The home left wing forced a corner, which was nicely placed, the visitors putting through their own goal. Widne tried hard to reduce the lead, but were met with a dour defence, and Mellor settled the issued with a grand goal from twenty yards' range. Result: Cairo-street Unitarians, 4; Widnes St. Ambrose, 1. The home team's centre-half (Whitlow) played a great -game and was easily the best player- on the field. Maney and Mellor made a good right wing. For Widnes the centre-forward and right full-back were the Pick.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0310" n="art0310">
        <head>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE DEFEAT PEN KETH ALBION.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL INSTITUTE DEFEAT PEN KETH ALBION.</p>
        <p>These teams met at Penketh on Saturday, the result being a'one-sided•vietory for the visiting eleven. It was •evident practically from the kick-off that the Institute were the stronger team, and when the interval was reached they were three goals to the good, the Penketh nicu being outplayed at practically eyery•pcint, and and the advantage . ga.ined by "n.; means adequately representing the-.superiony. of the Emmanuel lads. In the second half the insti- tute piled on another four points, and when the final whistle sounded the sc3ro read:— lat.oruel, .7 goals; Penketh, rill. Goals wee s(-wed filr Emmanuel by Stub:)s T. 11 ughes. Eaton, Oakes and a substituze.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0311" n="art0311">
        <head>HOSPITAL SHIP SUNK.</head>
        <p>HOSPITAL SHIP SUNK.</p>
        <p>Steamer That Brought The King Home Mined.</p>
        <p>The Press Bureau issued the following state- ment on Wednesday night:— The War Office reports that the hospital ship Anglia struck a mine in the Channel to-day and sank. The total number on board was 13 officers and 372 other ranks, of whom about three hun- dred were saved by a patrol vessel. Another ship proceeding to the rescue was also sunk by another mine. • The names of those who lost their lives will be communicated to the next-of-kin and made public without delay. The Secretary of the Admiralty communicates the following telegram which the First Lord of the Admiralty has received from Lord Stam- fordham "The King is shocked to hear that the hospi- tal ship Anglia, which so recently conveyed him across the Channel, has been sunk by a mine. His Majesty is grieved at the loss incurred and trusts that the survivors have not unduly suf- fered from the terrible exposure to which they must have been subjected. Please express the King's heartfelt sympathy with the families of those who have perished. (Signed) STAMFORD- HAM." RESCUING SHIP BLOWN UP.</p>
        <p>The Press Association correspondent at a Channel port says : A steamer in the vicinity (which proved to be the Lusitania, of London) at once lowered -two boats to go to the assistance of the Anglia. Just after their boats had left the collier the men saw their, own vessel blown up, but were able to save the remainder of the crew, who alto- gether numbered about 25. The second Lusitania was really the first, hay- ing borne the name since 1903. She was a small steamer of 1,834 tons trading regularly between the Thames alld Spanish and Portuguese ports. Lloyd's. also reports that the British steamer Treneglos (3,886 tons, St. Ives) has been sunk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0312" n="art0312">
        <head>MANY COT CASES.</head>
        <p>MANY COT CASES.</p>
        <p>A torpedo-boat steamed to the scene and brought some of the rescued to port. Those saved include the matron, two nurses, and the captain. There were 'a large number of cot cases on board. The Anglia (1,862 tons) was a London and North-Western Railway steamer, and previous to being taken over by the Government was, like the lost transport Tara, on the Holyhead and Dublin service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0313" n="art0313">
        <head>LORD HALDANE'S DEFENCE</head>
        <p>LORD HALDANE'S DEFENCE</p>
        <p>Information Obtained And Acted Upon.</p>
        <p>Lord Haldane, speaking at Hampstead on Wednesday night, said he had been charged with not having warned the country and the Government about the war. Such information as he obtained—and it was a great deal—was communicated without a moment's delay to the Government, who not only took counsel but immediate action. Had that not been done we should not have commanded the seas to-day, and the Germans would have been in Paris, and perhaps Calais. The Struggle After the War.</p>
        <p>- -- Lord Haldane went on to say that there were matters about which he felt equally responsible if he did not try in 4 very humble fashion to warn his fellow countrymen, and one was about the tremendous significance of national educa- tion. An immense struggle must succeed the war, a struggle of the people of contending nations who were trying to save from wreck all they could and, more than that, get a lead in the future. We had hitherto the immense goodwill and advantage which arose from the possession of great resources, great wealth, and the lead in the commerce and industry of the world. But was it so clear that after this war was over we should still possess those advantages? Capital would be very scarce on this side of the Atlantic, and our competitors would be many, each looking forward to make up for the past and for getting their ascendancy in the future, and unless we did something more than put merely British courage and British influ- ence into the struggle, unless we did something to develop the British 'mind and put that into the struggle, tlien he, for one, frankly warned them he could not answer for the future of this country and the Empire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0314" n="art0314">
        <head>Question of Education.</head>
        <p>A Question of Education.</p>
        <p>It was a question of national education if only to put ourselves on the level on which some other nations were at this moment. That was the grave consideration, and it was not the only grave consideration. "We are face to face," continued Lord Haldane, "with the advent of democracy to power, a democracy which will be still more powerful after the war, when. everything has to be cleaned up and the ques- tion is to be put whether the old order of things should not finish and a new order of things take its place. We shall be face to face with the advent of democracy, and let us see. that we do our best to educate our \masters before they can exercise the mastery over us." We should sternly criticise every suggestion of economy in the teaching of children. He knew of no problem more pressing, more urgent, than the call for solution of this problem.</p>
        <p>The loafer has the longest hours and the shortest years.</p>
        <p>FOR</p>
        <p>Best House Coal NUTS &amp; SLACK, WASHED NUTS, BEANS, &amp; PEAS, CROMPTON &amp; SHAWCROSS, LTD.</p>
