      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0323" n="art0323">
        <head>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</head>
        <p>EMMANUEL CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Successful Sale Of Work To Help Funds.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work in aid of the funds of the Emmanuel Church, Bold-street, War- rington, was held on Wednesday, Thursday and Friday at the Institute, Knutsford-road. The first day's sale was opened by Mr. Percy W. Thompson, of Birkenhead. The Rev. William T. Jaye (incumbent) was in the chair, and,' in introducing the opener, he said that the Emmanuel Church was alone of its kind in Warrington. Its congregation was made up chiefly of the working classes, and it was really marvellous how the money was raised to carry on the Church. He explained the absence of the men of the church at that opening, saying that they were all engaged on Government work, either directly or indirectly. Before declaring the sale open, Mr. Thompson spoke of the old Mosaic law, which required the people to give one-tenth of their goods back to God. That, he observed, was a right thing to do. He proceeded to quote examplEs of people who had given one-tenth of their income and had been richly blessed in return. The munition work on which so many men of the congregation were apparently engaged was very remunerative work, and, that being so, he hoped that the men, when they had finished their work in the evening, would support the sale in a,worthy manner. He then declared the sale open. Mr. Thompson was thanked for his services on the motion of Mr. John Bibby, seconded by Mr. T. Whitehurst.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0324" n="art0324">
        <head>IS ESTABLISHED CHURCH ON DOWNWARD PATHS?</head>
        <p>IS ESTABLISHED CHURCH ON DOWN- WARD PATHS?</p>
        <p>On the second day the sale was re-opened by Mr. Thomas Heywood, of Manchester.</p>
        <p>- - After being •briefly introduced by the Rev. William T. Jaye, Mr. Heywood said that for many years he had heard of the work of the Emmanuel Church. in Warrington, and/it had often been his wish to come amongst them. From what he had, heard from Mr. Jaye he was sure that they were doing the right work in the right way. The right way was God's way. It, was necessary that in these times everything should be kept upon lines that were acceptable to God, and especially was that so in Christian work. Lately one had noticed that there had been a departure from the true Biblical way. The work of .the Reformed Church of England was a noble work and he trusted that God would provide the means for it to be carried on. Referring to sales of work, MP. Heywood said that they were necessary evils in Christian work. Mr. Heywood went on to say that the Church of England had been going on two downward paths. One was the path of ritualism—in the direction of the Church of Rome; and the other was the path of rationalism—which was • the path of infidelity. A vote of thanks was accorded to the opener, on the motion of Mr. E. Fludder, seconded by Mr. J. Bibby.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0325" n="art0325">
        <head>RECEIPTS.</head>
        <p>THE RECEIPTS.</p>
        <p>The reopening of the sale on Friday evening was purely formal, the Rev. William T. Jaye performing the ceremony. Good business was done and it is anticipated that the result will be quite up to the average. The receipts on the first day of the sale totalled £5O Os. Bd., and on the second day .P... 37 19s. 4d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0326" n="art0326">
        <head>STALLS.</head>
        <p>THE STALLS.</p>
        <p>The stalls were in charge of the following, who were assisted by many willing helpers:— Minister's Stall.—Mrs. Jaye and Mrs. Lees. Congregational Stall.—Mrs. A. Gandy and Mrs. Atherton.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Stall.—The Sunday School Teachers. Bible Class Stall.—Mrs. W. Bibby, jun. Refreshment Stall.—Mrs. Booth. Household Stall.—Miss N. Gandy. Flower and Fruit Stall.—Mr. Fludder. The tea room was under the management of Mrs. William Bibby, sen.</p>
        <p>CHARGE OF THE lilt RIFLES By RIFLEMAN C. F. WILSON.</p>
        <p>Now listen to me, ye true English lads; A story to you I'll unfold: How the King's Royal Rifles in the thick of the fight Inscribed their names in gold:</p>
        <p>For if ever a victory was more nobly won— Not one of the troops did fly— 'Twas by the boys of the King's Royal Rifles that morn Who took the trenches at Saint</p>
        <p>The morn broke bright, with a cloudless sky, With glorious refreshing air, As our gallants stood with bated breath And offered silent prayer.</p>
        <p>They waited for the moment when the gates • of Hell would loose For they knew what they'd got to face: A faint vision of home, then all is past, They waited in God's grace.</p>
        <p>At last the signal came to charge, And no pen or words can tell! With their heads well down, their bayonets sharp. And a cheer that rent the air—</p>
        <p>On, on, they went like Devils untied, No foe could face such men: So the enemy turned and fled like curs And the King's Royal Rifles had won again.</p>
        <p>The victory won—the roll was called— Alas! many a mother's son Laid down his life in that gallant charge Before that day was won.</p>
        <p>The names of such heroes WA must inscribe On the immortal roll of fame, For such as these have saved our homes, And saved old England's name.</p>
        <p>Now listen to me, you true British lads, An appeal to you I'll make! Enlist to-day, fill up the gaps For our brave heroes' sake!</p>
        <p>Rifleman C. F. Wilson, who sends us the above lines, resides at 26, Clifton-street, Latchford, and serves in the "Good Old Fighting Sixtieth. the K.R.R. in which so many Warrington lads have enlisted."</p>
        <p>NEW COUNTY TREASURER OF</p>
        <p>LANCASHIRE.</p>
        <p>Mr. Edwin Pomfrett, county auditor, was on Wednesday appointed county treasurer of Lan- cashire.</p>
        <p>w.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0327" n="art0327">
