      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0328" n="art0328">
        <head>EXAMINER, S ATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 1915. 101.UNTEER OR CONSCRIPT? IFARMERS AND WAR FUNDS! LOST TWO FINGERS.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, S ATURDAY, NOVEMBER 20, 1915. 101.UNTEER OR CONSCRIPT? IFARMERS AND WAR FUNDS! LOST TWO FINGERS.</p>
        <p>Something For Eligible Men To</p>
        <p>Ponder Over.</p>
        <p>The work of searching out and putting "the straight question" to the men of Warrington who are eligible for service in the Army, and who, as yet, have not joined up, is still in progress, and much yet remains to be done, further help in completing the big task being earnestly sought, by the joint secretaries, Messrs. E. A. Sanders and H. Hayes, who have their office at 3, Horsemarket-street. The local Recruiting Committee held a meet- ing yesterday morning, and, though the num- ber of canvassers was stated ~to be still disappointing, it was regarded as •encouraging that there were now some eighty available for the work, an increase of thirty over the previous week's total. The need for more canvassers is urgent for 60 far it has only been possible to canvass about a third of the eligible men. "It does seem strange," said a responsible official to an "Examiner" representative, "that a town like Warrington should not have in it at least a hundred patriotic men and women who, real- ising the amount of work to be done, would come forward and offer their services; more • particularly seeing that it rests entirely upon whether this scheme of Lord Derby's is a suc- cess or not as to whether the nation is to resort to practically 'pushing-methods' of en- listing men." A WORD TO THE MARRIED MEN.</p>
        <p>There seems, the official in question added, to be a feeling—no doubt inspired by the speeches of certain politicians—that the mar- ried men will be taken advantage of, in their patriotic desire. to enlist, and, as compared with the previous week, there has been a falling off in the number of married men who have offered themselves, during the past few days, at 3, Horsemarket-street. "There's no ground for that feeling," continued our informant, "for we have it on the authority of all the leading Statesmen that the course out- lined by the Premier, in his speech on Novem- ber 2nd, will be strictly adhered to. It will be as well for the single 'eligibles' to take to heart the pronouncement of Mr. Bonar Law to the effect that if it is found that they do not come forward in their classes they will be separately dealt with. That does not neces- sarily mean that they will be compelled, but that the position of the 'shirker' at the end of this recruiting campaign will not be an enviable one. The married man need have no fear that his position will be taken advantage of. It is desirable that all 'eligibles' shall come and enrol themselves at 3, Horsemarket- street, so that they can be placed in their proper groups and their appeals tcf be dis- pensed with, or placed in later groups con- sidered."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0329" n="art0329">
        <head>TRADES COUNCIL CRITICISED.</head>
        <p>TRADES COUNCIL CRITICISED.</p>
        <p>The decision of the local Trades and Labour Council to "remain neutral with regard to recruiting" comes in for criticism at 3, Horse- market-street, one of the officials informing our representative that the local Recruiting Committee are determined that Labour shall be represented on that body, and that, accord- ingly, it has been left in the hands of a pub- committee to appoint representatives from the local Labour party to act in their individual capacity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0330" n="art0330">
        <head>ROUSING RECRUITING MEETINGS</head>
        <p>ROUSING RECRUITING MEETINGS</p>
        <p>Recruiting meetings have been held at Market Gate, Warrington during the last few days, and at each eloquent addresses have been given on the necessity for a continuation of the recent recruiting revival. Last night the speakers were Captain Mathams and Mr. Charles Plin- ston. In a speech full of fervour, Mr. Plinston spoke of the formation of the War Council, and declared that only the combination of all the Allied forces could overcome the Germanic Powers. "A strong pull, a long pull, and a pull altogether," were needed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0331" n="art0331">
        <head>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER ORDERS.</p>
        <p>SOUTH-WEST LANCASHIRE REGT</p>
        <p>(VOLUNTEERS).</p>
        <p>Warrington Company. Headquarters: Drill Hai.. Orders for week ending' November 27th, 1915, By W. Peter Rylands, Area Commander. Parades. Monday and Thursday, 8 p.m., Company Drill and Rifle Exercises.</p>
        <p>Recruits. Band Room, 8 p.m. Trench Digging. To-day and Saturday next, 2.30 p.m. Site: Westerly side Old River. Com- pletion Of Dug-outs and Bomb Shelters. Stockton Heath Company (Cheshire Regiment) will co-operate to-day, and the Grappenhall Section will also probably parade.</p>
        <p>Section Commanders' instruction Class. Tuesday, 8 p.m. Semaphore Signalling Class. Monday and Thursday, 9 to 9.30 p.m.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0332" n="art0332">
        <head>M EMO RAN DA.</head>
        <p>M EMO RAN DA.</p>
        <p>1. Corps Badges.—Extract from War Office letter (Q.M.G. 7) to the C.A.V.T.C. :---" The Proficiency Badge is the only badge approved by the Council for wear with plain clothes by members of the Affiliated Volunteer Corps," and under Regulation 41 of the Defence of the Realm Act the wearing of unauthorised badges must be discontinued.</p>
        <p>2. Christmas Prize Shooting.—December 7 and 14. Open to all members who have qualified for the proficiency badge. ' 3. Semaphore Class.—Twelve members or more wanted to form this class, names to be handed in to the C.S.M.</p>
        <p>(Signed), W. PETER RYLANDS,</p>
        <p>Area Commandant.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0333" n="art0333">
        <head>INVITATION TO LORD DERBY RECRUITS.</head>
        <p>INVITATION TO LORD DERBY RECRUITS.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ls it not possible, through some official channel, to make an appeal to all men who have enlisted under the group system to ask them to join the local Volunteer Training Corps whilst they are on the Reserve waiting the time their services may be called upon? The Corps possess an efficient staff of instruc- tors, conform to all military regulations, carry out the identical drill which the man, if called upon, will have to perform, including modern trench digging, and it would in every respect be both instructive and helpful to the man so join- ing.</p>
        <p>There are on record quite a number of our members who have since joined the Regular Forces who have received rapid promotion through receiving their preliminary training in the above Corps. We cordially invite any man who has so joined to visit our headquarters, Drill Hall, on Monday and Thursday evenings, where we are prepared to offer them every assistance and in- struction possible.</p>
        <p>W. ASHTON, Joint Hon. Sec.</p>
        <p>Bridge Foot, Warrington, Nov. 19th, 1915.</p>
        <p>Hint to Poultry Fanciers. Not far from Bath a certain villager went to a lady who kept poultry to purchase a sitting of eggs. The lady, not having many on hand, inquired:— " Have you got your broody hen?"</p>
        <p>Well, no," replied the villager, "but our old hen has broke her leg, and I thought she might as well spend her time hatching out some eggs us sit about in the run doing nothing."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0334" n="art0334">
        <head>He Won.</head>
        <p>He Won.</p>
        <p>A Boston minister gleefully tells this story at his own expense. Noticing a crowd of urchins clustered around a dog of doubtful pedigree, he with fatherly interest asked: "What are you doing, my little men?" " Swappin' lies," was the answer. "Feller that tells the biggest one gets the pup." "Shocking, shocking !" exclaimed the minis- ter.- "Why, when I Was your age I never thought of an untruth." " Youse .win," chorused the urchins. "Mister, the dawg's yours."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0335" n="art0335">
        <head>Local Agriculturists' Helping</head>
        <p>Local Agriculturists' Helping</p>
        <p>Hand For Allies.</p>
        <p>A SPLENDID EFFORT.</p>
        <p>Smaller than it would have been but for a dispiriting fog, allied to marrow-chilling cold. nevertheless a considerable crowd of farmers and others interested in the oldest of the arts and crafts—that of husbandry—assembled on the bleak Brewery Field at Wilderspool on Thursday in order that they might help witi- their cheque books and purses two of the many deserving war funds—the Agricultural-Relief of Allies Fund and the British Farmers' Red Cross -Fund. The raison d'etre, as we have already stated, was the raising of money for two splen- did causes, the occasion was a jumble sale of agricultural stock, implements, and other requirements, organised by a numerous committee thoroughly representative of the local farming community, ancl conducted by two of the best known local auctioneers—Mr: John Mainwaring of Messrs. Mainwaring and Co., and Mr. Leonard Bennett. of the Warrington and District Farmers' Auction Co., Ltd. The committee in question had as its president bir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.O.—whose interest in all that appertains to farming is known throughout the length and breadth of the country—and it§, deliberations were presided over by Mr. Wm. Bainbridge. who had the assistance of Messrs. John Backhouse and Edward Marsh as vice-chairmen, Mr. Harry Gandy undertaking and most ably discharging the secretarial duties, and Alderman Bennett— whose services are never lacking in a good cause—lending assistance as the keeper. of the cash account, in his capacity as treasurer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0336" n="art0336">
        <head>THOSE WHO HELPED.</head>
        <p>THOSE WHO HELPED.</p>
        <p>The premier officials—if we may style them so—had the help of the following gentlemen as stewards :—Messrs. Hugh Darbyshire (senior steward), J. Backhouse, W. Blackshaw, Herbert Brocklehurst, Henry Brocklehurst, Jas. Ditch. field, P. C. Dutton, R. Fairclough, W. Fowles, Peter Hall, J. Pemberton, J. Taylor, T. M. West, T. G. Williams, and H. B. Wright, and of the following catering committee :—Mr. A. J. Willett (chairman), Mr. and Mrs. J. Atkin- son, Mrs. Astbury, Mr. and Mrs. E. Britch, Mr. and Mrs. T. B. Blackshaw, Mrs. Daybell, Mr. and Mrs. B. Fowles, Mr. and Mrs. E. Marsh Mr. and Mrs. W. Norcott (Penketh), Mr. F. A. Milling, Mr. and Mrs. J. K. Priestner, Mr. and Mrs. R. Tickle, Mr. and Mrs. John Whitlow (Preston Brook), and Mr. and Mrs. John War- burton (Burtonwood). 1 900 LOTS FOR SALE.</p>
        <p>There were somewhere about nine hundred " lots " for sale, and the hon. auctioneers grew hoarse long before they had got through so gigantic a catalogue, but they stuck to their task nobly, feeling that they had ample reward for the inconvenience of even a sore throat in the knowledge that it was, so to speak, incurred in "doing their bit." There was a great differ- ence between this sale and the ordinary sale by auction; in fact, all the difference in the world. for at the ordinary auction sale one is out to get as much as possible at as little detriment to the banking account as maybe; whilst, at this safe. the desire seemed to be to give the maximum nrice for the minimum amount of value—at least, that waS the desire that apparently possessed the more richly blessed among the bidders.</p>
        <p>THE CAUSE—AND THE CAUSE THEREOF! Just after the luncheon interval—a very necessary interval, by the way; for the sale lasted from 11 o'clock in the morning until somewhere about 8 o'clock at night—Mr. John Mainwaring made a brief speech to the assembled company concerning the objects of the sale. He urged his hearers to bid high and often, and reminded them that it was not often they had the opportunity of distinguishing themselves at a sale of that sort, whilst it was to be hoped that the war would soon be over and that, in consequence, they would not have need to bid at another auction of the kind. That day it was not so much a question as to how many shillings there were ip a pound, but rather a question as to how mtich they could spare to help the helpers of the wounded, and to aid their brother farmers across the Channel. British farmers were trying to do something to put their fellow agriculturists over the water into the position that they were prior to "the Man of Kultur " upsetting them. They knew what that individual had brought about— misery and ruin that no man in this country would ever have dreamed of, perpetrating. In organiAing that sale he and his co-workers had met with a very ready response, and he had never known a committee to work more man- fully to make any object a success. (Cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0337" n="art0337">
        <head>SIR GILBERT GREENALL'S THANKS.</head>
        <p>SIR GILBERT GREENALL'S THANKS.</p>
        <p>Sir Gilbert Greenall then briefly addressed the company, thanking them for their assistance. It was, he said, very gratifying to the promoters that they should have met with such a ready response when collecting gifts of cash and kind for the sale. Nobody would deny that the object of the sale was a worthy one—to help the Red Cross Society, which had done such marvel- lous work, and also to aid the gallant allies to whom this country was so deeply indebted. Terrible havoc had been wrought in Belgium and Northern France, as well as in the countries ,of our other Allies, and he was sure that every agriculturist in Britain would feel that it was up to him, in some measure, to take a share in the task of re-establishing their brother farmers who had suffered so heavily in the common cause. (Applause.) He felt that they all owed a great debt of gratitude to Messrs. Main- Waring and Bennett, to Alderman Bennett, to Mr. Gandy, and to all who had helped in organising and carrying through that sale. (Hear, hear.) THE FIRST £l,OOO.</p>
        <p>The sale had an encouraging send-off, for it was announced, before ever the first lot was sold, that the sum of £BOO had been donated, and the catalogue contained • a printed list of the donors of that sum. The total was made up to £l,OOO by the luncheon interval, and thereafter climbed up by tens and twenties of pounds to a total of which the promoters and contributors may well feel proud, and of which the agricultural community who constituted, in the main, the buyers, need never feel ashamed. Mr. Leonard Bennett sold the first lots, and in the course of a few preliminary observations he reminded his audience that every bid they gave him was a bid in the great cause of the Allies. Every shilling given was a silver bullet to—well, to help to settle the Kaiser's hash. (Laughter, and applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0338" n="art0338">
        <head>SPADE REALISES OVER £9.</head>
        <p>SPADE REALISES OVER £9.</p>
        <p>Some fancy prices were realised, many of the head of stock and articles on sale being bought and sold over and over again, generous pur- chasers calling out the instruction to the auctioneers "Put it up again!" after a lot had been knocked down to them. Thus, for in- stance, after buying a spade at 555., Sir Gilbert Greenall asked the auctioneer to offer if again, a process which successive purchasers repeated, with the result that before the spade was finally disposed of it had brought to the funds a total of over £9, being eventually sold for 3s. Special interest attached to the sale of a donkey, which had already realised no less a sum than £359 for the cause. It was a condition of sale that the donkey must be returned to the owner—Mr. W. H. Wright, of Brooklands—by the pur- chaser, owing to the fact that it had been entered for other jumble sales. The company entered into the spirit of the " sale " with gusto, and after it had been " sold". several times over it was announced that it had fetched altogether £sB—and still belonged to its original owner. But this animal's "selling price" was totally eclipsed by the amount raised for another quadruped of the same speCies.' This *donkey was—and is—the property of Mr. E. W. Sankey, who bought it back for six guineas after it had been " sold " and returned for. re- sale somewhere about a score Of times—the total realised in the process being £125 9s. 6d. Similarly other lots of various descriptions were "knocked down" time after time before they were finally disposed of, and thus was it that the receipts were largely swelled until they reached the fine total which yet remains to be disclosed, but which, we are- assured, comes quite up to expectations,</p>
        <p>The man who rides a hobby must expect to get his bumps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0339" n="art0339">
        <head>Youth Who Got His Hand In A Die-Stamper.</head>
        <p>Youth Who Got His Hand In A Die-Stamper.</p>
        <p>ACTION AGAINST MESSRS. CROSFIELD'S.</p>
        <p>A claim for compensation was made at the Warrington County Court on Thursday by George Henry Clarke, aged 15, the son of Mr. Thomas Minton Clarke, of 17, Ellison-street, Stockton Heath, against Messrs. Joseph Cros- field and Sons, Ltd., soap manufacturers, War- rington. The action arose out of an accident that occurred to Clarke whilst in the employ of the respondents. Mr. Dehn (instrueted by Mr. F. H. Swallow) was for the applicant, and Mr. W. Greaves Lord (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) for the respondents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0340" n="art0340">
        <head>TWO FINGERS AMPUTATED.</head>
        <p>TWO FINGERS AMPUTATED.</p>
        <p>Opening the case, Mr. Dehn said that the ap- plicant was a boy who would be 16 years of age next June, and the defendants were the well- known soap manufacturers of Warrington. The accident occurred on August 3rd. The appli- cant was then working on a machine which pressed out tin lids. The first and second fin- gers of his right hand were severely crushed, and they had to be amputated. It was slig- gested that the accident did not arise out of or in the course of the boy's employment, on the ground that the work on which the applicant was engaged was no part of the work for which he was employed. The applicant started work at Crosfield's on July 16th, his wages being Bs. a week. On Monday, the 19th, he was told to report himself to the foreman of the tin shop. The first work he was given to do by Mr. Wells (the foreman) was to hammer out bits of tin. This did not involve working on a machine. Soon after that he was put on a machine by Mr. Dobson (the under-foreman) to assist an- other boy. All the machines, with the excep- tion of the "die,'" were identical. After a. time the applicant worked that machine himself, and later on he worked on another machine, which was used for the purpose of cutting out the tops of tins.</p>
        <p>Changing Machines.</p>
        <p>Nobody had told the applicant to make the change, but, so far as the applicant could ob- serve, the boys frequently changed from one machine to another. And, so far as he knew, this was done with the approval of the foreman. Whilst working on that particular machine, the under-foreman named changed the "die" for him, and Mr. Wells, the head foreman, put the guard right for him. He worked on that machine until the day of the accident, on which day Dobson was in charge. The appli- cant exhausted his stcick of tin, and in order not to waste time, he started to assist another boy, who was pressing out flanges on some small tin lids. The applicant put the lids into the machine and the other boy handed them to him. The boy, when he had nearly used all the discs for these flanges, went for some more. The applicant went on working the machine with the discs that were left. He seemed to over- balance, the reason probably being that as he was keeping his foot in position ready to_press a lever, he slipped. The result was that the " die " came down on his right hand, and his fingers were badly crushed. It was impossible to say how the hand got underneath the "die," but it was probable that, in an endeavour to save himself after he had slipped, the lad clutched hold of the machine. Each machine had a guard, but in this case the guard seemed to have failed. The lad was taken to the In- firmary, and was an out-patient there for over two months. The hand was now substantially healed, and the question for his Honour to de- cide was that of liability for compensation.</p>
        <p>"WANTED TO TRY EVERY MACHINE." George Henry Clarke, the applicant, gave evi- dence bearing out counsel's opening statement. He added that previous to working at Cros- field's he was employed by a butcher at Stock- ton Heath.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. Greaves Lord, the wit- ness admitted that Mr. Wells had told him to be very careful. He denied that he had been told twice that morning to put the lids down, and said that he had been given permission to do the work a day before the accident. He was 'not aware that he had to apply to the foreman before he could change his job or machine. He denied that he wanted to try every machine in the shop, but admitted that once he tried to work the steam crane when the foreman was absent. Re-examined: He was at the time of the acci- dent doing similar work to that which he had done before. WANTED TO GAIN EXPERIENCE.</p>
        <p>For the respondents, Mr. Lord said that Clarke was a " keenish " worker, and wanted to gain experience. On this occasion he went to a dangerous machine, and one with which he had nothing to do in the ordinary course of his occupation. It was no part of his work to go on to that machine, and therefore he could not recover compensation.</p>
        <p>Henry Wells, foreman, said that at first Clarke was assisting a boy named Pzitchard to finish lids. Clarke told him that he could work the machine and the iwitness told him that he might work the machine if he was very care- ful. Clarke was never put on by witness to "finish' lids. No boy was allowed to work an- other machine, nor was he allowed to transfer from one machine to another without peimis- sion- This was known to the boys. Thomas Dobson, who was in charge of the tin shop in Mr. Wells's absence, said that the morning after Mr. Wells went away Clarke was cutting lids for a tin. He had ne duties which would take him near Sankey's machine—on which the accident occurred. So far as witness knew, Clarke had never' worked at "finishing" lids.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0341" n="art0341">
        <head>PROHIBITORY RULE.</head>
        <p>A PROHIBITORY RULE.</p>
        <p>At this stage Mr. Lord read the following rule, which, he said, was exhibited in the works: "No machine must be worked, except by per- mission of the foreman." James Sankey, who, on the day of the acci- dent, was employed on the finishing machine, said that previous to the accident Clarke asked him twice if he could work his Machine. The witness refused to allow him to do so. At the time of the accident he (Sankey) had gone for some lids. When he was coming hack with the lids, he saw Clarke working his machine. Judgment was given in favour of the appli- cant, the amount-of the award being Bs. a week from the date of the accident, his Honour ob- serving that though Clarke might have broken the rules he had not broken them in a manner which removed him from the scope of the Act.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0342" n="art0342">
        <head>AUSTRALIA'S SOLDIERS AND OUR EARLY WINTER.</head>
        <p>AUSTRALIA'S SOLDIERS AND OUR EARLY WINTER.</p>
        <p>It is several years since such wintry weather has been experienced in England so early. In some parts 18 degrees of frost have been regis- tered, and winter sports have already com- menced at Buxton and other places. Wounded Australian soldiers in the Cedar Lawn Hospital, Hampstead Heath, London, when they looked out of their windows the other morning and for the first time of their lives saw real snow, con- sidered it a wonderful sight, and adventured in a snowball fight. Fortunately the severe weather did not come without warning, and people were prepared for it. Warm clothing is what is required only in order to enjoy the frost. A comfortable and fashionable Overcoat can be bought from SUITS, LTD., of 17, Horse- market-street, Warrington; from 205., and Ladies' Nap Coats from 305., made in the newest styles, with either the Medici or convertible or military collars, from .30s. Both are Tailored thoroughly, and are worth fully 305., and at the prices they are sold at are remarkably cheap. Those who prefer the handy throw-on Raincoat will find at Suits, Ltd., all the Latest Shades, the new Brown in particular, with all the different styles of Collars to choose from, at from 21s. in Ladies', and 20s. in Gent's. Really, the Low Prices charged by this enterprising local Firm, combined with the high-grade tailor- ing they produce, would make the old-fashioned. Tailor bankrupt. Their Suits, made to measure from all British materials, at 20s. are marvel- lous value. Ladies' Costumes are produced from 305., To Measure, and bear all the exquisite finish and appearance of the Five Guinea Tailor's. A new department has recently been added at Suits, Ltd., in the shape of Juvenile Suits and Overcoats. Boys' Clothing is marked at lower figures than they cap be purchased at elsewhere, and special emphAsis should be laid on the fact that these are made by Suits, Ltd., of the same hard-wearing materials as the Men's and not from the lighter-weight cloths, as used by other firms for boys' garments. The address of Suits, Ltd., is 17, Horsemarket-street, War- rington.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0343" n="art0343">
        <head>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL AMUSEMENTS.</p>
        <p>DELICIOUS COMEDY AT THE ROYAL COURT. ,</p>
        <p>Last week the enterprising management of the Royal Court Theatre gave lovers of light and airy music and blithesome jest, a turn. This week they cater for the devotees of that which is delicious in comedy—comedy that has no relationship to the broad, " red-nosed " variety, which finds its supporters in the "gods," but that comedy which is writ for the more delicate palate, and which remains for long in the memory as a thing of joy; a trifle to dwell on and to love. And the name of this comedy—this ultra-brilliant contribution to the contemporary drama of love and life and laugh- ter; with just a suspicion of tears—is "Peg o' My Heart." We are a somewhat dour, worka- day set of people in Lancashire—" gradely folk," we flatter ourselves, in many a way—and some- how the finer of the refined sentiments do Rot make that appeal to us that they make to the more volatile Southron of our land. And thus, whilst Londoners raved of "Peg o' My Heart" and crowned it with such success as it has been given to few modern plays to attain, the War- ringtonians who accompanied us to the theatre the other night did not, apparently, see "much to go nap on "—as one of them phrased it for our benefit. If they did really enjoy "Peg o' My Heart "—as we cheerfully confess that we did—they had a most dispiriting way of dis- sembling their feelings. When we saw "Peg o' My Heart" in London more than a twelve- month ago, we thought it one of the cleverest, most " heart-ful "—if we may be pardoned a coined word—and delightful plays we have seen since—well, since Pinero gave us "Sweet Laven- der"; and after seeing it again on Monday night we feel that we are not called upon to revise our first enthusiastic impression, despite the fact that the "all ' star ' " West End company who then " did " the piece for our delectation, is, of course, now absent. But in its place we have as good a touring "crowd" as could well have been assembled; a coterie of polished artistes, who feel the worth of the piece and who play up to it, one and all, so whole-heartedly that to dis- tinguish between them is really being invidious. But Miss Ena Douglas must have a special word of praise for her ideal interpretation of the title role. As for the rest—well, they are worthy to act with Miss Douglas, and what better praise could they wish?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0344" n="art0344">
        <head>"THE CINEMA STAR" AT THE ROYAL COURT NEXT WEEK. Musical Comedy.</head>
        <p>"THE CINEMA STAR" AT THE ROYAL COURT NEXT WEEK. Musical Comedy.</p>
        <p>-Light and merry, pretty and melodious, witty and clever, "The Cinema Star," Mr. Robert Courtneidge's latest musical comedy, from the Shaftesbury Theatre, London, will be presented at the Royal Court Theatre next week. A matinee is announced for Monday at 2.30.</p>
        <p>The story is amusing, farcical and satirical. The music is a feature, particularly such num- bers as "Girls," "In the Night," and "Louisa," and the piece is staged with charac- teristic Courtneidge completeness. The dresses are beautiful in design and colour, while the scenes are remarkably effective. The story t.f " The Cinema Star" exploits the picture palace. It concerns the humorous troubles and misfortunes of one Josiah Clutterbuck, a millionaire moralist and prude on the prowl, whose bete noir is the film play. Clutterbuck denounces the cinematograph in unmeasured</p>
        <p>MR. EDGAR STANMORE Mr. Clutterbuek, in "The Cinema St</p>
        <p>terms from variout? platforms, and it is only poetic justice that the cinematograph should lead to his social discomfiture'. Enmesheil in the seductive toils of "Louise," a film actress, the eminent moralist becomes the unconscious hero of a cinema play, and " Clutterbuck," or " The Rake's Progress," becomes the talk of the town.</p>
        <p>The strong company, which has won- golden opinions in all the , principal towns, includes that very clever and charming actress Miss Lilian Gilbert as Louise, the film princess; and the whimsical part of Mr. Clutterbuck is played by Mr. Edgar Stanmore; as Phyllis we are to have Miss Leslie Graham. In a very strong cast, other especially interesting members are Mr. Hampson Lawton as Victor de Brett; Mr. Horace Percival as the stammering stage manager of the Film Company; Mr. Arthur J. Denton as Crocker; Mr. Horace Lingard as Freddy, Mr. Somers Bellamy, Mr. Edmund S. Phelps, Miss Violet Campbell and the clever Miss Doris Passmore.</p>
        <p>The powerful chorus and augmented orchestra will be ..under the direction of Mr. Frederick Rosse. Seats .should be booked early for this big production. .</p>
