, ''.::,:.:::::,:•::::::.:t?,:::: i"?.';44,.5,,,5t,:••:, . ..... A:•:::;::'''.&gt;•..'• ' .. ...: :. ...., ..,.::...;;;.;i-: ;:::::iiiiiiti::;:. , '•.':',-.......,'• 14.4ifg. :•::::•m:.:•::::.::.:'.Z .......:.::1•Wtif:i::::•::::::::::I.:::::::: t'• :''..::.:'::. :::.:: :1'......• . -.: .. :-::::::•::i:t:i:.:.:.:iii:::::;.:.:••:Ar.::::::::::::h:tt s . ...... . .. - • '''• -.'" '-'. .'- ',',.....:::::titt::::..ttt,•:•,.*.••••:' . . ........., :.:,.. i .::.... • .:::: ..... -:,:::'..:;:......" ::-..:::::::::::::'•::.:::::.4 : .......A.,,,*,,,,,•••*:::::::::::•.::::::::. . •,.....:•:,0,Witi::::::.:.:..':., . : .4.: • :.::::..........t.•::.:•,•:•:,..:4;:x.::,...,' ' ' : • ::.: ' ••••.,•"..x$,:ik::::::-:::tt.::::•:::::: :- . . • ::::...iiti:::;;:::::::•:,.::.::•,.,..:.:::...... ' . •:-.:•::::::.:ii.'t:.:!:]:::.1.:(.....'5.,;:§ti..' :.::::. ' . -:::.:,:.. ::::'..titi:i,t,„.• ..... .........,....:::.... 4›,.. . gao:::::*".. : .. ..:41%;:;',..' :. • . :' ';.::•:.;::: •-::!.•.'• '' '. - - • , . -..... ,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0345" n="art0345">
        <head>FUN, FROLIC AND FEATS AT THE HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>FUN, FROLIC AND FEATS AT THE HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Many tip-top " bills " have the management of the always-popular Hippodrome vouchsafed to us, but few can have been more replete with real good thingh than that which is "on offer" this week. The first " house "on Monday night was packed; you couldn't have got a seat for money ten minutes after the doors opened-- though, of course, it is just on the tapis that you might have got one for love; 'because people even sacrifice theatre seats on the altar of that undeterminate quantity. As we have said, the " show " fully merits all the support which has been accorded it, for the two-hour programme does not include a dull, or even a second-rate turn. From the moment the first " number " goes up to the second when the curtain settles with a "plonk" and the orchestra strikes up tile first bars of "God Save the King!" those who plank down their modest " bob " or their humble "thrip'ny bit" get champion value.for their money; indeed, from long experience of vaudeville-witnessing we asseverate without a tremor of the eyelid, that you will not get better value in the same line of goods elsewhere—not even in the great " Metrollops," as one of the comedians christens London.</p>
        <p>Fred Conquest is a great hit in his farcical absurdity, "What a Dog." Fred bears a name that is honoured in the histrionic profession and, although his particular line is not exactly the line that the other Conquests conquered in, his turn is nevertheless clever and bright. The Sie Tahar Troupe of Franco-Algerians present feats quite novel in the way of aerobatics and balancing, and Harry Linden and Ida Berridge, who come from "way down under," show</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0346" n="art0346">
        <p>\</p>
        <p>N</p>
        <p>RAILWAY FARE ALLOWED.</p>
        <p>appreciative auditordi what sort of entertainers Young Australia can produce and send along to the old country, to teach her things. Jim Corlett dances and the people like it; therefore is Jim justified. Zona and Milwy prove vastly amusing and not a little clever as versatile mirth and merriment makers, and the Great Virto does much to, live -up to his reputation as a "musical marvel," prov Ming a turn that is as talented as it is entertaining. Margaret Golda, of Irish descent, though born in the Antipodes, makes splendid use of a splendid soprano voice—which "gets right there all the while "—and Leo Nasselas appears in his latest Parisian novelty and—well, go and see it for yourself. You will thank us for the advice, the Whole show being Al at—at Macnaghten's. Fine " Bill" for Next Week.</p>
        <p>The programme for next week promises to bo equally as enjoyable as this week's has been. The "star" turn will be provided by Arthur White and Co. in a -new, screaming absurdity," entitled "The Detective." Sammy Shields, the "Speakable Scot," is an artiste who is bound to prove a great draw, for he has gained a big reputation throughout the country. Other big items in the "bill" will be Otto Viola, " Ameiica's clever clown," with his funny and original assistant, and the Bernard Ash Quartet, who give one of the most artistic musical shows in the realm of vaudeville. A number of other good items will also be provided.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0347" n="art0347">
        <head>" STAR " PICTURES AT STAB</head>
        <p>" STAR " PICTURES AT STAB</p>
        <p>KINEMA.</p>
        <p>More excellext pictures have been shown and' are being shown at the Star Kinema this Week- The chief film at the beginning of the week was.' the first episode of "The Exploits of Elaine." An episode of this exciting story will be shown week by week, and will provide many exciting. moments. Already two murders have occurred, and this is only the first instalment of the thrills promised. Another good film was "The Shortest of Heads," a racing drama, in which an exciting horse race is filmed. The lighter part of the beginning of the week programme was provided by the films "The Substitute," a Billy Reeves comedy, and a Keystone production entitled "That Springtime Feeling." The "big hit" this week-end is a telling drama entitled "As Ye Sow," in which that famous cinema actress, Alice Brady, enacts a leading., role. The film is decidedly worth seeing, ancl! holds the audience from beginning to end. The programme contains many other excellent sub- jects.</p>
        <p>Next Week's Leading Lines.</p>
        <p>" Rags " is the title of the big ram booked for the first three nights of next week, and "The Derby Winner," the name of that which will occupy place of honour in the week-end bill.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0348" n="art0348">
        <head>XVONDERFUL PICTURE AT THE</head>
        <p>XVONDERFUL PICTURE AT THE</p>
        <p>CRAND.</p>
        <p>"'Cabiria " is the title Of what must, surely,. be among the most wonderful and costly films. ever dreamed of; or executed, by the cinemato- graphist. It is, in verity, a stupendous produc- tion, and one that no lover of the great in the. way of living-pictures should miss. The film, has brought people, from all parts of Warring-. ton, ,flocking to the cosy Grand Electric Theatte, at Bridge Foot, and nobody..amolig: the crowded audiences has regretted either the. distance travelled or the comparatively nominal' sum charged for admission to see so powerful -a, production—a production that holds the im- agination and the interest from first flicker to. last, and has but few if any, equals in the. whole ever-widening realm of film-pliotography..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0349" n="art0349">
        <head>AQU:ITIC CO‘V.</head>
        <p>AQU:ITIC CO‘V.</p>
        <p>,4e ***:</p>
        <p>&lt;</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1915-11-20_WidnesExaminer_art0350" n="art0350">
        <head>Said To have S‘i mu Two Hundred'</head>
        <p>Said To have S‘i mu Two Hundred'</p>
        <p>Yards.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thurs., day, Mr. Arthur Browne intervened during the hearing of a case to ask the Judge to allow him to mention the case of John Rimmer, of Lodge Farm, Burtonwood, against Mr. John Hum- phreys, manager for Sir Richard Brooke, of- Cuerdley Ley. The case, ,he said, consisted of a claim for £l6 106., the value of a heifer.</p>
        <p>Mr. Browne said that the heifer -1;;;;;;;"t Out to . ley in April this year, and it was dis- covered to be missing in October. No trace, could be found of the heifer, and Sir Richard Brooke denied liability, as he contended that the animal was put out at the owner's risk. An action was therefore entered. Through a con- versation overheard in a local hotel on Monday,. the heifer was discovered to be at Mr. Joseph •Morris's farm, Moss Side, Moore, on the Che- shire side of the Mersey. The " ley " fronts the Lancashire side of the Mersey for about 11- miles, and to have crossed to Moss Side, Moore. the heifer must have swum a distance of at least 200 yards. Mr. Browne added that he had' given an undertaking to Mr. Unsworth (Mr. Rimmer's solicitor) to bring the heifer back and' to deliver it to his farm. On behalf of Sir Richard Brooke he made a gift of a certain amount to cover the costs.</p>
        <p>The Judge consented to the withdrawal of Ow action.</p>
