        <p>GALLANT SERGEANT. Warrington Man's Bravery Brought To Official Notice. RESCUED COMRADE UNDER FIRE. It is possible that another military distinc- tion will be awarded to a Warrington soldier— at any rate the gallantry' of•Staff-Sergeant Peter Cunningham, of Froghall-lane, whilst serving with the R.A.M.C. at," Chocolate" Hill at the time of the Sulva Bay landing, has been brOught to the notice of the proper authorities. Staff-Sergeant Cunningham is a member of the firm of Messrs. B. Cunningham, brewers, of Warrington. He enlisted within a fortnight of the declaration of war, and proceeded to the</p>
        <p>• • -.3.!*:f&lt; • • • . • , 040- • : . • .• .„ • . . • • . . . ' .;: , Nftr'</p>
        <p>'PRISONERS IN GERMANY. A Group Of Warrington Men In Confinement. CAPTURED AT MONS. We have been favoured with permission to publish the accompanying picture of five local men, prisoners of war at Gefangewn-Lager, Doeberitz, Deutschland. The picture was sent by Private Oswald Sant to his mother at 100, Algernon-street, Manchester-road,• and., by</p>
