        <p>+++++•:••:••:.4.0 •i“: 40•0 *4.4.44 • • Children 8 Corner • • • 411 Conducted by UNCLE JACK. 4,:t• • •4. 4. (;) 4. • Children, Join This Corner! Enter The Competitions! Write Your Best! Always Be Glad! Uncle Jack, " Examiner," Warrington... • WHO WILL WIN THIS WEEK'S PRIZE M Y DEAR • CIIILDREN,—• Last week I thought I would give your busy pens a rest, and, in consequence, there was no essay competition. This week, however, I am going to set you one, and I hope that as many as possible will enter, for I shall award a lovely book prize for the best effort that reaches me. The subject I select for the essay is: "My Favourite Game—and Why?" I am sure every one of you has a favourite game. You have told me about your favourite songs, and books and birds, and now I want, you to, write me about your pet recreation. Please write as plainly as you can, and let me have your inter- esting letters not later than Tuesday morning. Besides making the competitions a success by entering them in your scores, you can all do me another service, and that is to organise little "recruiting ,campaigns"- among "S-our play- fellows, and persuade, as many of them as you can to enlist in the ever-growing army of the nephews and nieces of UNCLE JACK. A WONDERFUL INSECT. The libellulit, or dragon-fly, passes the whole of its early life in the cold. shades of plants be- neath the water; mud is its dwelling-place, and its food consists of little insects which feed upon the roots and stems. of the water plants. Its appearance at this age (the larva state) is ugly enough. As the time for change approaches, it begins to crawl upwards on some plant or stem. When near the surface of the water, it remains quiet for some: weeks, in the iymh or chrysalis ,state; it then leaves the water, attaches itself-te</p>
